      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>SUCCESS Di THE POULTRY</head>
        <p>SUCCESS Di THE POULTRY</p>
        <p>YARD.</p>
        <p>The Chance To Double The Receipts Offers Itself.</p>
        <p>The average English farmer ought, with but little effort, to double the receipts from his poultry. Of course, this does not mean that he can continue with the usual haphazard system of setting hens just when they happen to come broody. At least one up-to-date incubator ought to form part of the equipment, of every farm, the larger farmers should have more. There is no great skill required in the manage- ment of these machines, just a little care and common sense ensures success. Even in the early part of the year, in January say, eggs can be produced at a cost of about lAd. each. To this must be added the cost of running the incubator, say about 2d. per head of the chickens hatched, which gives a total cost ,of 34d. per bird. These can readily be sold at from 6s. to Bs. a dozen. And even if they are not sold they can be reared and made to yield just as good a profit. . A twelve to sixteen weeks old cockerel weighing from 31b. to 311 b. can be reared at a cost of not more than is. 6d. In the early spring . anything from 9d. to is. a lb. can be realised, thus showing a profit of from 70 to 130 per cent. The pullets, of course, will not be marketed. They can be still kept and will find a ready sale when August or September comes along and the foolish or half-hearted poultry. keeper, who has done no hatching wants to replenish his old stock. To keep a pullet for the first six months of its existence, even with the present high price of foods, does not cost more than 2s. In better times it can be done for is. 6d. Add to this the 31d. cost of egg and hatching, and it will be seen that, reckoning the selling price of the six months old pullets at from ss. upwards—a very reasonable price in- deed for pure bred stock—the gross profit that accrues is fully 100 per cent. Say that 50 per cent. is deducted for losses in the flock by death —and that would be an exceptionally heavy percentage—even then 50 per cent. of profit would remain, a very desirable resiilt, as most men will agree.—" Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>INFERTILE EGGS.</head>
        <p>INFERTILE EGGS.</p>
        <p>- - A frequent - cause of infertility is that the birds are in too fat a condition. Pat hens not only produce fewer eggs, but a larger percentage do not possess the germ of life. Moreover, male birds that are too fat do not perform their work in a proper manner, and become lazy and in- different. The birds should be periodically ex- amined, and if too.fat the supply of food should be reduced; if too thin, increased. It is im- possible to fix 'any, standard.amount.of food for laying hens, as the quantity depends upon the nature of the food, the breed, the time of year, and the conditions under which the birds are living, whether they are in confinement or 'at liberty. Maize should be avoided, as this en- courages .the birds to produce infertile eggs, throughcausing them • to: become too fat. In- breeding is responsible ,for a large number of infertile eggs, and only birds that are entirely unrelated should be mated together. There are other evils attending inbreeding; when the eggs are fertile the germs are often exceedingly weak,- and the chickens, when hatched, are sickly, difficult to rear, and frequently stunted in growth. I have known many cases in which in-breeding has been successfully carried out, but it requires to be very carefully done, and only by those who have experience in the mat- ter, and know 'what they are doing. In-breed- ing, is sometimes necessary, such, for instance, as in fixing a,new, caldur, or in establishing a new breed, :but, generally speaking, for utility purposes, in-breeding should be altogether avoided.—Middlesex, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>TO KILL A FOWL.</head>
        <p>TO KILL A FOWL.</p>
        <p>As a general rule, killing by dislocation of the neck is the better, since it is more expeditious and cleaner than killing by what is termed "knifing." The two chief advantages possessed by the former method is that no blood is seen, and that plucking can commence at once. The bird is held in the left hand by the hocks, and the long flight feathers; the neck immediately below the head is gripped between the first and second fingers of the right hand, and given a gentle pull, at the same time bending the head backwards. Killing by knifing is rather more trouble. In this instance the bird is laid on a table on its back, and the 'point of a sharp knife is inserted into the slit in the' roof of the mouth; it is then given a .sharp press, entering the brain.—Expert, in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>PROFITABLE FAVEROLLES.</head>
        <p>THE PROFITABLE FAVEROLLES.</p>
        <p>It is more than surprising, when one con- siders the great economic qualities of the Fave- rolles, that it has not become more popular both with the ordinary towfl poultry-keeper and the farmer. The breed is more appreciated in the South than in the North of England. It is extremely hardy, so that there is no objection to it on that score so far as northern breeders are concerned. It is, of course, to be found in all parts of the United Kingdom, but not nearly to the same extent as it deserves to be. All those who have kept it for utilitarian purposes readily admit that the birds have fully justified their reputation from. an economic standpoint. For the benefit of those who are not familiar with the Faverolles, a few particulars regarding its good qualities may be of service. There can be no question that this breed is an excellent one for the farmer, because it combines the possession of the two essential economic quali- ties, flesh and egg - production. This should especially appeal to farmers, because, no matter how great is the demand for eggs, they generally prefer to keep fowls that can be readily disposed of for table purposes. The Faverolles is of French 9rigin, although several English breets were used in its production, notably .the Dork- ing and the Brahma. It has been bred suf- ficiently long, however, to fix the type, and it may be absolutely relied on to produce chickens tcue to the parent stock. One marked peculi- arity of the breed is that the sexes are dis- tinctly different in colour of Plumage, so that the pullets may be easily distinguished from the cockerels. From a commerciail standpoint the birds leave little. to be desired. They are compactly built, and they weigh more heavily than their fluffy appearance indicates; they are long, broad, and very deep in body, taking in this respect after the dark Dorking. The males should weigh from 71b. to 91b., and the hens a pound or two less when fully matured.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>HOW TO GET RID OF FLEAS.</head>
        <p>HOW TO GET RID OF FLEAS.</p>
        <p>Bulletin, No. 248 of the IT.S. Dept. of Agri- culture says that it is useless 'to attack the adult flea if no attention is paid to its breeding places. In houses the cracks of floors or under matting or carpets are favourite breeding places. The conditions under buildings are often favour- able for breeding. For this reason it is desirable that dwellings, stables and sheds should be so arranged that cats, dogs, chickens and other animals that harbour the pests cannot go be- neath them to sleep. The ground should be sprayed with kerosene or crude petroleum'. An inexpensive preventive measure is the liberal use of salt scattered about and wet down.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>TRAP NESTS.</head>
        <p>TRAP NESTS.</p>
        <p>I would advise poultry-keepers to adopt trap- nesting, says Expert in "Farm and Home," but at the same time they must not place too much reliance on the results that accrue. They must be read in the light of experience and according to the general laws of breeding. For elimin- ating the poor layers, discerning the 'early layers; and for securing cockerels from the heaviest layers, this system is admirable. It must not be expected, however, that like pro- duces like," for this is not the case. The trait of heavy egg-production appears tc‘ be transient to the extent that it. cannot be transmitted in its entirety to the progeny. The use of trap- nests entails a certain amount of labour, but even in face of .what I say above I am a strong believer in their adoption. In themselves they are not sufficient, but as a means to an end they are well worth the initial cost and additional labour.</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>yOU WANT "EGGS," THEN READ 7 SECRETS OF SUCCESS IN POULTRY</p>
        <p>KEEPING, BY GEO. A. FERRIS, The White Leghorn Specialist,</p>
        <p>Park Hatcheries. 31, Sandringham-road, Sunderland, POST FREE SEVENPENCE. • AND YOU WILL NEVER BE WITHOUT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
