      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>FARM AND G.Ak.O.',N.</head>
        <p>FARM AND G.Ak.O.',N.</p>
        <p>She was a charming little thing, but she was not familiar with the cotintry and its ways. Still, although she was from London, that great brute of a cousin of hers had no right to attempt to deceive her. He had volunteered to show her round the farm, and by and by they strolled into the cow shed. "Dear me, how closely the poor cows are crowded together !" she remarked. 'Yes," he said. "But, you see, we're obliged to pack them close." " Why ?" "So that they'll give condensed milk." And the dear girl smiled, and said she hadn't thought of that.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>ABOUT RAT TRAPS.</head>
        <p>ABOUT RAT TRAPS.</p>
        <p>One reason why many people have little suc- cess in trapping is that they do not use traps enough. Where rats are numerous Dr. Forbush, of Massachussetts, advises that 25 to 50 traps should be set, so that a considerable number of rats can be caught at first before the alarm is given. To get the best results traps should be handled only with gloved hands, and with great care andlgentleness. They should be disinfected in boiling water to destroy the odour.. In scent- ing the bait, use a single drop of oil of anise or caraway on a piece of paper, and rub this paper on the bait. This will do for several traps. More is unnecessary, and may repel the rats. A close study of the habits of the rats is neces- sary for successful trapping.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>WHEN TREES INJURE CROPS.</head>
        <p>WHEN TREES INJURE CROPS.</p>
        <p>"Ex-Farmer" writes to the "Times "4 --The letter on "Trees for Fuel " invites me to draw attention to another good reason for the felling of some of our trees. I refer to those planted in the hedges along boundaries of fields that are under crop rotation. Full-grown trees, such as ash, elm or oak, standing at say 40 to 60 yards , apart round a field of say 10 acres, will cause El loss in yield of grain or root crops of 10 per cent. The percentage would be even more in a field of under 10 acres, and of course less with one of greater area. The loss of crops from overgrown hedges is also very considerable. I do not see that those who are working for an increased supply of home-grown food refer to this, so I venture to bring it forward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>HELP FOR ALLIED FARMERS.</head>
        <p>HELP FOR ALLIED FARMERS.</p>
        <p>Farmers in Cheshire and Lancashire, who took the lead in raising funds for the stricken agriculturists of allied countries, will be in- terested to learn that the Agricultural Relief of Allies Fund stood at £42,633 on the last day of 1915. The example of the two counties in the successful organisation of jumble sales has been followed extensively throughout the country, ' and 'during (the past month the county of Hufntingdon has forwarded 0,000 to the fund mainly raised by that means. The £42,633 also includes £4,800 from the record jumble sale at the Smithfield Show.</p>
        <p>In addition, the committee which administers the fund has sent out to the small farmers of the Marne and the Meuse large gifts of live stock, implements and seeds, which have been accepted with the deepest gratitude. Arrange- ments are now being made for the consignment of a quantity of seed oats, which will be fol- lowed by further gifts of live stock. In this way the committee is endeavouring to restore the peasants of our allies to their lands devas- tated and laid waste by the Germans</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>MANURIAL LOSSES.</head>
        <p>MANURIAL LOSSES.</p>
        <p>The value of liquid manure and the waste I often incurred is a commonplace of agricultural ' literature, but it is of such vital importance that it will bear repetition, especially as the extent of the loss is probably unknown to many. There are reasons, well known to scientists, why this loss is so very great, but they are not half appreciated by those whom they especially con- cern. It may astonish some to learn that far and away the biggest part of the manurial value of foods goes clean away if the urine is not pre- served, even though drainage by rain from the (lung heap is rigorously guarded against. The latter is only a detail compared with the other, though it has often been alluded to.—" Agri- cultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PROFITABLE CHEESE.</head>
        <p>PROFITABLE CHEESE.</p>
        <p>The Agricultural Committee of the Cheshire County Council are recommending that the.in- struction in butter-Making in rural districts of the county shall be discontinued for the time being, and demonstrations in cheese-making substituted. It is recommended also that in- struction in butter-making at the Cheshire County Dairy Institute, Worleston, be discon- tinued for a time. In the opinion of Cheshire dairy farmers, cheese-making at the present prices for -the staple article is 50 per cent. more profitable for the farmer than butter-making.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>PRUNING GOOSEBERRIES.</p>
        <p>In country districts where birds are numerous it is often advised that the pruning of goose- berries be left till the spring. We have always found it better to do the necessary pruning in the winter, and then dress the bushes ,with a mixture of soot and lime, which renders the bud p distasteful to birds. If this mixture of soot and lime is applied to the bushes through a syringe the buds will, as a rule, be safe, while the mosses and insects are destroyed. In many gardens the gooseberry caterpillar is ram- pant. When this is the case all the prunings and leaves should be raked up. and thrown on the fire heap. Then remove some few inches of the soil underneath the bushes and replace it with same from the vegetable quarters. After this has been completed dress the newly added soil with soot and lime, which will destroy any caterpillars that may have been left.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>INCREASING THE MISTLETOE.</head>
        <p>INCREASING THE MISTLETOE.</p>
        <p>Now that Christmas has gone, many readers will be found who no doubt would like to try to grow this parasite. The idea is prevalent that the berries can be put on to the host at this _ season. This is a mistake, the .proper season to increase it being the months of April and May, and the way to do it is as follows. Never cut slots in the bark into which to insert the berries. Simply press them into the under side cf the bark and cover with a piece of netting or muslin to protect from the birds. No growth will show itself until the berries have fallen away, but if the pl-ce to which the berries were affixed is closely examined it will be found that the "bark is swelling. In the following year , the young leaves and shoots appear. Several sorts of trees are used as- a host for the mistletoe, the apple generally being chosen. Many years ago we recollect having seen in a Wiltshire garden some 'very large bunches of mistletoe on the topmost bough of a very high lime tree. In this case the proba- bility is that the original berries had been dropped by birds.—Wick, in " Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>PROTECTING ROSES.</head>
        <p>PROTECTING ROSES.</p>
        <p>Happily it is not essential that dwarf and standard roses shall be protected in all districts, but where the soil is really heavy, and particu- larly when the garden lies low, it is a wise pre- caution to take with all valued plants, particu- larly those belonging to the tea section. For dwarfs, ashes which have been in heap for a , few months are admirable, but soil may be used. It suffices to raise a mound ,six inches or thereabouts up the stems of the plants; this will afford protection to the lowest buds,- to which the pruning will be done in the normal course of cultivation between the middle of March and the middle of April next year. Early in the spring the upper buds will probably start, and the chanck are that they will be kill, but the dormant ones at the base keep the grower free from anxiety. In the case of standards the difficulties are greater, for the • reason that if the material utilised for protective purposes is permitted to become overloaded with rain water or snow, there is a danger that the heads will be blown right off the stem in windy :weather. Therefore pack the dry bracken, fern or hay well down, and • keep watch on it for the removal of superfluous water or snow should be necessity for so doing arise. The roses which are grown to such advantage on arches, trellises, arbours, pergolas, and in other similar positions do not require special protection any more than do those which are comprehensively grouped under the heading of "Garden or Decorative' by the National Rise Society.—" Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 8, 1911.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 8, 1911.</p>
        <p>DAY OF INTERCESSION.</p>
        <p>Why Does Not God Stop the War?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>VERSION OF THE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>VERSION OF THE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Sunday was observed throughout Great Britain as a special day of intercession to -Almighty God on behalf of the nation and Empire in this time of war. The church and chapel bells everywhere were muffled as a tribute' of respect to our fallen soldiers, and it is intended that this tribute shall be repeated to-day (SaVaday) and to- morrow. Collections were made in the churches and chapels throughout the United Kingdom for the Joint Committee of the British Red Cross and the Order of St. John of Jerusalem in Eng-</p>
        <p>land. The King and Queen, with Princess Mary and the Princes Albert, Henry, and George, at- tended the intercession service at Sandringham.</p>
        <p>doubts and fears did uot daunt them, for they saw the goal and knew they were nearing it. The former might be, faint, but they could hardly be said to be pursuing, and there was no need for their foes to be afraid of them. It was the latter who used the words in their right sense, and saw in them the note of assurance, despite all obstacles. The Midianites were of that class.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MAYOR AT THE PARISH</head>
        <p>MAYOR AT THE PARISH</p>
        <p>CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A special service was held at the Warrington 'Parish Church on Sunday afternoon., and a stimulating sermon preached by the Rev. T. Vickers. The Mayor an d Mayoress- (Alderman and Mrs. Peter Peacock) were present, and were attended by :several members of the Coun- cil and air number Of officials. The party was met at the church-door by the clergy and choir. The necessity of prayer formed the subject of the sermon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>PLACES OF PLEASURE FULL; PLACES OF GOD EMPTY.</head>
        <p>PLACES OF PLEASURE FULL; PLACES OF GOD EMPTY.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. Vickers, selecting as his text "Jesus also being baptised, and praying, the heaven was opened," said that prayer opened the vision of the glory of God.; it was the one certain way to, open the heavens. They were gathered together that afternoon to`pray. What for? That the war might be brought to an end by the vision of God. The vision of God' was the only secure basis of .a real peace in Europe, and that vision depended upon prayer. Why God Does Not Stop The War. The question was very often asked him, "Why does not God stop the war ?" The answer was because we were not prepared for peace; because there was not the spirit of prayer in England; because the ehurches were not full; and because the altars were deserted. That was why God did not stop the war. They heard 'a good deal about preparations in these days, about Germany forty years and England's forty minutes' preparation, .but Eng- land was not prepared for peace or for victory, because the spirit of prayer was lacking. It was not enough to pray; they ought to pray as though everything depended upon God. And ' they ought to work as . th. ugh, everything de- ' pended upon -God. The men at the Front prayed in that spirit. A wounded soldier had told him that at the Front they did not pray in church or chapel fashion, but they went down on their knees with their hands up to God. Before the battle of Loos they could hear the prayer, "Jesus," going from one 'end of the line to the other. They had' heard a good deal about the three 3l's—lneni-raunitions and money. Yes, they were indeed wanted, but there was something much more -needful, they ought to mind their P's and Q's—peace and quietness. Had Warrington got prayer and peace? They knew it had not. Plef.sure went along as usual. .It ought to do, for they wanted recreation and ought to have recreation, but, alas, the balance was not true. The places of pleasure were crowded out, but the places of God were quiet and empty. God did not stop the war because England Ihad not bowed the knee to the Almighty. Another reason, he believed, was because the prayers were not of the right sort. War found out their weak, spots, It put to the test the Church as - well as the nation. They were not able to say like the allies—and if report be true, like the enemy—that the churches were full of people praying for the men whose blood was their shield. Why was it that the English people had not crowded to the churches? It was because there was no real penitence. Prayer opened heaven, but sin stopped the way. Drink in the Way. The nation had a solemn warning from its allies that the great national sin of drink was standing directly in the way of the production of munitions. There was the voice that came ,from%Westminster and said that they had three enemies: Germany, Austria and drink, but the greatest was drink. They were told that the child life was being debased as a result of the increase of drink amongst women. What was the response? If they did not repent of this sin they would show themselves to be utterly feeble,' and their prayers would have little effect with the Almighty. England must be penitent before she could pray. What of the End? They must not think that he had painted the picture too blackly. There was hope. The war would not last for ever, but what would • the .end be? It must be set in the framework of he vision of God. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>BEWARE OF SECULARISM.</head>
        <p>BEWARE OF SECULARISM.</p>
        <p>Rev. E. C. E. Carleton Stands For Definite Religious Principles.</p>
        <p>Preaching at Holy Trinity Church, the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton said that one of the lessons .of the war, perhaps its greatest lesson, was the exposure of Creedlessness in religion, and which so many were professing and propagating at the present time—those who were so fond of telling the world that one religion was as good as another, and that if they would only live quietly and comfortably together without troubling themselves about differences between man and man, and denominational differences, all would go right. . "This war is a great and , solemn warning to our nation," he. added, "and • a warning that cannot be ignored—a warning as to the dangers of secularism and the fatal effects Of the non-cultivation of religious prin- ciples, and of tampering with or perverting Divine revelation, and of neglecting God's wor- ship. The German system and the German idea have eaten themselves into the public life of our country more than we are aware. •We have been on the downward course; and this visita- tion has come to pull us up. In the providence of God we may make a fresh start, and it is a great help to God's people in this struggle that we find ourselves allied with the great Russian nation who in the matter of religion have stead- fastly kept the old faith in spite of the efforts of the Roman Church, .and in spite of the. irre-</p>
        <p>_ ligieus influence of their German neighbours —holding fast to the .Sacred Scriptures., the Church's early Creeds. and the primitive formularies, principles, the value of which their splendid contact during the war. above all re- proach, has revealed t 9 the world."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>HAVE FAITH IN LEADERS.</head>
        <p>HAVE FAITH IN LEADERS.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Superintendent's Frank Words On Conscription.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. T. Derry preached a stirring ser- mon at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Sunday evening, when he urged his people to stand by the Government in their efforts to pull the nation through the present crisis. "Much that we prize must go for a time, if we are to gain for ever what we prize the most," he said in a thoughtful reference to the conscriptionist con- troversy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>TASK OF PURSUING.</head>
        <p>THE TASK OF PURSUING.</p>
        <p>Using as his text the words "Faint but pur- suing," he said that they might mean two differ- ent things and all depended on how they were spoken. They might be something between a sigh and a groan, or they might be a sober statement of confidence. some said, "We are faint, but pursuing," and they meant that their faces were set in the right direction, hut their progress was imperceptible, and one more mis- take or piece of had news would make them fall into the depths of despair. Others said, "We are faint, but pursuing," and they meant that the dangers or difficulties were many, but that</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Not for Material Gain.</head>
        <p>Not for Material Gain.</p>
        <p>"We entered into this war," the reverend gentleman proceeded, "not to gain trade, or territory, or indemnities, or prestige, or power, but to keep our pledge to Belgium and protect her from the treachery or tyranny of the Ger- mans; to help the friendly nations of France and Russia; and to keep our own rights secure from the menace of a ruthless and 'unscrupulous despot. But the first of these was the assigned and the chief reason. So high was our aim in going into the conflict and so noble the enthu- siasm kindled throughout all classes, that it was said that this feeling could not last, and that, as time wore on, selfish and sordid ambi- tions would appear. But the bravery and self- sacrifice of Belgium, and the valour and devo- tion of France, have been written on our hearts in writing that can never be effaced, and we are as far as ever from thinking that success can bring us material advantage in any quarter o" the grobe.</p>
        <p>The Stake at Issue. • "An artiste, who has been entertaining the men on active service in a kind of autobiog- raphy makes this amazing statement: I have made trips to the Western fighting front, and I have talked with wounded soldiers of all nations participating, as well as with the prisoners, and up to the present I have not found one who knew what he was fighting for, or what caused the war.' - "As far as our own men are concerned that is. the exact opposite of the truth. It is refuted by our knowledge of the men through personal intercourse .and private and published :letters; and it is further refuted by the faet that only the justice of our cause made it possible to recruit a voluntary army of millions. Truth and freedom are the two levers which have-up- lifted men from the depths of barbarism to the heights of civilisation, and they are the states that keep them there. Remove them • and they fall back into the abyss. Honour and liberty 'call its to arms; they keep us in the field, and we can think of nothing else until the war is won. These men of Israel were men of resolu-</p>
        <p>tion, and their object would have been un- attainable without it. When they felt faint they held to the object they were aiming at, though thousands gave up- A Marvellous Unity. "The men at the Front are full of deter- mination and confidence, despite their perils anti disappointments,and all they ask of us at home in the safety they afford, is that we should be of the same mind. Evelyn Orchard has put into the lips of a soldier on furlough, 'You have got the means, the men, the power, but not the will.' That is painting with too big a crush. Accustomed as we are to freedom of speech and of assembly, a free Press and Parliament, we give the chance to every critic and every crank. In the 'whispering gallery' of St. Paul's Cathe- dral the words spoken by one weak voice are echoed loudly from many quarters and make the impression of a vocal crowd, though only one man speaks. An insignificant minority amongst us may be irresolute, but the overwhelming majority are resolved to refuse a patched-up peace. That Would mean that our dear dead had died in vain, that we had departed from our great tradition, that our children would have to pass through the same furnace of afflic- tion, and that falsehood; force and frightful- ness had triumphed. While shrill outcries had been heard here and there our Unity has been marvellously maintained hitherto, but it is possible it may suffer a great drain when there has to be decided a 'question of a slight departure from the voluntary system of service, so</p>
        <p>rightly prized and so jealously guarded. "There is a Latin proverb, 'lnter arma silent leges ' (In war time the laws are in abeyance). Much that we prize must go for a time if we are ! to gain for ever what we prize the most. This ' is the principle on which we have acted hitherto, and it is a wise and safe one, both for states- men and workmen. If the present Ministers bring in a measure and promise it shall be a temporary one, for the d4ration of the war only, we cannot but believe them. Our leaders know the needs of the country as we cannot, and they know in which direction they are most pressing. Let us trust them and pray that on leaders and followers _alike there May rest the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and sympathy, and the spirit of knowledge. , "The resoluteness of the Empire at large is</p>
        <p>shiiwn by the increasing numbers coming from Canaeia; the new preparations in Australia in face df the losses and seeming failures at the Dardanelles; and tlie further efforts of South Africa, which might well have 'claimed that it had done enough. The reSOlution of our Allies is clear .from their closer co-operation .with ,us in counsel, in the factory and in, the field. A new spirit is pervading France, and in proof of it we find for fickleness, steadfastness; for ex- citement, calm; for frivolity, faith. Russia is extracting strength from' suffering and learning victory from defeat . Tantalising Prophecies.</p>
        <p>"As a nation we showed the courage that dares When we sprang to the side of .bleeding Belgium, and now we are called upon to show the courage that endures. It is being tested by the prolongation of the war. Some of those at the head of .affairs predicted success for .us last spring, but such prophecies serve only to tantalise. We are beingf tested by the reverses of Russia, the desolation of Servia, the partial withdrawal from the Dardanelles, the hostility of Bulgaria, the doubtful Attitude of Greece, the waiting policy of Roumania, and, - above all, by our immobility on the Western Front. •. "But great causes. have never been .carried, save by great courage and great sacrifices. Both ' Lord Kitchener, and Mr. Asquith, at the be- ginning of the war, warned us that the struggle would be a long one, and success would be given to those who endured to the end. One of the greatest figures of George lll.'s reign said, 'War nevef leaves where it 'found a nation. -It is never to be • entered into without mature deliberation. When so taken up, it is not to be abandoned without reason as valid, as fully and as extensively considered. The fire of such a conflict Will either consume or cleanse, and if we keep before us the objects which first kindled our courage they will keep it burning to the end. Pertinacity is in our blood and bone. We belong to an ignorant race that does not know when it, is beaten, and we must be true to type, worthy Of our inheritance.' "'No good deed is done by falterers Who ask for certainty. No good is certain but the steadfast mind, The undivided will to seek the good.'</p>
        <p>"-And if we are seeking the good we are 'seek- ing what God •wills; we are in line with the eternal purpose; we can rely on the Divine, aid, and our courage cannot fail. These heroes of Israel were men with experience, and it was one of the foundations of their courage. It was the memory of their victoo that kept them to the pursuit. • Weary though they were, they knen that they were victors, and they bore them- selves as such. At Morah the taken numbered 135,000; the remnant they pursued in their faintness was but 15,000 defeated fugitives. No task that lay before them could compare in difficulty with the first. If Germany ---</p>
        <p>"Have we no experience to encourage us? We have seen the supremacy • of our unrivalled Navy, as it has restricted our foes, protected our shores, provided our food, and. transported our soldiers. We have marked the magnificent increase and valour of Our Army. We have noted the reliability and bravery of our allies. Taught by our enemies, we have learned the only methods by which success in such a strife can be achieved. If Germany after preparing for more than "a generation cannot conquer us in. seventeen -months she never can. " We Look to God."</p>
        <p>"We take courage, too, from our experience as</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <p>a nation. Let one instance suffice. In our great struggle with the despotism of Napoleon sometimes we had allies and sometimes we stood practically alone. Then, as now, the tyrant regarded England as the foe cif all foes. Men, as now, he aimed at invading our shores from the jcoast of Northern France. Then, as now, we had to retreat in order to strike harder. Then, as now, they promise to do, our seamen wove the rope that strangled the oppressor. Men with an object, men of resolution, men of courage, men with ex- perience, it was all accounted for by their faith in God, Who had "made their cause His own, and we, too, are warranted in trusting Him, for the truth, freedom and humanity for which we are contending are dear to the heart of God, and He will defend His own.. "If, in this terrific contest, we are trying to do our duty as His Word and Spirit pointed out to us, then we all have the consciousness of vocation; and with the call comes the equipment: and with the equipment power. With clean hands and pure hearts in this matter we may pray for victory and expect it in spite of our national sins and shirkings."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>CHANGING IDEALS.</head>
        <p>CHANGING IDEALS.</p>
        <p>Rev. P. Meson And The Preciousness</p>
        <p>Of Life.</p>
        <p>There Was a deeply impressive service in which a large congregation took part, at the Bold-street Wesleyan Church. Special prayers for the wounded and for stricken homes were offered, and the reading of the roll of honour, containing 100 names, was followed by silent prayer.</p>
        <p>INDIVIDUAL AND COLLECTIVE DUTY. The TOM</p>
        <p>Rev. Percy meson, preaching St. John xv. 13, "Greater Love hath no man than this, that a man lay down his life for his friend," said that as we looked back over the past year, the thing that would strike us most would probably be the revision of our, ideas of the value of men and things. Politicians, who a year ago were anathematised by us now run the risk of being canonised. Lloyd George is becoming the patron saint of the "Daily Mail." Time honoured methods of warfare have been shown to be use- less, and machines and methods that were laughed at as being fantastical have been shown to be of first value. Men's estimate of the Church is under revision. It is her testing time. Men are viewing Christ afresh and won- dering whether He is an impossible idealist or the only really sane man who ever lived. Then too the working and the leisured classes are revising their estimates of each other and find- ing that the other man isn't such a bad chap after all. And perhaps nowhere has this revision of values been greater than in the de- ciding of the relative claims of a man's family, his friends and his nation. Many a man whose view had been bound by his family and friends has now had it broadened to include the whole nation. He has come to realise that somehow we are all bound together; that underneath all our differences, political, social, religious, there is after all some great unifying principle, some inseparable bond; that, strictly speaking, when men touch reality every man is a friend, or ought to be. And many a man too has come to know that to sacrifice for his nation is to sacri- fice for his family and his friend in the truest sense. It has not been an easy lesson to learn, and we have not all learnt it yet. There are many who would face death for home or friend who cannot yet bring themselves to face it for the nation—somehow it is too indefinite. But in this process of revision men are coming to realise that we are all linked the one with the other, and that in a man laying down his life for the nation in her battle of righteousness, he is in.the truest and widest sense laying down his life 'for his friend. "Greater love hath no man than this, that he lay down his life for his qiend."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>Joy and Responsibility of Life.</head>
        <p>Joy and Responsibility of Life.</p>
        <p>In those words Christ recognises that life is a man's precious possession. Christ ever did recognise, that. "What will a man give in ex- change for his life?" Constantly healing men as he did, what was Christ doing but recognising the value of a man's life? He appreciates its value and does not condemn men for prizing it. He enters into the joy of life in the home, mingling with the marriage throng at Cana, finding comfort in the communion of the home at Bethany. He even yearned for friendship, though He gave far more</p>
        <p>than He ever received.- " I Call you not servants but friends." Yes, life with all its joyous activity is a precious thing. It was precious too when discipline entered, for discipline, rightly received fitted the soul for higher activities. Life was not a thing to dispise or get tired, of when discipline came. The true attitude was to accept the yoke, for the yoke meant that there was work to be done, and effort to do the work would increase the strength of a man's soul. So Christ even urged men to accept His yoke joyfully, as a sign of honour, and the way to increased joy. Hats Off To Our Soldiers.</p>
        <p>In these days, when our eycs are constantly gazing at casualty lists, let us not forget that life is precious. Do not forget that those lists constitute an awful " agony " column. Let us not treat as commonplace the action of the man who enlists. Men may and do enlist from different motives, but at the bottom their action means that they are willing to give up that which is precious, they are willing to give up life. A great writer recently said that he instinctively wanted to take his hat off when a soldier passed in these days. It is a true instinct. Don't be too respectable when our defenders are passing; don't be afraid to give them a cheer; don't let us pride ourselves on our solidity when, after all, it is only indifference to the fact that here are men who are willing to give up that which is precious to them "for. our sakes." But Christ not only recognises that a man's life is precious to him, and that the greatest mark of his love is to be willing to give it up for someone, but Christ ever taught that all life is bound together, and that a man's life is precious to his fellows. He raised Lazarus and _ _ _</p>
        <p>estored him to those who loved _ him in</p>
        <p>Bethany. The corollary of "Love to those who cause stumbling" is "Blessed are those whose lives are helpful." "This is My command- ment that ye love one another." He wants all men to be bound together in the bonds of love,</p>
        <p>for men are dependent on one another. , Even Christ was dependent upon his disciples, and is even now dependent on us for the bringing in of His kingdom. We are necessary to one another. Men are being drawn closer together and are recognising that each is,valuable to the other, that every life in whatever station, is valuable to the nation, and because we believe ,we are battling for the right, valuable to the kingdom of God.</p>
        <p>The Call To Penitence.</p>
        <p>Grinding competition, continued the reverend gentleman, and the energy we had spent on making man-killing machines had ignored the true value of life—and England in this had not been guiltless. She ought to be penitent and ask whether much of the present difficulty was not a chastening from God. True, he con- tinued, God has done us the high honour of call- ing us to stand for righteousness, but let us not miss the call to penitence which is assuredly. there as well; and let us realise where Christ's teaching about the preciousness of every life to the world leads us. Not only English life is valuable, but the lives of our enemies. Diffi- cult though it may be, we must try to rise to the view of tie preciousness of all life and strive for the abolition of all that would destroy it. _ _</p>
        <p>they see now? They have given their life for love of us. What do they See to-night? We have one more link with the unseen in the lads who have died for our sake, therefore see- ing we are compassed about with so , great a cloud of witnesses, let us lay aside every weight and the sin that doth so easily beset us and enter into the spirit of our warfare realising that greater love bath no man that 1 this that he lay down his life for his friend.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>Forces That Will Improve The World.</head>
        <p>Forces That Will Improve The World.</p>
        <p>Because life was so precious, Christ said that a man could show no greater love than by lay- ing it down for his friend. And He Himself set the example by dying on the Cross. And is there not some truth in that idea that we have not grasped? Christ began a world-wide "ministry of spiritual usefulness after His sacrifice. And the way to true usefulness ;for us is through ' sacrifice. For some of us it may be through death. I believe that the brave fellows who are laying down their lives "for our sakes" are freeing spiritual forces that will better the world. Many a brave fellow has entered a life. of wider service since he showed his' love for us by laying down his life. And if this be true we as Churches are entering on a time of great spiritual opportunity and responsibility. _ Stand- ing between us and unnameable horrors are the lads whom we knew, counting life as nought. It. is for us to carry the battle forward. They are facing stern realities, are we facing them? We say we shall be glad to see them back—but what will they find? A church full of slack- ness, unreality and sham, or- a church that is pouring out its life blood to save the world? And those who will never come back, what do</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>FOR THE SAKE OF COUNTRY. ev. W. Reid Insists On Importance</head>
        <p>FOR THE SAKE OF COUNTRY. ev. W. Reid Insists On Importance</p>
        <p>Of Prayer.</p>
        <p>The Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. P. Peacock) attended the Presbyterian Church on Sunday evening, and the former read the lessons. He also made an appeal to the congre- gation to subscribe liberally to the collection on behalf of the needs of the Joint ComMittee. of . the British Red Cross Society and of the Order of St. John of Jerusalem. The preacher was the Rev. W. Reid, who said that intercession was the keynote of the services that day: Intercession was prayer, but all prayer was not intercession. Intercession was distinct from adoration, confession, &amp;c., and was the outpouring of the soul to God on behalf of men living—not dead—and of whose needs we knew something. We must not be satisfied with simply praying for this and that object. That was not complete intercession, it was only half. We must use all the means we had to try to fulfil those prayers. There must be a directness and a definiteness in prayer. Prayer had become a lost art with too many. We were to pray not only for the world, and all kinds and generations of men, but to [ravel en the definite lines God had directed us. We must pray definitely for the .King, and if we knew of any imperfections in him we must pray to God to remove them for the sake of the country as well as for- his own sake. We must pray for the members of the Cabinet and of the Houses of Parliament—he did not say we were not to discuss them--hut if we could catch the spirit of Jesus, prejudice, bias and other things of a like character would be entirely removed from our conversation and thoughts, for we should realise that these men were nothing in themselves as compared with wh.Bt they were as leaders, &amp;c. We must pray for the course of events to be properly guided. Why, also, could we not pray for the Press and ask God continually on its behalf? it was only in prayer that their truest strength and value were discovered. God, if we asked Min, would direct us as to where we were doing an injustice, and as to where our prayers were weak. We Must not forgr. that Christ hal said "I will pray for thee." The great ex- "nnic,, the grant assurance was with us to-day.' Christ still interceded, and if we were less blind and deaf we should see some of the glory and the privilege when He called us to intercede. We had not yet grasped the full significance anl the full power of prayer. When the soul poured out its best definitely and continuously in the name of Jesus and joined hands with Him then would come victory, because we should be fit and ready for victory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>PROPHECY OF BROTHERHOOD.</head>
        <p>PROPHECY OF BROTHERHOOD.</p>
        <p>Rev. If. 'Ogden Strongly Condemns • Racial Animosity. The Rev. H. Ogden -invited the congregation at Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday evening to look forward to the time when, in spite of the present Conflict, there would be one great brotherhood of the race. In these times, he said, there were those who sought to foster race hatred, men who were still preaching the creed or would continue to clothe the nations of Europe in steel. It was, there- fore, refreshing to look forward to a time of unity and to know that its counterpart was re- vealed to us in the unity of Heaven, which was a great common meeting-place of all "nations, multitudes, peoples and tongues." No Armaged- don could alter that, and, whilst some men preached the boycotting of a race and races, the. Celestial Kingdom was still the common meet- ing-ground of all humanity. The great note of hope was that we looked forward to the coming days with a hope of brotherhood. War had interrupted a grand evolution, but, when the interruption was over, the grand scheme must be completed. The Heaven, of God and God's Kingdom on, earth even "gas it is in Heaven," must come. The common meeting-ground of all people was the worship of the Lord and Father of us all. They wore the "white robes" which were. holy habits. There could be no cohesion between those who were of unclean and clean habits. The great pest of the cleanly was filth and dirt, and the white robes of international brotherhood could only be secured when nations and their leaders had holy habits. Ententes disappeared When leaders disagreed; treaties became "scraps of paper" when material interests were jeopardised ; offensive and defensive alliances were often regulated by the state of banks. The only brotherhood that could endure must be founded upon holy habits —i.e.. allegiance to and worship of God. Our object should be to serve and not to command. Family Life an Example. • The secret of family life was' the same secret which must prevail in international affairs, and the family which was founded on individual and selfish ambition was a faniily of discord and trouble. And as in the case of. individuals, so in the case of nations. The palm of self-con- quest was the greatest conquest and victory. Other forces had had abundant opportunities; other methods had been tried and had failed. Low Ideals.</p>
        <p>Even co-operation; as known in the industrial world, might soar no higher than the " divi." Federations of masters developed into mere "rings." Alliances of trade unions might not soar beyond "ca' canny." There must be found a more lasting or enduring basis—a basis that did not .change with changing times; one which was not affected by material interests; one -.which did not depend upon a world-empire, racial conquest, or world domination. What was needed was the recognition that all nations ,were one great family, with one Father, one God, and Lord. "Some of us prior to the war," concluded the preacher, "belonged to church, federations which co-operated with church federations in Germany ,to bring about a lasting • and eternal peace. The power of material interests has wrecked for a while that good and noble work, but the day will return when was is over and there will come the opportunity, perhaps a better opportunity, to establish. a brotherhood, not merely on federation or trade union lines, but a brotherhood moved and actuated by ,the Christian spirit."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>CAIRO-STREET UNITARIANS.</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET UNITARIANS.</p>
        <p>. Irvine Lister And The Possibilities</p>
        <p>Of The 'Future.</p>
        <p>At a special service of consecration and :fact- cession held at Cairo-street Unitarian C'lapel on Sunday, 0 The Rev. Irvine Lister, who preach:A (p.,oicd Wordsworth's "Ode to Duty ": "To humbler functions, Awful power! , I call thee.- I myself commend Unto thy giiidance from tins .oh, let my weakness have an .1141 Give unto me, made lowly w 3) , The spirit of self-sacrifice, The confidence of reason giv'. And in the light of truth thy bondman let - me live." Never, he said, had Our nation PtiterA o n a . New Year so fully conscious of all it might mean, and with such deep and fervent hope that goodness and righteousneSs trw.v tti imph. Never had we approached the future with such solemnity, and never had the wh:o!e been so clearly at one in all is purpose: ard hopes. We had passed through a night of gloom and darkness, and we were anxiously looking for the brightness and joy of 'a new day. _ - . . .</p>
        <p>_ It was with a heavy heart that we enterei upon the New Year, with the contemplatio:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>of tasks to he performed which were distaste- ful, which filled us_ with some dread and</p>
        <p>- horror, and which robbed us of our happiness and peace. But, we entered upon it, believing that our hands , were clean, that duty and righteousness called us to do that which we did not wf4h to do, and we were prepared for the suffering and the sacrifice. We were now just realising how great was the task we had under- taken, but we were not dismayed, and, as a nation, we were solemnly. pledged to put forth all our energy to resist the invader of other lands, and to secure right and justice .for thR small nations of Europe. Resolution Unshaken.</p>
        <p>As we looked back over the year of triai_and, illieulty, we realised that all we had hoped for ad not been accomplished, that all the saeri-</p>
        <p>flees and the suffering demanded had not been. sufficient, and that there was a time before;.us, sterner, more difficult, more fraught with possi-</p>
        <p>Wales of pain, which we should still have to, face manfully, if we would escape disaster or calamity. 'Nothing, however, had happened to shake our resolution, or to make- us seek to evade .the responsibilities we had undertaken.. We were still united .in our determination to ensure the complete victory of what we believe to be right, • to establish the right of small nations to live, to oppose right to might, and tt.t uphold _the moral law.</p>
        <p>'Sir Oliver Lodge, in a little book recently pub- ished, said: "We live in great and invigorat- ng times, when long-dormant energies are set free, and revolutionary changes can be made.</p>
        <p>times .which may be the turning point in the history of the world." This fact was being more and more borne in upon men. It had been</p>
        <p>our fate to live at a time when the whole of civilisation may take a real turn, when from this tim?, we may date fresh developments, almost a fresh epocli in the history of the world. Pit</p>
        <p>was certain that we should see a reconstructed Europe, when the relations would be what they, have never been before. We might see the total. break up of what we call militarism. We might see the abandonment of force as a method of securing either .developments or rights, and we might see 'something established which will war impossible again for ever. It was quite possible also that from this time we shall commence a fresh grouping within the nation. The old parties and classes might be destroyed. new influences might come to mould public opinion. and it was not impossible that govern- ment. commerce, and all things necessary to the life of a community might change considerably. But, what was perhaps more important still. if was quite certain that moral and ethical ideals would have to play a greater part, and have a• greater infinencie in the near future than they have ever done before. Co-operation of the Human and Divine.</p>
        <p>During this period of transition, rapid though it might be, whilst we were passing rapidly from one phase to another, that which counted most, that which is of the utmost importance was the high moral effort and resolution we put into it. "It is tremendously important." said the reverend gentleman, "that we should approach all our present_ problems and difficul- ties with minds full of the righteousness, the justice and the truth of God, that we should look on all things with the eyes of love, and that we should be prepared for the greatest of all sacrifices. That is what we mean when we say that the human should co-operate with the Divine, we, simply mean that all the energy we can pour out should be poured out on the side of freedom, of truth, and righteousness and love. These are the eternal things to guide us amongst the difficulties and vexations of our present life. and without them we shall founder and perish." /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LECH-STREET PRIMITIVE MEMO-</head>
        <p>LECH-STREET PRIMITIVE MEMO-</p>
        <p>DIST CHURCH.</p>
        <p>An intercession service was also held at Legh- street Primitive Methodist Church on Sunday evening, the service being conducted by the Rev. a Parkin. of Manchester. Miss M. Jones sang impressively "God's Slumberland " (Hamilton Gray).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>AT ST. ALBAN'S CHURCH.</head>
        <p>AT ST. ALBAN'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Father Whit Ile On The Anti-Christ. Preaching to a large congregation in St. Alban's Roman Catholic Church, .Bewsey-street, the Very Rev. Father. Whittle said that we were fighting for Christ against anti-Christ. He did not say that the Kaiser was the anti- Christ, hut he was an anti-Christ. He was fighting against Christ—God. We were fighting, for God and Christ, against the Devil and anti- Christ, and for-God-Empire in the world. It was not the .Emperor's world; it was not Ger- many's world, but God's. We were fighting for . _</p>
        <p>national liberty for each country, whether large or small, for the right to live, and not to be interfered with. We were fighting against oppression and tyranny, for a plighted faith. against 'broken treaties, and what were called scraps of paper." Germany premised to pre- serve the integrity of Belgium, but we knew •what had been done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>ORFORD CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ORFORD CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Rev. G. It ale ()Alen On Fear Ami Pessimism.</p>
        <p>The. View. of Orford has given his congrega-</p>
        <p>lion the opportunity of offering prayers for their friends Sunday after Sunday, and a roll- call numbering over 150 has been read weekly. This ordinary form of intercession was adhered to on Sunday, but at the evening service The Vicar based . his address upon the Bishop's pastoral letter. He hesitated to add any words Of his own to that beautiful and inspiring letter, which called forth resolute</p>
        <p>and ind-omitable optimism in the present crisis. Fear and pessimism were the lowest depths to which they could sink. Though they were facing what be felt was destined to be a ter- rible year, they Must endeavour to bear the strain. StSmetimeS he personally felt he was inwardly shirking his responsibilities. They needed all the sympathy and help they could derive from one another. He sincerely thanked _ _</p>
        <p>the congregation for the real 'help they had given him in his duties. Their nieeting at church Sunday by Sunday would help Ahem to think kindly thoughts. and ,to render deeds of kindness and affection one to another.</p>
        <p>" Peace, perfect liea,ee," was sung after the )lessing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>PADCATP.</head>
        <p>PADCATP.</p>
        <p>At the morning service at Christ Church, Pad- gate, the l?ev. J. Hirst read.the pastoral letter. forwarded for the occasion by the Bishop of Liverpool, and in the evening preached on the necessity of throwing 'ourselves on fhe mercy of God and pleading for forgiveness for Our sins and His assistance in helping us to win a: war fought in a. righteous cause. Archdeacon Spooner's "War Hymn "vas ,sung by the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>THELWALL.</head>
        <p>THELWALL.</p>
        <p>The Rev. C. Alallam Johnston, vicar of All Saints' Church. said, at the morning service, that they could look rip and tell God that our quarrel was just, but that should not blind our eyes to our need of humility before Him. War was the devil's work. but God was stronger than the devil. The Good Physician would not have given us this bitter medicine unless it was for our good. We. as a nation, did not realise our many sins. We were fond of alluding to Germany's "scrap of paper," but we forgot that English workmen went on strike, in spite of their solemn pledge not to do so. We were struck with horror at the Germans dropping shells on Rhehns Cathedral, but were not ashamed to treat God's house/ with utter in- difference.</p>
        <p>When- a man comes home he asks his wife one or two questions. One is, "IS dinner ready?" and the other is, " Is supper ready?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>taters to Or Oita</head>
        <p>taters to Or Oita</p>
        <p>RAGGED SCHOOL TUE Vl'.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Please allow me on behalf of the com- mittee of the above school to express through the columns of your paper our thanks to those who assisted us on Boxing Day and also on New Year's Sunday in the treat which was given to the children. The children. who immensely .enjoyed themselves, many to whom Christmas would have brought very little cheer had it not been for this treat, owe their thanks to his Worship the Mayor, who kindly sent two boxes -a Oranges; alsci to Messrs. Carter and Son, Toft, Hallwood, Co-operative Stores, }larding, Han- nan, Strange, and an anonymous friend, all of whom sent either cake or bread; Messrs. Povey and Leigh, providing tea and sugar.</p>
        <p>JAS. SKET,LAND.</p>
        <p>January 5, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>BLIND SOLDIERS' A NI) SAILORS'</head>
        <p>BLIND SOLDIERS' A NI) SAILORS'</p>
        <p>FUND.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l should be glad if you will allow me, through the columns of your valuable paper, to thank those people who have so generously con- tributed to the above fund, and hope to be able to give later the total amount, together with a list of subscribers. I should also like to take this opportunity of thanking the head school teachers of the various schools, and choirmasters, and last hut not least, the managers of the Royal Court Theatre, the Hippodrome and Picture Houses, for their kindness in allowing the collections to take place, not forgetting, of course, the members of the Church Lads' Brigade, the Free Church Boys' Brigade, and the Baden-Powell Boy .Scouts, whose services were indispensable.</p>
        <p>- W. B. RANDALL, • lion. Or,ganising Sevretary. Hanover-street, Warrington. sth January, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>IRRANGENIENTS FOll CONVALES-</head>
        <p>IRRANGENIENTS FOll CONVALES-</p>
        <p>OFFICEI IS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The committee of the Public Schools Alpine Sports Club have accepted an oiler made to them by the Board of the Palace Hotel, Montana (Switzerland) Limited, to place that had at their disposal, rent free. during the present winter season, for the reception of con- valescent officers. It is intended to follow this shortly with arrangements for Great Britain. A charge will be made to each officer of Gs. 6d. per day to cover cost of food and other expenses. Friends who accompany them will pay Bs. per ay. Any balance remaining over after defray- ing the cost of food, wages. and other working expenses, will be devoted by the committee to .defraying the expenses of necessitous officers. Montana-sur-Sierre enjoys the highest record far winter sunshine of any Swiss mountain resort7'.l hours of possible sunshine on the .shortest day.</p>
        <p>Any convalescent officers, and especially those suffering. from bronchitis, pneumonia, and general debility and overstrain, are invited to send a statement of their ease and the dates within which they would like to avail them- selves of this arrangement, to the Honorary Secretary of the Sub-committee, Lady Waterlow, .1, Maresfield Gardens, Hampstead, N.W.</p>
        <p>LYWON, Chairman of the Sub-N,/nruittee 3, Upper Woburn-place, Tavistock-square, London. 3rd January, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>DRINK RESTRIt .1 14</head>
        <p>DRINK RESTRIt .1 14</p>
        <p>Sir;—After • " Citizen's " ungracious reply to my offer to bring this protracted controversy to an end, and his unprincipled attempt to con- strue this offer into an acknowledgment of -defeat, I am reluctantly compelled to re-enter the field, if only; to shatter his flattering belief that I am anxious- to "run away " from his arguments." As regards professional teetotalers—who are now thrown overboard by "Citizen "—his state- ment that I "dragged them into the contro- versy" is not 'quite a correct vie of the case.</p>
        <p>-" Citizen " denounced the opposition to the restrictions as simply a bogus agitation fostered by the Trade; and it was in reply to this that I exposed the vast financial backing behind the teetotal 'movement. " Citizen " is not very, original in his choice of proverbs; I used the 'one about "people in glass houses" some weeks ago, and I think your readers will agree that it is not at all applicable to my side of•the ease, as all I have done is to iTturn the stones thrown at me. Perhaps your readers will re- member the story of the schoolboy essayist will described the subject of his discourse as " a wicked little animal—it defends itself when at- tacke'd' In the present controversy I have -emulated this "little animal," and I think I -dcserve great credit for my Christian charity, ind•lamblike forbearance. •</p>
        <p>..Now let us come to " Citizen's " great point-- the now famous -" 5 per cent. minority" men- tioned by Mr. Tillett. • Despite my very plain expla nation' that I in no way adopted this figure, and my pointing out to "Citizen "• the possi- bility that Mr. Tillett was speaking of his own .C-nion—despite this; your correspondent sends forth a prolonged paean Of teetotalist triumph, claimin,g that, in some way best known to him- self, this " admission knocks the 'bottom out Of' my case.</p>
        <p>I will not follow " Citizen " into the arithmetical procedure by which he arrives at his "aggregate," but if it be true that 5 per cent, of munition workers have at various times overindulged, it is plain that 95 per cent. are innocent. These 95 per cent., in addition to other workers and the general public, are being severely penalised for the fault of the few. So far from being frightened at the 5 per cent. estimate, I am prepared to maintain Mint even 10 per cent, would not. justify the present drink restrictions. Even 10- per cent. could be more justly and more efficiently controlled without •interference with the rights and liberties of the others.</p>
        <p>So much for "Citizen's " triumph! In future I hope he will not start cheering before he has won.</p>
        <p>J. E. SIMPSON. Liverpool, January 14. MC.</p>
