      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>VIEW OF THE NARROWS.</head>
        <p>VIEW OF THE NARROWS.</p>
        <p>How The South Lancashires</p>
        <p>Had Victory In Sight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>SUPPORT FAILED.</head>
        <p>SUPPORT FAILED.</p>
        <p>The long-expected dispatch -from Sir lan .HamiUon on the Anzac and Silvia Bay opera- tions in August, which was issued officially on Thursday night, gives a vivid story of the heroic fighting and the stirring incidents, but the chief interest centres in his .statement of the reasons why the boldly-planned scheme failed to achieve its purpose. In the ease of the Silvia Bay landing Sir lan attributes the ultimate holding up of the advance to the failure Of the local corps- and divisional commanders to push on during the first two days. While recognising the diffi- culties and the fatigue and thirst of the soldiers, Sir lan says:—</p>
        <p>"Driving power was required, and even a certain ruthlessness, to brush aside. pleas for a respite by tired troops. The one fatal error was inertia. And inertia prevailed."</p>
        <p>And now, says the report, under that fine leader, ',Major C. G. 1,. ;Manson, the 6th Gurkhas of the 2,9 th Indian Infantry Brigade pressed up the slopes of Sari Bair, crowned the heights of the col between Chunuk Bair and Hill Q, viewed far beneath them the waters, of the Hellespont, viewed the Asiatic shores, along which motor transport was bring- ing supplies to the lighters. South Lancashires on Crest of Sari Bair.</p>
        <p>Not only did this battalion, as well as some of the 6th South Lancashire Regiment, reach the crest, but they began to attack down the far side of it, firing as they went at the fast retreating enemy. But the fortune of war was against us. At this supreme moment Baldwin's cotumn was still a long way from our trenches on the crest of Chunuk Bair, and instead of his sup- port came suddenly a salvo of heavy shell. These tailing so unexpectedly among the stormers threw 'them into terrible confusion. The Turkish commander saw his chance; in- stantly his troops were rallied and brought back in a counter-charge, and the South Lan- cashires and Gurkhas; who had seen the pro- mised land and had seemed for a moment to have held , victory in their grasp, were forced backwards over the crest and on to the lower slopes whence they had first started. But where was the main attack—where was Baldwin? When that bold but unlucky com- mander found he could not possibly reach our trenches on the top of Chunuk Bair in time to take effective part in the fight he deployed for attack where he stood, i.e.. at the farm to the left of the New Zealand Brigade's trenches on Rhododendron Spur.. Now his men were coming on in fine style, and, just as the Turks topped the. ridge with shouts of elation, two companies of the 6th East Lancashire Regiment, together with the 10th Hampshire Regiment, eltarged up our side of the slope with the bayonet. They had gained the high ground immediately below the commanding knoll on Chunuk Bair, and a few minutes earlier would have joined hands with the Gurkhas and South Lancashires, and, com- bined with them, wOuld have carried all be- fore them. But the Turks by this time were lining the whole of the high crest in 'Over- whelming numbers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>British Pressed Back.</head>
        <p>British Pressed Back.</p>
        <p>The New Army troops attacked with a fine audacity, but they were flung back from the height and then pressed still further down the slope, until General Baldwin had to withdraw his command to the vicinity of the farm, whilst the enemy, much encouraged, turned their attention to the New Zealand troops and the two New Army battalions of No. I Column still holding the south-west half of the main knoll of Chunuk Bair, Constant attacks, urged with .fanatical persistence were met here with a sterner resolution, aml although, at the end of the day, our troops were greatly exhausted. they still kept their footing on the'summit. • That evening our line ran along Rhododen- . _ _</p>
        <p>dron Spur up to the crest of Cliunuk Bair, where about 200 yards were. occupied and held by some 800 men. Slight trenches had hastily been dug, but the fatigue of the New Zea- landers and the fire of the enemy had pre- vented solid work being done. The trenches in many places were not more than a few inches deep. They were not protected by wire. Also many officers are of opinion that they _ _</p>
        <p>had not been well sited in the first instance. During the night of the 9th-10th the New Zealand and New Army troops on Chunuk Bair were relieved. For three days and three nights they had been ceaselessly fighting. They were half-dead with fatigue, and owing to the difficulty of bringing up supplies it was neces- sary to withdraw them.</p>
        <p>The two battalions of the New Army chosen to hold Chunuk Bair ,were the 6th Loyal North .Lancashire Regiment and the sth Witt-4kire Regiment,</p>
        <p>The first of ' these arrived in good. time and occupied the trenches. Even in :the dark TIPs. their commanding officer, Lieutenant-Colonel Levinge,. recognised bow dangerously these trenches were sited, and he began at once to !lig observation posts on the actual crest, and strengthen the defences where he could. But he had not time given him to do much. The second battalion, the Wiltshires, were delayed by the intricate country. .They did .not reach the edge of the entrenchment until 4 a.m.,•and were then told to lie down in what was believed, erroneously, to be a covered position.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>North Lancashires Overwhelmed.</head>
        <p>North Lancashires Overwhelmed.</p>
        <p>At daybreak on Tuesday, August 10th, the Turks delivered a grand attack from the *line Chunak Bair Hill Q against these two bat- talions, already weakened in numbers, though</p>
        <p>not in spirit, by previous fighting. The North Lancashire men were simply over- whelmed in their shallow trenches by sheer weight of numbers, whilst the Wilts, who were caught out in the open, were literally almost annihilated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>NOT NEUTRAL NOW.</head>
        <p>NOT NEUTRAL NOW.</p>
        <p>Trades Council Oppose Compul- sion In Any Case.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Trades Council were neutral on the question of recruiting. They have left that attitude now—or their Executive Com- mittee have. That body met on Monday even- ing and decided to send Mr. Charles Dukes (the secretary) to London to attend the Labour Con- ference which was held on Thursday to discuss the Prime Minister's, Bill.</p>
        <p>A prominent member of the executive has informed an " Examiner " representative that judging from the attitude of the Council in the past, he is of. opinion that the members would be opposed to compulsory military ,service of arV description. Asked if the Council would be affected by the publication of the Derby figures, he said that this did not make any difference. The Council were opposed to compulsion in any circum- stances whatever.</p>
        <p>At a Christmas dinner in Washington a statesman was called upon after the meal to make a speech. lie rose and began:</p>
        <p>"You have been giving your attention so far to a. turkey stuffed with sage. You are now about, to give your attention. to a sop stuffed with turkey."-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 8, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 8, 1916.</p>
        <p>THE '''OLiTiCS OF THE YEAR.</p>
        <p>Coalition Government Take Away Semblance Of Party Management.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>NATiON'S RESOURCES MOBILISED.</head>
        <p>NATiON'S RESOURCES MOBILISED.</p>
        <p>The end of the year brings us also to the close of the seventeenth month of the greatest war that the world has ever seen. The broad features of the course which the war has fol- lowed during 1915 can be described in two or three sentences. While on the Western Front the Allies have achieved some substantial gains, there was for a long time a position of stalemate there, of which Germany took advantaga to make a tremendous onslaught upon the Russian forces. She failed to win a decisive result, either by overthrowing Russia or making her willing to arrange peace, but she succeeded in creating an impression in the minds of the Balkan Rulers that the Central Powers were bound to win. Bulgaria's -attack upon Serbia, in conjunction with Germany and Austria-Hungary, was replied to by an Anglo-French expedition. This expedi- tion was decided upon by the Entente under circumstances which had become radically altered before it had even been despatched. M. Venizelos, who had invited the co-operation of the Allies, was dismissed by King Constantine, and for a considerable time it was doubtful whether they could depend, in using Salonica as the base for their efforts to relieve Serbia, even on the benevolent neutrality of Greece. The effect of the new situation in the Balkans, by affording Germany the opportunity to establish communications with. Constantinople by land, made it desirable for the Allies to reconsider their position at Gallipoli, and our force q have been withdrawn without loss from Suvla Bay and Anzac to be employed in another locality. Early in the year—on February 4th, to be exact. —the German Admiralty declared "the 'waters around Great Britain and Ireland . . . a war area." Germany believed that she could starve out Great Britain by using submarines to tor-</p>
        <p>case of men who, though unstarred, may claim to be really- indispensable in. their various occu- pations, local tribunals are to be provided for deciding whether exemption should be granted on this ground. (3) To mobilise the nation's. resources of skilled industry and trade "for furthering the efficient manufacture, transport, and supply of munitions," the Government has provided for the control by the Ministry of munitions of any establishment in which muni- tion work is carried on. This control involves (a) tile prohibitiOn of lock-outs and strikes; (h) the limitation of profits; (c) the regulation. of rates of wages and discipline; (d) the prohibi- tion of transference of labour to another estab- lishment except under a certificate from a Muni- tions Tribunal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RE-CONSTRUCTION OF THE CABINET.</head>
        <p>THE RE-CONSTRUCTION OF THE CABINET.</p>
        <p>The re-construction of the Government on a broader basis necessitated the ret;rement of a number of Ministers who had done excellent work, but whose places were required for mem- bers of the Opposition whom the Prime Minister 'wished to include in the Government. Mr. al- four became First Lord of the Admiralty, Mr. Bonar Law Colonial Secretary, Mr. Austen Chamberlain Secretary of State for India, and Mr. Walter Long President of the Local Govern- ment Board. Lord Lansdowne, Lord Selborne, and Lord Curzon also joined the Cabinet, and there were many changes in minor offices. Sir Stanley Buckmaster succeeded Lord Haldane as Lord Chancellor, and Sir Edward Carson was made Attorney-General, with a seat in the Cabinet. Mr. Gulland was appointed Joint Chief Whip with Lord Edmund Talbot. Mr. McKenna took over the Treasury on the creation of a Ministry of Munitions, for which Mr. Lloyd George was obviously. the ideal selection. On</p>
        <p>land issue in stock or bonds; smaller bonds of £5 or multiples of „t 5 up to .V2OO, obtainable through the Post Office; and War Loan Vouchels of 55., 10s., and £l, obtainable at the Post Office, or through the medium of the trade unions, friendly societies, and various works offices. Provision was made for the conver-sion of the old War Loan and of Consols and Annuities by those who subscribed proportionately to the New Loan. The Loan proved a gigantic success. The actual number of subscribers to the Loan through the Bank of England was 550,000, and the actual amount subscribed £550,000,000. But the methods adopted for attracting the working classes to invest their savings did not meet with the response expected, and Mr. McKenna de- cided to offer to this class of investor the in- ducement .of bonds, which at a somewhat lower rate of interest, would always be realisable at par.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>"GERMANY A BEATEN NATION."</head>
        <p>"GERMANY A BEATEN NATION."</p>
        <p>, In the debate on the motion for the adjourn- ment over Christmas, there was a discussion' On the problem of transport, due to the necessity of taking so many ships for conveying troops and supplies to the 'Front. Mr. Balfour pointed out that the demands of those responsible for military operations had to be considered before everything else, and the best remedy for the scarcity of tonnage was the encouragement of the building of merchant ships, which had almost ceased since the beginning of the war. Mr. Runciman, in a striking speech, declared that "so far as commerce was concerned, Ger- many was a beaten nation, .and the Govern- ment was taking care that she (lid not get her head up after the war?' Lord Robert Cecil ad- ministered a scathing rebuke to the group of malcontents who insinuate • that the Foreign Office is influenced by prejudice in favour of Germany, so that it does not do its best- to pre- vent supplies going to Germany. Lord Robert Cecil: "I think we should be extremely careful how we make such charges against people who are honestly trying to serve their country. There are some people who are not able to bear the stress of war; they are merely hysterical neurotics; when things are not going, right for the moment they seem to think the proper thing is to turn round on those who are trying to serve their country and say, 'You are traitors; we are betrayed; you are the friends of our enemies, and that is the reason we are not succeeding.' I say it is a disgrace- ful state of things; it is destructive of the moral</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>pedo her merchant ships, and she was in nowise deterred from adopting the plan by the know- ledge that it would in many cases condemn the crews and passengers to certain death. Great Britain retaliated by enforcing measures "to prevent commodities of any kind from reaching or leaving the German Empire . . . without risk to neutral ships or to neutral or non-com- batant lives." The German piracy was marked by atrocities which shocked the whole civilised world and provoked strong and repeated pro- tests from the Government of the 'United Stites. But our Navy found means of grappling effec- tually with the submarine menace, while tight- ening its strangle-hold upon the source of Ger- , man3,-'s supplies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>POLITICAL AND SOCIAL RE-ORGANISA-</head>
        <p>POLITICAL AND SOCIAL RE-ORGANISA-</p>
        <p>TION.</p>
        <p>The exigencies of war have necessarily prO- duced grc-at modifications in our political and social organisation. (1) The past year has seen</p>
        <p>the Government reconstructed on a basis which was intended, in Mr. Asquith's words, to "take away from it even .the semblance of a one-sided or party character," and to demonstrate to the whole world—Allies, enemies, and neutral-that "the Ilritish people were more resolute than ever with one heart and with one purpose to obliterate all distinctions, and to unite every personal and political, as well as every moral and material force, to the prosecution of their cause." (2) In order to eatable the Government to ascertain what resources the nation can com- mand in this great national 'crisis for maintain- ing, on the one hand, our Army and Nay, and on the other our industrial and financial posi- tion, a measure was passed for compulsory regis- tration of all the people, male and female, be- tween the ages of fifteen and sixty-five. Under a. scheme laid down by Lord Derby, who was appointed Director of Recruiting,' every man of military age not "starred" as employed in an indispensable occupation, was called upon to offer himself for attestation, if found physically fit, and either to join the colours at once or be placed in the Army Reserve to be called up as required. The latter class of recruits is divided into two series of groups, married and single, according to ages, and Mr. Asquith pledged him- self ih unambiguous terms, that the married men will not be called upon unless and until all but the inconsiderable minority, of recruit- able single men have been called up. In the</p>
        <p>October 18th Sir Edward Carson resigned, de- claring that he found himself "unable to agree in any respect" with what he understood to be the Government's policy in the Balkans. Mr. Churchill, who had accepted the office of Chan- cellor of the Duchy of Lancaster on vacating the Admiralty, resigned on November 11th. The Prime Minister was unable to give him a seat on the new War Council, and Mr. Churchill felt that "he could not accept a position of general responsibility without any effective share in it guidance and control."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>MR. McKENNA'S BUDGET.</head>
        <p>MR. McKENNA'S BUDGET.</p>
        <p>Mr. McKenna's Budget, though in certain details it came in, for some sharp criticism, wat universally 'acknowledged to be an achievement of sound and brilliant finance. In regard to direct taxation, it increased the income tax rates by 40 per cent. (of which only 20 per ceot. will be payable in the current financial year, 1915- 16), reduced the limit of exemption from .tl6O</p>
        <p>to £l3O, and .the abatement from £l6O to £120; increased the super tax on a gradual scale, rising from 2s. 10d. on incomes between £B,OOO and .29,000 to 3s. 6d. on the surplus of all in- comes above 10,000. An extra duty of 4d. per lb. was imposed on tea, making the whole duty is. per lb.; the duty on cocoa was raised from to lid.,. and on coffee and chicory from lid. to ; the duties on ilried fruit's (except currants) and tobacco Were raised 50 per cent., and on patent medicines were doubled, and the duty on motor spirit was raised by 3d,' a galhin. A higher duty on sugar was partly compAnsated by a reduction in the price of sugar supplied by the Royal Commission, with the net result that the rise in the retail price would be 0. per lb. ImPort duties of 331 per cent. were imposed on imported motor cars and Cycles and parts thereof (except tyres) and business cars, cinema films, clocks and parts, and musical instru- ments. There was also an iperease in post office charges, though the proposal to abolish the half- penny post was withdrawn. Excess profit, due to the war, after thb deduction of £lOO, will be taxed 50 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>GREAT WAR LOAN.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT WAR LOAN.</p>
        <p>A new War Loan (there had already been one of £350,000,000, issued at 95, and producing £331,000,000) was announced on June 21st. No preCise sum was asked for. There were three methods of issue: the ordinary rank of Eng-</p>
        <p>of this country, and it is the duty of all patriotic men to stamp upon it the moment the charge is made."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>LOOKING BACKWARDS.</head>
        <p>LOOKING BACKWARDS.</p>
        <p>When we look back on the past year we have to admit that we have made mistakes, and we have received some heavy blows. Such is the in- variable fortune of every nation in war, how- ever successful the ultimate issue proves, and however capable its leaders may be. But the section of the nation which magnifies our mis- takes and losses is a comparatively small and insignificant one, and of the nation as a whole the words employed by Mr. Asquith at the Guildhall banquet hold good: "After fifteen months [it is now seventeen] spent in the strenuous and undivided pulisuit of .the supreme aim, we have only one party. The voice of faction is silent, and with 'a few insigni- ficant and sup7rficial cross-currents, the stream of our national life flows with gathered and</p>
        <p>growing volume in an ever-deepening channel of united purpose and of concentrated resolv. In the year which has passed since I last spoke at this table the whole face of the world, present and prospective, has been changed. It is nbt too- much to say, I think, that we here as a people lave had a transformation of our own. We have brought together the scattered shreds of our varied activities, and we have gradually but suidly woven them, to a degree unknown before, into a single web. Our whole life has taken a new,. a sobered colouring under the sub- duing consciousness of great issues steadily laced, and of at times the overshadowing pres- encb of sorrow and of loss. A year ago we were confronted as a people by a great and seaiching test. We chose what was certainly the harder, but we believed then and believe now, the better course. We staked everything on our choice, and we have no more doubt now than ye had then that we were right, and that we shall win."</p>
        <p>Don Juan in Khaki.</p>
        <p>Shop Assistant (to Tommy who has asked to see some silver name-brooches): "Here you are. Take your choice. Ethel, Maud, Gladys, Pearl, Dolly—any of these?" Tommy: "Yes, all those but Gladys. Ana I'll have Polly and Alice too, if you've got 'em."—" runch."-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>STORP:S FROM THE RENCHES.</head>
        <p>STORP:S FROM THE RENCHES.</p>
        <p>IMPRESSIONS OF FRANCE.</p>
        <p>"Tommy "Writes About Confirmation And The Cinema.</p>
        <p>As usual, this month's "Orford Parish Magazine," Contains some excellent letters from local soldiers and sailors. One in the "Unbreakable Fourth" says:— " Just inside the town there has been built a large Y.M.C.A. hut, where we can. go and obtain refreshments at a very small cost, and also write our letters there, with writing paper and envelopes provided free. Every evening a voluntary-concert is given and any 'Tommy' can get up and provide his comrades with a little amusement in any way he feels inclined. • "It was at this particular hut that the Con. firmation service. .took place, but owing to the Bishop of Khartoum not being able to get down, the Chaplain-General. to H.M. Forces in France Bishop Taylor Smith, offibiated in his stead, with the aid of the Senior Chaplain of our Division. There would be roughly about 120 men of our Division who received the Confirma- tion, and they were from different regiments. I think there were about ten from our battalion. After the service we were provided with tea by the Y.M.C.A. officials, at the expense of the Chaplain-General, I think, and he shook hands viith each one of us separately before leaving. "We have also got a picture theatre in this town, erected in a, large barn and managed by soldiers under the supervision of our Divisional Headquarters, and, I can assure you, it helps to relieve the monotonous feeling which often comes over ' Tommy ' out here. I was present at the opening last Friday. and I enjoyed it immensely."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>"TERRIERS' LOOKED ALL RIGHT."</head>
        <p>"TERRIERS' LOOKED ALL RIGHT."</p>
        <p>"The French people are very generous to us fellows," writes another. "They supply us with hot water when we need it, and make a fine fuss of us, and we. appreciate their kina- ness to the full, as we are • strangers in a strange country far from home.. "I am pleased to say I saw all the Warring- ton ' Terriers ' this week. They stayed in this place for two days, but have done on higher up now. I saw several of the Orford boys and they looked all right, and we were pleased to meet each _other.</p>
        <p>"After an uneventful crossing—except that the ship showed no lights and we all wore life- belts—we arrived safely on this side. Then a few hours in a rest camp, to gain which we travelled about five miles, and then a twelve hour ride in waggons to a village some distance behind the actutrl Front (I think that's vague enough for the most censorious of censors), where we remained until last Tuesday. At that place my billet, shared with about twenty others, consisted of a disused empty house of two rooms, where we slept upon straw on a stone floor. As it rained, snowed and' thawed all the first week, our welcome to France was a rough one. Our cooking was done over a fire in a large ,chimney, and our meals eaten sitting around on straw. There is an abundance of good plain food, such as bacon, cheese, bread, beef and jam. By the Way, an appetising mixture can be made out of bully, army biscuits, Mac9nochie's tinned stuff and any vegetable attainable, all boiled up together, the whole well done and stirred with a stick.</p>
        <p>"•We return to civilisation on every free evening by visiting a small country cafe, where coffee (au lait or noir) could be had served in cups. A decent glass of the French beer could be got for a penny. From that place we marched, or rather rode, over roads covered with frozen snow, for sixteen miles, to another area, about thirty miles from the Front. The billet this time changed to a room over the harness room of a chateau, the said harness room serving as the Divisional Signal Office. Conducive to Sleep.</p>
        <p>"This billet is not so bad,lt having.a wooden floor, which is dry and, from the variety of snores I hear, is conducive to sound sleep. "At present I am , working from eleven at night till eight in the morning, doing work which is something similar to that of a tele- graph office at home. The villages I have lived in have not made a very favourable impression so far. I miss the neatness and clean look ol an English village, while the sanitary arrange- ments are awful. Up to now I have seen very little of the war, being, as it were, on the outer fringe. The fellows I have talked with, who have been up there, express a quiet confidence. They seem to think that there is no chance of the Germans ever breaking our lines, and say our Generals have them whenever they want. We have got relieved for a good rest,. and I am pleaSed to tell yob. I will have Christmas Day .out of the trenches this time, as I had the last one in, when we had a talk with the Germans. Ant- I don't think it will happen this year. The weather is very cold here, but we can ex- pect that now, and it is a lot better this winter than last, for we Are more prepared for wet weather. Each man has a pair of gum boots issued when he gets to the trenches, and they I protect him from the wet, right to the hips. r You can wade up to the knees in it and not get wet. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>After Loos.</head>
        <p>After Loos.</p>
        <p>"I left the firing line a few days after the battle of Loos, when we had to take up another position at Ypres. I was sent to the Versailles hospital after three days in Chokes hospital. We were two days in the train. The hospital Is a large hotel largely managed by an English- man, and finding room for nearly a thousand beds. By the way, a strange fact about the visitors to the hospital was, that if a Roman Catholic priest came he would only, attend to those of his own creed—the priest was a French Count. The Scottish minister only gave to Scotchmen. But the Salvation Army sisters gave such things as writing paper, sweets. tracts, and the New Testament to all, in- dependent of creed. Then we were sent to England, and had three days on the barges which sail down the river Seine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>NAVY LAUGHS AT SHELLS IN SUVLA BAY.</head>
        <p>NAVY LAUGHS AT SHELLS IN SUVLA BAY.</p>
        <p>A Railor writes:—" It is very laughable at times to see the shells when they drop close to us, and hear the comical remarks that are passed by some of our boys. We all take it as a joke when they come flying round, as we have got so used to them. And then there are the different names of parts of the peninsula that our boys have given them, such as 'Fisherman's Hut," Deadman's Ridge," Happy Valley," No Man's Land,' and Hell's Gate,' which last is where the firing is so immense and terrific at times. It is a narrow passage between two hills. The Australians made a- splendid attack the other day 4round here. They advanced 600 yards and eaptureil a great many prisoners. Our troops are doing grand, considering the con- ditions they have to fight under, for they have to fight every inch of the way, for there are so many obstacles to overcome before a big attack can be made, and the place is very hilly indeed.</p>
        <p>I think it is the most hilly part of Europe. I am now taking part in a great struggle at Suvla Bay, which you have most proba,bly read a good deal about in the papers. Last week we had two thunderstorms.- It reminded me of a typhoon, the lightning fair lit the heavens. There is one thing about it—it keeps the flies away; they were a terrible pest. I think it was owing to so many flies that so' many of our boys have been down with dysentery. It is not so bad now."</p>
        <p>IN THE EAST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>IVarrington Man Says We Are Upsetting Bulgarians.</head>
        <p>IVarrington Man Says We Are Upsetting Bulgarians.</p>
        <p>A member of our reporting staff has received the following interesting letter from his brother, who is stationed at the base of the British Mediterranean Force :</p>
        <p>"During these last few weeks I have been travelling about the world a little, and am at last settled down. I received your letter, dated 30th October, yesterday December 13th, so you will observe that it was six weeks on the jour- ney. It appears that it has been following me about and, now that I havn stopped, it has caught me up. This move that I have had came quite as a surprise to me. We are giving the Bul- garians a rub now, and I dare say they will find us a tough lot. It was a very interesting jour- ney and the scenery was beautiful. Marseilles</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>COLD-WEATHER AILMENTS</head>
        <p>COLD-WEATHER AILMENTS</p>
        <p>Quickly Cured By Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>There is plenty of healing work for Zara-Bak in the cold weather, quite apart • from cuts, bruises, burns, scalds, and skin diseases. For instance, in cases of COLD IN THE,HEAD.—Itub some4kam-Buk between the hand. and breathe in the. medicinal odours from the evaporating balm: This loosens the stuffiness in the •nose, clears the bead, and relieves the dull heavy feeling. Putting a little Zam-Buk up the nostrils with the: tip of the finger is also very beneficial. • ,• .• CHAPPED HANDS.—Cleanse the sore place of any dirt, and then gently anoint with Zam-Buk. Zam-Buk reduces and soon ends the inflammation, pain, . and swelling. Zam-Buk should be applied liberally night and morning; and through the night wear an old glove ventil- ated by slits in the palm, and inside of which more of the Zam-Buk has been smeared.</p>
        <p>RH EU MATISM.—Sound rubbings of the affected parts with Zam-Buk drive out the pail and restores lissomness. The penetrating power of the pain-killing essences in Zam-Buk produces excellent results in muscular pains generally. Salt THROAT.—Rest the voice and inhale the fumes given off by rubbing Zam-Buk be- tween the hands. Extra precaution is to rub Zam-Buk over the throat and chest, especially for BRONCHITIS.</p>
        <p>CHEST CHI LL—Rub the patient's back and chest soundly with Zam-Buk, slightly warmed and spread on the palm of the hand. The whole circulation will be stimulated, and tight- ness of the chest soon relieved.</p>
        <p>CHIL BLA I NS.—Zam-Buk should be applied as often as possible by gentle friction. If the skin breaks, leaving a raw surface, Zam-Buk is doubly valuable because of its great healing and antNeptic power. Apply liberally, and cover w/ith flannel or carded cotton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>am -E•uk. •</head>
        <p>am -E•uk. •</p>
        <p>is a very nice place. I was there for a week and thoroughly enjoyed myself. Eventually I was dumped down at Solonika for a week. I don't want to go there again; it was an awful place. We lived on nothing else but bully beef, biscuits and jam the whole time we were there These things were served up for every meal. Unfortunately, while at Lennos, I missed Lord Kitchener by a few hours. I would have liked to have seen him very much. We were there nearly a week, and now lam landed and will probably be here for. the duration of the war. It is not 'a bad place, and I don't mind being here. It is mid-winter now, and we are having cold water shower baths. The heat must be terrible in summer. They are issuing us with topies ' (pith helmets) and Scouts' hats. . .</p>
        <p>We- have a splendid hotel to use as an office, and there is ample .accommodation for us. We sleep under canvas on the sea front, which is very healthy. We are getting good. food, and on the whole have npthing to grumble about:"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WHAT IT IS LIKE:</head>
        <p>WHAT IT IS LIKE:</p>
        <p>St. Helens Cable Company's Man Describes Treneh Life.</p>
        <p>Private J. Tozer, Devon Regiment, an employe of the St. Helens Cable Co., Warring- ton, writes to the firm as follows : 'You asked me to give you an idea what it was like out here. Well the best way I can describe it is this :—Go out in your back garden, dig a trench 7ft.- deep, 3ft. wide, fill it up half- way with water. Get up at 2 o'clock in the morning, put the coal scuttle round your neck, walk -round the town for four. or six hours (the coal scuttle will represent a pack), then go to the trench, get in it and stop there for four days and nights. For the excitement of being under rifle and shell fire get Your next door neighbour to throw a bottle or 'lump of stone at your head every time you put it over the top of the trench. This is the best way I can describe it.</p>
        <p>Held Up, But Killed Germans in Thousands. "L will try and tell you the way we went over the top on September 25th. We were in the trenches all night, at 5.35 o'clock we were going to have 40 minutes' bombardment and then go over the top. We went over at 6.50 o'clock. I was with the battalion bombers, as I had been put with them as I had passed my first-class bombing course. We went over with smoke helmets on our heads in case they tried to gas us. Well, we did'not know what we were doing at tle time, but We took,the first three lines of German trenches and two guns. We could not advance or retire as we were itl a hell of fire from machine-guns: shell and rifle fire, so we had to stick there. • . The Germans counter-attacked but could do nothing but get knocked down in thousands."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>SOLDIERS' IA ISIIES.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS' IA ISIIES.</p>
        <p>llcv. T. 'l'. *lames On Christmas•amonll</p>
        <p>The Troops.</p>
        <p>The Rev. T. T. James, who is with the troops in France, has written an interesting letter to the Wycliffe P.S.A., whose members heard it read to them by the Chairman (Mr. B. Farrar) on Sunday afternoor. "hearty good wishes for the New Year," he wrote, "Christmas has- been strange enough out here in many ways, but messages and letterA from the old country have helped us all. And we have managed to get some touch of the real spirit of. Christmas titne. You may be certain that the hearts- of all the lads here have been, very near home at this time, but though they have often wished themselves back in the old familiar spots, they have all kept brave nnd cheerful. We had a great service here on Christ- mas Day morning. There were ,hundreds of men present, and the Christmas hymns went with a. fine swing. It is always interesting to get news of the P.S.A., and I am glad that, as usual, you have been suppotting so many worthy objects. I hope the New Year will bring you much encouragement with good attendances.; and a spirit of enthusiasm. We depend upon you to keep the home fires burning. Please remember me to all the members of the meet- ing and wish them God speed. Out here we are always thinking of you."</p>
        <p>• • Goodness preserves the world from moral ruin; it acts as an antiseptic.—ll. McKellan.</p>
