      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>FINAL CHANCE.</head>
        <p>THE FINAL CHANCE.</p>
        <p>Lord Derby's report on the result of his recruiting scheme was published on Monday. It was, of course, known before the issue of the report that the number of unstarred single men who had failed to attest was regarded by Lord Derby as "far from being a negligible quantity," and that after earnest considera- tion the Cabinet had decided that, in order to redeem the Prime Minister's pledge to mar- ried men, a measure should be introduced into Parliament to compel the residue of available single men to attest. Mr. Asquith accord- ingly introduced the Military Service Bill on Wednesday, which passed a first reading on Thursday by the great majority of 298. It was with great regret that the House received from the lips of the Prime Minister confirma- tion of the news that Sir John Simon, who has done il'uch good work as Home Secretary, and previously as Attorney-General, had resigned.</p>
        <p>There are comparatively few people in the country now who are adverse to making the hangers-back take up their place beside their more patriotic brothers. We shall all—or all but the active advocates of compulsory service —be exceedingly sorry to give up our ideal of voluntaryism, but we must live according to the times; and a time that has caused between 5,000,000 and 6,000,000 voluntarily to give their services to their King and couniry 'is scarcely the occasion to talk of the liberty of the subject. The only subjects involved would appear to be those who desire fo have the protection of the State, but would rather stay at home and remain at home practically outside the State. So far as we can see, the Compulsion 3ill is a very mild affair. In fact, the remaining eligibles are given the chance to come in voluntarily by the re-opening of the groups. We hope they will see the wis- dom of submitting their cases to the judgment of the courts that have been constituted to hear them, and so prevent in the best and most practical manner possible even this sug- gested small breach in our Voluntary System.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>KAISER'S PRAYER.</head>
        <p>THE KAISER'S PRAYER.</p>
        <p>The Kaiser, in reply to New Year's wishes by the Archbishop of Munster, sent following reply :---- " May the fervent prayers of Oerman people imploring Divine aid to triumph over the hard tribulations which weigh so heavily upon them be heard in the course of the New Year."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>First Pantomime Of The Season At</p>
        <p>Warrington.</p>
        <p>The advent of pantomime in Warrington this week has meant crowded house's, for on Monday afternoon and evening it was nigh impossible to obtain a seat in any part of the house. The attraction this week is "Aladdin," and the management have hit on a popular choice. A brilliant cast, including such favourites as Dolly Ross in the title role, Eric Dudley as Abanazar, and Teddy Skeam as Widow Twankey, goes to make an entertainment of high-class merit awl excellence. The production is one of Messrs. Nightingale's, and is quite up to the standard of those famous producers. A bevy of charming chorus girls and splendid scenery, together with "gags " new and appropriate, songs patriotic and senti- mental make this pantomime a first-rate one. .</p>
        <p>A Play From The St. James' Theatre.</p>
        <p>The management of the Royal Court Theatre have for next week obtained a strong attraction in a play entitled "The Turning Point," which some time ago played to crowded houses at the St. James"Cheatre, London. The very fact that it is by arrangement with Sir George Alexander is sufficient to ensure of its being of high excellence. In an'eminent cast are included the names of Rupert Lister, Horace A. Whitmee, Kathleen Harrison and Cecilie Barclay, all of whom have brilliant reputations in London and the provinces. The play, which has an element of warfare, is by the well-known Belgian author, Henri Kistamackers, and has been translated by Peter Le Marchant. There is to be a bedroom scene in the play and the plot, we are led to believe, is both interesting and engrossing. It is the play for sheer excitement, and has had magnificent houses wherever it has visited.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>TIIE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>TIIE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>An entertainment of high-class merit is this' week provided •to the patrons of the Hippo- drome, and one that has been thoroughly enjoyed by crowded houses. The premier attraction of the bill has been awarded to Scott and Whaley, coloured comedians, who have thoroughly earned the position as head of the company. Their per- formance is entirely free from vulgarity and ranks as one of the best turns we have as yet witnessed in this particular branch of •enter- tainment. Their visit to a haunted house in search of old clothes proved a real scream, and evoked whole-hearted applause: Another item of note is Gyto, the jocular juggler, whose fare is of the best, his dexterity with billiard balls being especially clever. The Three Curtis's in songs and dances are also to the fore, and called for unstinted applause from an appreciative audience. Hensle, illusionist, assisted by Gertie Rees. renders his turn in brilliant fashion, and is really mystifying. Hall and Turn, tumblers, and the Reece Duo • in pretty vocal efforts, complete an attractive programme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>" Panto " Reigns Next Week.</head>
        <p>" Panto " Reigns Next Week.</p>
        <p>Promised for next week at the variety house is a gorgeous and up-to-date pantomime, "Robin- son Crusoe," the production of Messrs. E. E. Jazon and Montgomery. We have witnessed be- fore at this house pantomimes which have been of a high 'standard and, therefore, we further anticipate fare equal to former occasions. Special scenery and effects, together with bright and breezy songs, are to play a big part in the entertainment, and items of special note on the bill are The Eight Iris Girls, Joe, Joyce and Joey, and Angers and Escott. In all there are nine scenes, with no aggravating waits between. The title role is to be played by Miss Rosalie Jacobi and Man Friday by Jim C. Max. There</p>
        <p>is to 13-e -it matinee on Saturday next at 2.30 P.m., and notice should be taken that. Saturday evening performances commence at 6.30 and 8.30 respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>THE STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Another good programme was presented at the Star Kinema House during the firs,t three days of this week. The " star " picture "Such a Good Little. Devil " was shown in four parts, and proved to be very pc•puar with everybody. "The Hidden Voice," the eighthWisode of the "Exploits of Elaine," was very exciting, while a very laughable comedy, "Their Night Out." featuring, Sydney Drew, was, much enjoyed. " Pathe's War Gazette" was as popular as ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Next Week.</head>
        <p>Next Week.</p>
        <p>During the first three days next week, "The Heart of a Painted Woman" will head the bill, while during the latter half of the week the premier position will be held by "The Secret Orchard."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>"ABSOLUTE FEARLESSNESS."</head>
        <p>"ABSOLUTE FEARLESSNESS."</p>
        <p>Commanding Officer's Tribute To</p>
        <p>Policeman.</p>
        <p>The conduct of Corporal F. Rowles, of the Mounted Military Police, and formerly of War- rington, has been favourably commented upon by his superior officers. .He was on duty at the junction a two roads " somewhere in France" when shelling com- menced. He at once proceeded to the " shell " area to see if he could render any assistance to the troops or inhabitants. With the other mem- bers of the Mounted Military Police he returned to his post and found the streets full of female employes from one of the factories nearby. He persuaded these and other civilians also present to take shelter in the houses, and refused to allow the troops to stand about the roads. He pro- ceeded to control the traffic, and ensured the iree passage of the ambulance vehicles. Writing to his mother, who lives at Latchford, Corporal Rowles's commanding officer states: "His example of absolute fearlessness and calm in trying conditions undoubtedly helped to pre- vent panic amongst the civil population."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>SIR . ARTHUR CROSFI ELD.—Continued.</head>
        <p>SIR . ARTHUR CROSFI ELD.—Continued.</p>
        <p>known to us in Warrington, delivered a really splendid speech, which "brought down the house" in a'prolonged ovation at the close; but even more striking in its influence was the very simple but deeply impressive speech—not to be for- gotten by any one who heard it—delivered by Mr. Barnes. It well deserved the tribute of respect which was paid to it, as 'well as to the speech of Colonel Ward by Mr. Balfour im- mediately he rose to wind up the debate in a speech marked by all his customary ability and experience, and bearing the impress of his cultivated and gifted personality.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>SIR ARTHUR CROSFIELD</head>
        <p>SIR ARTHUR CROSFIELD</p>
        <p>ON THE</p>
        <p>IMMIIM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>COMPULSION BILL.</head>
        <p>COMPULSION BILL.</p>
        <p>TEMPORARY MEASURE &amp; MAY BE DEAD-LETTER.</p>
        <p>Sir Arthur Crosfield, writing to us, says:—</p>
        <p>As I was present during a great part of the debate on Wednesday and again on Thursday, it occurs to me that a few notes on that memor- able discussion and historic occasion may, per- haps, have some interest for readers of "The Examiner." .</p>
        <p>_ On Wednesday afternoon a House crowded to overflowing listened to the measured and care- fully weighed deliverance of the Prime Minister, and to the speech of the late Home Secretary, which immediately followed. Most striking in Sir John Simon's speech was</p>
        <p>his emphatic statement that he was opposed to the Bill on principle. There is, of course, a group of men in the House who take up the same position; nevertheless, even in their case, one cannot but feel, and indeed recognise as cer- tain that in the last extremity they, too, would be bound to admit that circumstances alter cases, and to act on that admission. If before proceeding to refer to the debate I may be per- mitted to say a word about one's own personal position in the matter, then, just as in private, I have said time after time for many a week that, in the last resort, I would not boggle at Compulsion, so I am glad to have an opportu- nity to make that statement publicly now. In my humble opinion this Bill was intro- duced in a needless hurry. When the figures resulting from Lord Derby's great undertaking were known, it seemed to me natural that-the next step should have been to summon every eligible, unattested unmarried man, or widower without children, to state before his local tri- bunal his reasons for not offering his services ,to the authorities. I believe that this process, accompanied as it would have been by an in- creased and increasing pressure of public opinion, would have reduced the residue of really eligible unmarried men to a minority, which would have been at one and the same</p>
        <p>time negligible, and, for all military purposes, undesirable, and I believe this could have been done long before the time came when the War Office required to deal, or for that matter were in a position to deal, with any of the large num- ber of married men .who have attested.. Had</p>
        <p>that course been taken, not only would the pledge to the married men have been fulfilled in the letter and in the spirit, but the Prime Min- ister's personal pledge, or that pledge given on behalf of the majority of the Cabinet—however</p>
        <p>it be regarded--would have been redeemed in its entirety.,</p>
        <p>Of course, I know there are plenty of people who protest against this pressure of public opinion as cruel and unjust. lutterly disagree. If ever there ought to be public opinion, and strong public opinion, with its potent influence throughout the nation, it is during a time of supreme crisis in the country's affairs, like the present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>6,000,000 MEN UNDER VOLUNTARY CONDITIONS.</head>
        <p>6,000,000 MEN UNDER VOLUNTARY CONDITIONS.</p>
        <p>Cases of hardship or injustice there may be. Would there have been none under a Compul- sory system? I for one glory in the overwhelm- ing a)nd ever-memorable fact that some six mil- lions of men are either serving with the colours, or have offered their services under the volun- tary system. The great majority of that six million men have come forward, not because they have yielded to Compulsion, or pressure of any kind, but because they have been actuated by motives of patriotism, personal pride, and personal honour. To have lost the willing service, which in gallantly and courage and endurance counts tor so much at the Front, would have been to inflict an irreparable loss upon the whole allied cause. Happily, there is, ef course, no question or pos- sibility of such a thing; and as regards the present position, 'now-that the Group system is re-opened. I, for one, shall not cease to hope before the day arrives for putting into force the Compulsory Bill it will have become (If this is ' not a rather Irish way of putting it) a dead ' letter. That was the hope expressed by the ' Prime Minister towards the close of his speech, when, with such emphatic and significant em- phasis, he declared his strong personal prefer- ence and strong unaltered adhesion to the Volun. tary system, and at the same time spoke of his great regret that there hail been any necessity for this Bill at all.</p>
        <p>_ Well. if we are to have Compulsion at all, and if the fast remnant of our forces—for it can be nothing more—is finally raised by compulsory methods, let us at least be thankful that, given these methods, the Bill is so fair and so moder- ate in its provisions. Let us bear those facts in mind, and also the fact that this Bill is also but a temporary measure during the war. I dare- say there are friends of mine in Warrington who are looking askance at the measure as being the "thin end of the wedge," and who distrust its influence and its consequences in the future. I think their apprehensions will happily prove to be unfounded. The tnagnificent total of six mil- lion men recruited tinder the Voluntary system is itself such a magnificent and unanswerable justification of that system as cannot fail to have its great and abiding influence on the whole question when the war is over..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>GENERAL SEELY'S ADVICE.</head>
        <p>GENERAL SEELY'S ADVICE.</p>
        <p>To return to the Debate. A very striking feature on the opening afternoon was, of cours-•. the speech of Sir John Simon himself, and Mr. Bonar Law's reply, delivered as far as I could see without a single note, and certainly a re- markable example of his ability, facility and lucidity in debate. General Seely, who had that morning left Flanders, reached the House be- tween four and five o'clock, and not many minutes after his arrival was on his feet. His vigorous and manly way of speaking are familiar and will be fresh in the recollection of many in Warrington, and on Wednesday we had a fine frank, appeal on behalf of the Bill, closing with an eloquent and fervent tribute to the limitless courage and sacrifices of our FrPu..l; Allies. "Let us," said General Seely, in effect, "make it clear to them beyond a shadow of a doubt that in fighting shoulder to shoulder with them we are prepared to support the Allies' great cause of liberty and justice in the world to the extent of the last shilling and the last man."</p>
        <p>Mr. Ahn Redmond immediately followed him, and his speech opened with a graceful reference to General Seely, delivered with all the natural courtesy and kindliness which, with his genial personality, patriotism and statesmanship has won for Mr. Redmond year by year universal popularity, and ever deepening admiration throughout the entire House of commons,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>THS IRISH POSITION.</head>
        <p>THS IRISH POSITION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Redniond congratulated General Seely, a;4! well he might, on having stood the wear and tear of war so well; referred to his tribute to the French, and endorsed his claim of what was due to our gallant Allies. It was, of course, well known in advance that Mr.. Redmond and his colleagues were opposed to the Bill but, as he at once explained, with him it was a matter not of principle but 'entirely of method and expediency. He failed to see that any ease had so far been made out for the Bill. If, said Mr. Redmond, it was made clear to him that the Bill would help to win the war—he went further than that and added, if it was clear that it would help to shorten the war—his whole attitude in regard to it would be changed. I only wish it was possible for me in this communication to attempt a more lengthy and detailed description of a debate • where pathos, and patriotism, energy and enthusiasm, sarcasm or contempt rang in +so many of the speeches. Dillon was severe in his criticism of the Wur Office and the Higher Command; and 'Tamar Greenwood, whose personality is, I am. su:•?, vividly remembered in Warrington, rose up to deliver, in ringing tones a reply which was a tremendous condemnation of 4.hose who, in years gone by, contrary, as he said, to War Office recommendations, stinted necessary expendi- ture.</p>
        <p>Yesterday afternoon Herbert Samuel had a quite dazzling success in a speech, ' in :tin course of which he subjected Sir John Simon a figures to a merciless and masterly analysis Many people regretted that his speech cou'd not have been delivered in time to be studied by those who attended the great Labour Congress which was held yesterday. Mr. Herbert Samael had a great reputation before the debate, but it is greater than ever now. Colonel Ward,also wet'.</p>
        <p>(Continued in-preceding column.)</p>
        <p>raiimewu</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>11 L 0 C L . .1.4 1.0 N C •</head>
        <p>11 L 0 C L . .1.4 1.0 N C •</p>
        <p>Successful Lady Chemist.</p>
        <p>Miss Mary L. Bamforth,-daughter of the late Mr. Joseph Bamforth, chemist, Winwiek-street. has been successful in passing the examinatilg of the Pharmaceutical Society held in Loud ri, and is now a qualified chemist and phuruilt- cist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Shorthand Successes.</head>
        <p>Shorthand Successes.</p>
        <p>The following Wood's College students were successful in securing their respective certifi- cates in shorthand at Sir Isaac Pitman and Sons' public examination, held at the College prior to the Christmas vacation :—Fifty words per minute: Florence E. Owen, Harold Shirt- cliff, Ethel Simkin, Florence Cain, Doris Hol- brook, Marjorie Leigh, Annie Mylott, Ivy Owen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Lancashire and Yorkshire Bank.</head>
        <p>Lancashire and Yorkshire Bank.</p>
        <p>The directors of the above Bank recommend a dividend for the past half-year of 16s. per share, subject to deduction of income tax. Last year the dividend was at the same rate. They add £5,000 to reserve fund and increase the carry forward by £lO,OOO. They have charged to reserve fund £lOO,OOO for depreciation of in- vestments caused by the war, leaving that fund at £565,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Attended the Council.</head>
        <p>Attended the Council.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Council only lasted about five minutes on Tues- day evening, when there were present: The Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock), Aldermen Tin- nion, Bennett, John Evans; Councillors Locker, Dale, Lindley, Wilkinson, Halliwell, Jenkins, Dickenson, Gough, Wilson, Skelland, Pember- ton, Cunnington, Chrimes and Chantler, with the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) and other officials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Mothers' Welcome Removed.</head>
        <p>Mothers' Welcome Removed.</p>
        <p>The Mothers' Welcome, which has hitherto been carried on at Academy-street, has been transferred to the Cairo-street Schools, where the accommodation will permit of the infants being separated from their mothers while the latter are giving undivided attention to the lectures on health. There are also' better arrangements for weighing the babies. The new quarters were used for the first time on Wednesday, when a large assembly of mothers and children were provided with tea.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Local Wills.</head>
        <p>Local Wills.</p>
        <p>J. R. Newton, Bent Farm, Warburton, 26,360.</p>
        <p>Mrs. S. Walker, Ship Inn, Liverpool-road, Warrington, £3,938.</p>
        <p>The la-te Mr. George Albert Kesseler, Haydock House, Newton-le-Willows, silk spinner, left £15,622 personalty and £15,785 gross. Captain Harmood Banner, South Wales Borderers, son of Sir J. S. liarmo.od Banner, M.P., chairman of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd., left unsettled estate of the value of £9,159.</p>
        <p>Two Hundred Happy Children. On Saturday afternoon, as in former years, took place the annual distribution of gifts of</p>
        <p>an ap-propriate nature to the out-patient chil- dren of the Infirmary. There were nearly 200 recipients, who were highly delighted with the treat. Miss Hammond (the matron) graciously entertained the children and their parents with kindly thoughtfulness and with light refresh- ments. The gifts were distributed by Mrs. J. H. Smethurst, assisted by her husband, chair- man of the Board of Management, Mr. W. Daintith, Dr. Seager, Miss Kathleen Holling- worth, Mr. Cook, Mr. Henry Collin and Mr. Cruikshank. All enjoyed themselves thoroughly.</p>
        <p>Births Down; Deaths Up. The report of the Medical Officer of Health for the week ended January Ist shows that there were 30 deaths in Warrington against 23 in the corresponding week last year. On the other hand, there were only 24 births against 39 in the,corresponding week. The deaths were caused as follows :—Phthisis, 5; heart disease, 3; accident, 2; influenza, tuber- cular disease not classified with phthisis. cancer, bronchitis, pneumonia, one each; all other causes, 11. Fresh infectious diseases notified :--Searlet fever, 9; phthisis, 3; naembranous croup, 3; entetic, 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Old Lady Burned to Death.</head>
        <p>Old Lady Burned to Death.</p>
        <p>An inquiry was held by Mr. S. Brighoue (coroner) at the Police Station on Tuesday even. jug, into the circumstances connected with the death of Mrs. Mary Gill, a widow, aged 67 years, who resided at 173, Wellfield-street. Alice Hope, a daughter of Mrs. Gill, deposed that her mother spent part of Saturday evening with her. Alice Randall said Mrs. Gill visited her shop for some potatoes at about eight o'clock. and a few minutes afterwards for a bottle of stout. Later she was told that Mrs. Gill had been badly burned, and on visiting her house found a lighted candle on the table and a spent match on the floor. A sister at the Infirmlty stated that shortly after being admitted, Mrs. Gill told her her shawl caught fire. A verdict of "Accidental death" was re- turned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Leather !</head>
        <p>Leather !</p>
        <p>That, output was rather larger than is usual in the week between Christmas and New Year was due to the precaution taken by some buyers who, wishing for deliveries to be in hands early in January, and painfully cognisant of the delays which are now regularised in ralway transit, ordered on goods a week earlier than they otherwise would have done. Throughout the week inquiries were plentiful and callers were very much in evidence, and from what hear a very fair amount of business has been put through for forward delivery. It is easy to see a strongly 'marked tendency in prices to harden. Select classes have in many cases advanced already, and tanners are much more determined upon their prices generally than they have been of late; a contributory cause of this is, of course, the rapid appreciation in hide values.—Warrington correspondent of the "Leather Trades Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Cottage _Homes Children Entertain</head>
        <p>Cottage _Homes Children Entertain</p>
        <p>Workhouse Inmates.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday forty-one children from the Padgate Cottage Homes gave an excellent entertainment to the inmates of the Work- house. They were accompanied by the Matron (Mrs. Lawrence) and by several of the officers. Mrs. Hall and Miss Broadbent were present. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell expressed many re- grets at not being able to come and sent his love to the children. Several other Guardians would have attended had it been possible. The children gave a musical play called "Queen Butterfly." They had been excellently trained by Mrs. Hilditch and took their parts with great spirit. Tweedledum and Tweedledee, imperspated by two small twins, were special iaVOUrlte alni ectived.beftrty applau4e, Their little frock coats and top hats were very effective, but all the dresses were good, and Mrs. Lawrance had shown great ingenuity in arranging them. The second part of. the enter- tainment consisted of songs, dances and recita- tions by the inmates, some of them quite aged folk. All were extremely good, and a blind boy, Ernest Davenport, recited a beautiful poem, which he had written himself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Emmanuel Institute Social.</head>
        <p>Emmanuel Institute Social.</p>
        <p>This very successful event in aid of the Insti- tute Debt Fund, was held on New Year's Eve, and was well attended. It deserves mention that some were so enthusiastic as to come from districts not in close proximity to the church's work; amongst these, Fyodsham was repre- seated. Miss L. Davey pleased the audience very much with her rich contralto in the songs "Down the Vale" and "Japanese Love Song." Miss M. Miller (soprano) was warmly appreci- ated for "Dear Home Songs.," Mr. Wallace Ray gave the "King's Own ' and "Any Rags?" in an excellent bass voice, and Mr. Herbert Ditchfield rendered "Somewhere a Voice is Calling" very nicely indeed.. Mr. Gordon B. Starkey was the humorist, and gave two items which were much enjoyed. Arr. 1. W. Jowett and Miss E. Landon were the accompanists. The remainder .of the evening W Era spent in humorous competitions and games, the prizes being carried off by Misses S. Fish, A. Johnson, D. Atherton, Messrs. Percy Heacock, S. Oakes and Arthur Oakes. Three members gave the dialogne "A Criminal Charge,' which proved very entertaining. The social was organised by the Institute Debt Fund Committee: The Rev. W. T. Jaye was chairman and Mr. T. Hughes the M.C. The caterers were Mesdam'es Heald, Oakes and Lloyd.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Local Examinations in Music.</head>
        <p>Local Examinations in Music.</p>
        <p>Application is invited for the position of Acting Secretary for a Warrington Centre for the examinations of Trinity College of Music. See advertisement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Wild Flowers.</head>
        <p>Wild Flowers.</p>
        <p>No fewer than 2,223 fresh wild flowers were received for the wild 'lower table during. 1915 by the Museum Committee. Especial thanks were made to ten juvenile collectors who con- tributed altogether 342 specimens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Benefits for the Town.</head>
        <p>Benefits for the Town.</p>
        <p>Councillor Jenkins has lent to the Museum Committee a landscape in water-colour by H. Hilton for exhibition in the Art Gallery; and Alderman Bolton has given the committee "Modern Artists," "Choice Examples of Modern Etching," and beads found at Wilderspool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Police and Military.</head>
        <p>Police and Military.</p>
        <p>Permission was given to alli;len in the Borough Police Force of military age to enlist upon the Reserve, in accordance with Lord Derby's scheme; and it was left in the hands of the Mayor and the Chief Constable to give permission in suitable cases for men to join for immediate service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>Motor Traction or Horses?</head>
        <p>Motor Traction or Horses?</p>
        <p>The Paving and Sewerage Committee have asked the chairman, deputy-chairman and offi- cials of the committee interested in haulage to meet the chairman and deputy-chairman of their committee for the purpose of discussing the question of the substitution of motor trac- tion for horses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Gas Committee's Affairs.</head>
        <p>Gas Committee's Affairs.</p>
        <p>The Gas Committee have decided to purchase 5,000 gallons of petroleum spirit at is. 60. per gallon. The automatic system for gas lighting is also to be adopted throughout the borough. The committee has also decided to support the action of the Salford Gas Committee in applying to have the nanies of their unskilled employes placed in the reserve occupations list.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-rOad Wesley Guild. The literary evening of the Guild was held on Monday, when Mr. Stanley Jones read a paper on "The Humour and Pathos of Hood." He dealt with the humorous side of Hood's works, and then passed to the more serious, contrasting clearly the dual sense of the poet's imagination. He briefly reviewed a number of Hood's poems, including "Nellie Gray," "Bridge of Sighs," and "The Song of the Shirt." The lecture added greatly to Hood's admirers.</p>
        <p>St. James' Vicar Recovering. The Rev. H. B. Firth, vicar of St. James' Church, is improving in health. In this month's parish magazine he says-: "The opera- tion that I underwent on November 16th was, I am thankful to say, successful, but the course of recovery does not always run quite smoothly, and I am still confined to my room. "I earnestly hope that in a short time t shall be able to appear again in aft.! it 4be parish. It was a great disappointment tc me not to be with you on Christmas Day, though it was some compensation to hear how bright were the services and how well the church looked with the new electric light and the taste- ful decorations."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Territorial Wives.</head>
        <p>Territorial Wives.</p>
        <p>The wives of the Warrington Territorials away on active service, who meet in the Officers', Quarters at the Drill Hall to work for the battalion equipment fund; reassembled in large numbers after the holidays on Tuesday even- ing.</p>
        <p>Whilst the work was proceeding, a most enjoy- able concert took place. This had been entirely arranged by the women themselves, who elected Mrs. Clark, of Orford (wife of Regimental- Quartermaster-Sergeant Clark) as chairman. In addition to acting in that capacity she gave several items. The other artistes were Mes- dames Savage and Coxless and Misses Shaw, Bate, Littlewood, Newall and Ward. Miss Mather acted as accompanist.</p>
        <p>The proceedings closed with a speech by the Chairman and the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>Fire at Sankey Wire Mills. On Monday afternoon a firs was discovered by an employe to have broken out amongst 601310 yarn in a section of• the wire ropery at the mills of Messrs. W. D. Houghton, Ltd., Sankey. Information was telephoned to the Warrington Fire Station for assistance. Super- intendent Turner sent the motor engine, in charge of Deputy-Superintendent Cook and Engineer Wakefield, other firemen following in a taxi and on cycles. On their arrival they found a number cd the workmen keeping the •fire in check with buckets of water. A good supply was obtained from hydrants and a couple of jets were soon at work from the engine. The fire k was spreading towards the valuable machinery ' bri •the upper floor, but the brigade soon confined it to the area where it had broken out. Part of the upper room flooring was destroyed and also a portion of the roof. After an hour the fire was in hand. The damage is not serious and is covered by insurance. The cause of the outbreak is un- known;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Bachelors Entertain.</head>
        <p>Bachelors Entertain.</p>
        <p>The proverbial idea that man makes a poor host without a woman to help him, was entirely put out of the picture on New Year's night, when the members of the Young Men's Class associated with the Working Men's Mission Church, entertained about 130 guests. An ex- cellent tea was provided, during which the company were loud in their praises of the good things set before them. A capital entertain- ment followed, which was ably sustained by Messrs. S. Millington (songs, "Off, to Phila- delphia" and "Anchored "), H. Dutton (songs, "The Troubadour" and "Oh. no, John "), , Nurse (song, "Go to sea, my lad H. Foden (song, "The Roamer "), H. Foden and A. Morris (duet "Life's dream is o'er "), Nurse and A. Morris (duet "The moon bath raised "), while Mr. Harrison kept the audience in a Merry mood with his humorous items. At an interval in the programme the Chairman (Mr R. Jones, teacher of the class), made reference to the war, and read a list of the names of the 15 members connected with the class, who are on active 'service. Light refreshments followed the musical programme, after which competi- tions and games were the order until the close of the evening, the hosts taking leave of their guests when all had joined in singing "Auld Lang Syne." Much credit is due for the success of the evening to the Class Secretary (Mr. A. Dennett) who was ably assisted by all the mem- bers of the class, who formed the committee, owing to the depletion of numbers on account of the call of King and country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Miss Marie Houghton's Workhouse</head>
        <p>Miss Marie Houghton's Workhouse</p>
        <p>En terfainment.</p>
        <p>The inmates of the Warrington Union Work- house were entertained to a capital musical treat on Saturday evening. The dining-room at the Union had been tastefully decorated and a stage erected, the whole presenting a very pleasing appearance.. The programme opened with the prologue of " Pagliacci " sung .by Mr. Charles Lambert with marked effect. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Lambert contributed a number -of char- acter songs, and on each occasion, so insistent was the applause, he had to oblige with an encore. The songs he rendered were "Star of my soul" "Dear Jap jappy," "Jack's the boy," "My old Dutch," "E dunno where 'e are," "John James Brown," and "Goodbye, Susan Jane." Mr. Lambert also gave two dramatic recitals, each of them being excel- lently delivered. They were Kipling's "Gunga Din' and "The Charge of the Light Brigade." Miss Marie Houghton rendered a number of songs and was a great favourite. Her songs were "Till the boys come home," "Cuckoo,' "Annie Laurie," "On the march," "If no one ever marries me," "Daddy's sweetheart," and "An old-fashioned town."</p>
        <p>Songs were also rendered by Miss May Cook, who sang "Cope back to me," "In happy moments day by day," "Three fishers went sailing, Little grey home in the West," and "Blossom of my heart." Three pianoforte solos were rendered by Miss Doris Read. At the conclusion of the programme the audience, consisting of the inmates and a num- ber of wounded soldiers from the Whitecross Hospital, sang "Auld Lang SYne". arid the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Beddard had charge. of the. arrange- ments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>"The Way Out."</head>
        <p>"The Way Out."</p>
        <p>Mr. George Benson, of Manchester, will speak at the Adult School to-morrow evening The War and the Way Out." A Pound Day.</p>
        <p>Pound Day will beheld at R-addon Court Red Cross Hospital on! Thursday. All gifts will b: gratefully received, but the spe2ial needs ari. tea, groceries, fruit, vegetables, cakes and eggs.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Tea Party. The congregational tea party and concert of the Padgate Wesleyan Sunday School will be held on Thursday next. On Friday evening the Sunday school scholars will be entertained, anti the prizes won by, them will be distributed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>First-Aid Lectures.</head>
        <p>First-Aid Lectures.</p>
        <p>- A course of first-aid lectures for members of the Volunteer Aid Detachment and those wish- ing to join when qualified, are to be given in the Cairo-street Schools at 4.30 by Major Adams. The first of the course will be given on Friday. The numbers will be limited to 40.</p>
        <p>British Red Cross Society. The Hon. Treasurer of the local division wishes to thank the employes of Messrs. Geo. Plumpton, Ltd., Albion Works, for their sub- scription. of ..t1 10s. Id. for the week ended December 25th; and 15s. Bd. for the week ended January Ist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>For the Prisoners-of-AVar.</head>
        <p>For the Prisoners-of-AVar.</p>
        <p>The whist drive held in the Church Room, Stockton Heath, on December 2nd, in aid of the Prisoners-of-War Fund, realised the sum of .212 15s. This sum is being distributed for the benefit of the Lancashire and Gheshire men who are now in Germany. Mr. E. Stanley Taylor acted as treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>British Women's Temperance Association.</head>
        <p>British Women's Temperance Association.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above associa- tion was held On Wednesday, when the Presi- dent (Mrs. Owen) gave an encouraging address to a fairly good attendance. It was decided to. hold a united meeting with the Latchford Branch in Bold-street Church Parlour on 'Wednesday, February 2nd. •</p>
        <p>St. John Ambulance Brigade Hospital. 'ln last month's "First Aid " there is a capi- tal photograph of the great field hospital which has been established at Etaples, in France, by the generosity of the St. John Ambulance Brigade and its friends. Warrington assisted in 'this good work, but we regret to say to only a small extent outside the ranks of the local ,itivision of the Brigade.</p>
        <p>Billiard Champion's Visit. Followers of the ivories and cue are promised n especial treat in the visit of the famous whampion Lancastrian player, Mr. T. Reece, who is due to play a match and give an exhibition at the Liberal Club on Wednesday next, at 7.30 p.m. The proceeds are to be devoted to the Liberal Women's. Sewing Fund for soldiers at the Front, in consequence it is to be hoped that the followers of this sport will roll up in good numbers.</p>
        <p>Typewriting Successes. In connection with the National Shorthand .Association (Typists' Section) Examination the undermentioned students of Wood's College 'have been granted Typewriting Certificates : :Senior Grade Certificate: Miss Bessie Wright. Junior Grade Certificate: Misses E. Harrison, Ethel Oakley, Dorothy Burthem, Ida Grundy, Katie Eddowes, Lily Bailey. Ivy Shaw, Ethel Hardcastle, Mary Tanner, Ethel Wells, Ruth Kilshaw, Hilda Howarth, Margaret Thompson, Edith Saunders, Janie Dodd, Marian: Srne- .thumt, Ethel Povey, Ethel Simkin, Lily .Gregory, Josephine Small. C.L.B.'s Concert.</p>
        <p>At St. George's Mission Church on Monday. evening, a whist drive and concert in aid of local war funds took place, and proved success- ful in every.way. It had been promoted by the St. Elphin'd Company of the Church .Lads' Brigade.</p>
        <p>The room was crowded when the entertain- ment commenced, and the programme, consist- ing of songs, several of which were humorous, ;and pianoforte solos, passed off splendidly. Mr. Randall opened the programme with a pianoforte solo, and MiS6 Ross followed with two good songs " 'nacre's a Land" and "Until." Master Neville Davies gave "Slave Song," and the Rev. S. G. Stanton "The Gentle Maiden" and "Less Than the Dust." Mr. Brimelow de- lighted with "Simon the Cellarer " and "Little Grey Home in the West" " I Woke Up" and . " They All Went Marching In" were the titles of two songs rendered by Mr. Delaney, and the Rev. J. M. Buekmaster gave "The Little Irish Girl" and "To My First Love." Messrs. Brinaelow and Bennett sang a duet entitled "The Moon Hath Raised Her Lamp Above," and later Mr. Dennett sang m Good Company." During an interval, Major Brookes, on behalf of the committee, took the opportunity of thanking the artistes for their services, and said he was sure that everybody had thoroughly 'enjoyed the programme. Following the entertainment, refreshments were served by the N.C.O.'s of the Church Lads' Brigade, and whist was afterwards played until ,a late hour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>CHILDREN'S PARTY.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S PARTY.</p>
        <p>Three Hundred Children Aid Sewing Fund For Soldiers.</p>
        <p>Pirouetting into the John Crosfield Memorial Hall on Thursday evening, upwards of three hundred 'children took part in a Grand March past, while the pianist played patriotic selec- tions.</p>
        <p>It was the beginning of a New Year's party, organised for the benefit of the Women Liberals' War Sewing Fund, and, as each child paid a shilling and all the expenses had been defrayed by Lady Crosfield and the committee, a con- .siderable amount was realised. Most of the evening was spent in playing -games and dancing. That agreeable and ex- .citing sport known as "musical arms" was a ..great favourite. With the bays standing at .ease down the central rows, the girls walked round singing fashionable songs such as " Tip- perary " and "Who were you with last night?" . and, when the piano ceased to sound, made desperate grabs for the hooked arms of the boys as if they were in fear of losing something—in this case, their places. It was an interesting game for the youngsters, and the adults delighted in watching them at their play. That simple and lively moveanent—the Polka —was the principal dance indulged in, and the .children enjoyed it, _both before and after supper. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>Gift For All</head>
        <p>A Gift For All</p>
        <p>But the feature of the evening was the 'Christmas tree prize giving. Each child had a sift—dolls, guns, khaki caps, &amp;c., &amp;c. They were given out by two charming little girls— Miss Etty Pendlebury and Miss Hilda Locker— who were dressed as if they had come from Fairyland. For the dancing they detached :their wings. Both of the rooms which the children occupied were decorated with red, white and blue .emblems and, with the children dressed in their best, the aspect was decidedly pretty. Mrs. Osborne, the secretary of the Women's Liberal Association, announced that Sir Arthur and Lady Crosfield had sent to the children -their best wishes for the year, and the speaker added that it should be particularly gratifying to those present to know that so early in the New Year they had done something to benefit the funds which provided comforts for soldiers. The Sewing Committee were responsible for the arrangements and had as assistants, Mrs. 'Thomas Locker, Mrs. Lindley, Mrs. A. Percivat Miss E. Pemberton, Miss I. Locker and Miss E. Birch, Mr. Charles Sinclair, Mr. Osborne Sinclair, Mr. and Mrs. T. H. Edwards and Mrs. Shelton Jones. Mr. George Pehdlebury acted as accompanist on the piano, and Mrs. George Pendlebury was secretary for the function.</p>
        <p>The party was so great a buccess that its repetition next year is contemplated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>rERRITORIALS' CHRISTIUSTIDE.</head>
        <p>rERRITORIALS' CHRISTIUSTIDE.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Widocks Tells Of Rich Fare And Home Thoughts.</p>
        <p>NO FRATERNISING WITH THE HUNS.</p>
        <p>What Do Unattested Men Think Of Themselves ?</p>
        <p>Sergeant E. J. Widocks, of the Machine Gun Section of the Territorials, has written a glow- ing account of tj:ke way our local Territorials spent Christmas this year to Councillor R. Henshall, Sunnyside, Latchford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>"4th SMILE" DURING RAIN.</head>
        <p>THE "4th SMILE" DURING RAIN.</p>
        <p>"I have just received your letter," says the gallant Sergeant, "written on December 22nd, for which please accept my thanks. It always cheers me up to see your familiar writing, as your letters are always so cheery. Well, first of all I must repeat that stale old phrase of mine—l am quite well. How many times have I written that these last ten months? Don't think I am complaining, though. I hope to repeat it every time I write a letter, so I sin- cerely trust that my luck will continue to be in. Since I penned my last (with a pencil) the gunners have been doing trench work. They, have been going in two teams at a time, leav- ing two teams behind, and they work a week in and a week out. We have been on fresh ground this time—ground I said, but I think water would have sounded more appropriate. It has rained some. If you have had as much rain as we have had—well, I should say the umbrella-makers have had a good time and also think the rubber works have had to enlarge their premises. With all the rain we have one consolation—it rains on the Bosches also! But it does no make very much difference to us. The 4th smile is still there! I have seen the boys come in this week on several occasion 4 with the water squirting out of their boots as they walked, and ten minutes after have heard them singing The sunshine of your smile.' I had the good luck to go up to the trenches last Thursday week and spent ten days there, so my wish of a Christmas Day in the trenches was gratified. Good Things Saved for Christmas!</p>
        <p>"1 and the team that was with me said we would do our best to have a good time, so a week before Christmas we started saving the good things that came in parcels from our friends. By Christmas Eve, sir, I can assure you that we had a goodly assortment of things that would have turned our neighbours across the way green with envy. Here are a few of the articles :—Chicken, roast , pork, sausages, Potted meat, tongue (you know by this sign that your consignment avrived quite safely), lobster, pineapple chunks, cakes, plum duffs, oranges, apples, nuts and chocolate, and a good supply of cigarettes. Don't you think we did well? We do. The chicken provided us with a good supper on the Sunday night, and roast pork and sausages formed the menu on the Monday at dinner. I have lost count of the different meals we indulged in up to Christmas Eve, but the following was the menu for Christmas Day : " Breakfast: Tea, bacon and cheese, and H.P. sauce.</p>
        <p>"Dinner: Roast beef (cold), tongue, pickles, sauce and plum pudding. "Tea: Lobster, pineapple chunks, tongue, pies, cake, oranges, apples, nuts and chocolate. "Don't you think we enjoyed ourselves? I can assure you we did, and the only little clouds that appeared on the horizon of our happiness were those little thoughts of home that would persist in rising up. We should have been inhuman if we had kept these thoughts down. I and the others wished our friends could have seen us in our 'little grey home in the west.' In the centre of the dug- out, hanging from the roof, we had a bunch of holly, and strings of snow stretching across and entwined together. " Examiner " Almanac Has Place of Honour.</p>
        <p>"We printed some Christmas mottoes, invit- ing all and sundry to have a `Merry Christ- mas and happy New Years' galore. The walls of our house' we decorated with cigarette cards, •so on my next leave to Warrington it will be useless for the younger generation to say 'Give us a fag card, Mester.' I had saved the almanac out of my 'Examiner' the week previously, so that came in quite nicely and had the post of honour on the wall. You might say the 'Weekly Post,' as it will be there some weeks. Not bad, is it We had rumaged an old table from out a ruined house, so you see we were short of nothing. We toasted those at home in—but I must not say it! We are 'still going strong,' though. 'Nough said! Men As Plum Pudding Cooks.</p>
        <p>• "Heating our plum puddings puzzled us a bit. First of all we wanted something to boil them up in; and then some were not quite sure whether we put them in boiling water first, Or whether it was cold water.. After a Pro- longed argument we deckled to put them iii hot water, and as they were very good we must have done the right thing. To get some kind of an article to boil them in we had to hunt about, and one of us eventually found an enamelled utensil from another ruined house, and all our difficulties were over.</p>
        <p>" It was R very quiet day as regards warfrare, but I don't think there were any scenes like last year. when both 'teams' fraternised be- tween the trenches. And a good job, too, I think. You see the Runs were not known quite as well last. Christmas as they are now. Well, Christmas is as far off as ever now, and we are all hoping to spend the next in dear old England. We shall all picture the scene in the dug-out in years to come. I hope. It was a vivid picture—the underground dug-out, the holly and snow,- the table with all the good things on it, the smiling faces grouped around, and the grim and business-like mouth of our gun poking its nose out of the loophole. and ready to speak .600 times a minute if our friends came across and opened the conversa- tion, but luckily for them (or us) they didn't bother. I wonder if our friends at home ever think of the way we get parcels and kttecs?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Praise For The Post.</head>
        <p>Praise For The Post.</p>
        <p>I' I think we possess the finest postal service known. Our letters, &amp;c., come with clockwork regularity. To see the postmen and their assistants bringing in huge sacks of parcels and letters this last fortnight was a scene never to be forgotten. In they came, stagger- ing under the weight of Christmas presents, with the perspiration dripping off their faces. Of course, you would hear some inconsiderate person saying 'Are you carrying that?' Then 'Tommy' would answer in his own inimitable style, but as the censor may be of a religious nature I will not tell you the reply. This is as much as I can tell you of our Christmas, with the exception of telling you that when I arrived back from the trenches last night (Sunday) I found 17 letters awaiting me—and I was so tired; but, needless to say, I read them all before retiring. Rather a big undertaking after a nine miles' jaunt along BELGIAN roads. Trench Reflections On Derby Scheme.</p>
        <p>"I see that Lord Derby's scheme has met with great success, and maybe will do away with conscription, for the present, at any rate. We shall have a. big Army by the spring, but I hope it will not be needed. Personally,. I don't think it will, as I think the spring will see the end. It can't last much longer. Of 'nurse, that is only my opinion. I hope I am right, for I want to be at home for Walking Day. The weather is very bad; not cold, but continually wet, but as the days come it may be better. I expect the Warrington streets will present a desolate appearance when the different groups are called up. What is to become of those who have not joined some group or other? I try and imagine how they feel when they walk out and know they are in such isolation. Everything has been done that can be done to stop conscription, and if that comes into force it will be because of a few. I think this will be the last letter, I shall write you this year, so in closing may ,I wish you. Mrs. Henshall and your son the very happiest of New Years? and I am sure that 1910 will be a happy year if we only can procure peace at OUR price. This must be all, so will bay 'an reroir,' and remain,</p>
        <p>"E. Z WIDOCKS."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SA_TU'' DAY, JANUARY 8, 1916.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>Local Territorial Officers Honoured by Sir John</p>
        <p>French.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>HEROIC LIEUTENANT BOMBER.</head>
        <p>HEROIC LIEUTENANT BOMBER.</p>
        <p>In last Saturday's " Examiner" we gave a full list of the twelve officers and men of the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment who had been that day mentioned in dispatches by Sir John French for gallant hnd distinguished services in the field. We now have pleasure in producing the photographs and records of two of the officers which the pressure of time caused us to omit a week ego :</p>
        <p>SECOND-LIEUT. F. BARNISIL</p>
        <p>of seventeen men of the Regular Army, who were told off to " bomb " the German trenches from a "dug-out." Lieutenant Barnish man- aged to bring his men through their difficult task without a casualty. Lieutenant Barnish is expected home on leave in a few weeks' time. His two brothers are also serving their King and country. One is a Lieutenant on the "Endymion," and the other is a Second-Lieutenant ix the Liverpool Scottish.</p>
        <p>SECOND-LIEUTENANT J. S. FRITH.</p>
        <p>Mentioned in Dispatches.</p>
        <p>This gallant officer is the elder son of Mr. SL J. Frith, a director of the Mersey White Lead Co., Ltd., Warrington, and resides as Ascog,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Mentioned in Dispatches.</head>
        <p>Mentioned in Dispatches.</p>
        <p>Second-Lieutenant Frederick Jardine Barnish is a Warrington architect and his home address is Appleton Cross, Warrington. He is the son of the late Dr. Barnish. of Wigan, and was educated at Lancaster Grammar School. At the age of eighteen he was apprenticed to Mr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ARTILLERY HONOURS.</head>
        <p>ARTILLERY HONOURS.</p>
        <p>SECOND-LIEUTENANT It. W.</p>
        <p>CORBETT.</p>
        <p>Artillery Officer's Second Great Honour.</p>
        <p>Mentioned in Sir John French's dispatch was the name of Second-Lieutenant Robert Wilfred</p>
        <p>Corbett, the son of Mr. and Mrs. Corbett, of 126, London-road, Stockton Heath. Lieutenant Corbett, who is a native -of Orford, has served in the Army for twenty years. Previous to the war he was a Warrant Officer in the Royal Field Artillery, and left England with the ammunition column of the 7th Division, landing at Zeebruge. His Division took part in the covering of the re- treat of the Belgian Army, until they were relieved by Sir John French's Army-from the Marne. He received his commission for s.A.N.;ce in the field in the first great battle of Ypres, and at the storming of Hill 60 was .engaged on the ammunition supply. After that he was ap- pointed Adjutant of the Trench Howitzer Brigade of the 2nd Army. In this position he is doing very efficient work, and is held in high esteem.</p>
        <p>During his experience as a soldier, Lieutenant Corbett has done ciuty in each of the four king- doms, in addition to 'spending eight years in India. His wife and two children-are now residing at Ethelstone, Penketh.</p>
        <p>BOMB XRDIER CII AS. H. CHADWICK.</p>
        <p>French Medaille MiMake Mentioned In Dispatches. Bombardier Charles Henry Chadwick, of the Royal Garrison Artillery, son of the late Mr. Isaac Richard Chadwick, 160, Liverpool-road,</p>
        <p>was mentioned in dispatches by Sir John French. It will be remembered that in Nov- ember we reported that the gallant Bombardier had been awarded the Medaille Militaire, the French equivalent of our V.C. for some con- spicuous services he had rendered to our Allies. Bombardier Chadwick was originally a plas- terer; but six years ago he joined the Artillery. At the outbreak of war he was at Gibraltar. When at home he was a keen yachtsman, and was one of the crew of the Seagull, Awned by his brother, Mr. William Chadwick, and Mr. William Rustage, which won the Easter Sat- urday race on the Mersey about seven years ago. When on furlough he was a meimber of the crew of the lona, another Mersey yacht</p>
        <p>which he assisted to win several races.</p>
        <p>Gibraltar he was a leader or captain in all branches of sport, his experienee ranging from being in the boxing finals on "The RA" to having a leg broken in a football match there. Bombardier Chadwick holds Army Certificates as a first-class gunlayer, marksman, telegra- phist, signaller, and swimmer, and second-class education certificate.</p>
        <p>He was educated at Sankey Day School, and attended Wesleyan Sunday School and Chapel, Sankey Bridges: He is a member of the "Tom Sangster" Lodge.</p>
