      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>NO WARRINGTON VICTIMS.</head>
        <p>NO WARRINGTON VICTIMS.</p>
        <p>It has been rumoured pretty freely this week that a number of Warrington ladies were on board the Persia, but so far as we have been able to trace, there is, happily, no founda- tion for these statements.</p>
        <p>Mr. John Harvey, of Wilderspool-causeway, has received many inquiries concerning his daughter, Miss Ada Harvey, who at the pre- sent time is on the way to India. She left Eng- land for Calcutta on board the British India Steam Navigation Co.'s vessel the Morvada, on December 18th, the same day as the Persia set sail, and this fact has paused some confusion, By this time she will be safely through the Mediterranean.</p>
        <p>Rumour has it that Miss Florence Groom, of Hugh-street, Latchford, was also on board the Persia, but, as a matter of fact, sir: sailed for India on the Kaiser-e-Hind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <p>ili, aluisimm011111111,111111•11=1.1.0.24</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <p>Platt is a local Territorial. He has been in the Army for some time and was at camp when the mobilisation order was issued. He is attached to the Battalion bombers. Before the war he worked at Messrs. Houghton's wire mills at Sankey. He is at present in a hos- pital at Nottingham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>SERGEANT WILLIAM CRAVEN.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT WILLIAM CRAVEN.</p>
        <p>Wounded In Action, But Now Convalescent.</p>
        <p>Sergeant William Craven, who, prior to join- ing His Majesty's Forces, lived at 80, Scott- street, has this week written home to his wife stating he has been slightly wounded in action, but that there is no need to worry as he is now convalescent. No more particulars are avail-</p>
        <p>able, but Sergt. Craven expects to return home shortly.</p>
        <p>6REIETINCS FROM AND TO THE FRONT. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Best Wishes Reciprocated.</head>
        <p>Best Wishes Reciprocated.</p>
        <p>Very thoughtfully, the Officer Commanding the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment has sent a Christmas greeting card from the Battalion to the people of Warrington. It was posted to the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), who showed it to the Council at the monthly meeting on Tuesday evening. He mentioned that it was from Lieutenant-Colonel Brereton Fairclough, the officers, non-commis- sioned officers and men of the Battalion, ad- dressed to ". The Mayor, Aldermen, Council and officials of the County Borough of Warrington." The Mayor intimated that he had replied, in the name of the Corporation, reciprocating the good wishes. (Hear, hear.) , The card is one specially designed for the Division of which our local Territorials are a part, and the Santa Claus drawn thereon is shown filling the soldiers' stockings with bombs, nails, tin-tacks and other ugly things repre- senting "the kind of Santa Claus we may ex- pect on Christmas Eve." Then is appended the greeting "Wishing you the best of every- thing, from all ranks."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>ON HORRID GROUND.</head>
        <p>ON HORRID GROUND.</p>
        <p>Hut itatialion Has Earned More Leaves For Laurel Crown.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant-Colonel Fairclough, commanding the 4th. South Lancashire Regiment, writes as follows to the Town Clerk, under date Decem- ber 28th:— . •</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Whittle,—Would you kindly thank the subscribers to the Tobacco Fund for their kindness in providing such a handsome Christ- mas gift, which arrived yesterday? The cigar- ettes are most welcome and are very much appreciated_ by the men of the Battalion.</p>
        <p>We have hen having a fairly hard time of since our "Rest," and have also earned a few more leaves for the laurel crown.</p>
        <p>The weather has been very wet lately and the attendant evil—mud—is omnipresent, even if there comes a fine day such as we have had to-day—strong wind and sun. The sub-soil here is a peculiar one. Top soil clay for about 2ft. 6in., under that is a rotten kind of soil. It has been described as if, when the soil is dry, you spit on it and mark time two paces you sink in up to the knees. The description is something like a modern novel, founded on fact; anyway, it is .horrid stuff to tackle.</p>
        <p>We are coming to the conclusion that the rat population of Belgium is considerably more than the human population—Allies, Bosch and others. My servant caught a beauty this even- ing—lft. 4;in. over all, fgin. from snout to root of tail.</p>
        <p>Our friends, the enemy, let fly a cloud of gas the other day, but evidently our artillery and rifle fire was a bit too strong for them, as they made a bad start for an attack 'afterwards. I see from the papers the old town made a fair response to the recruiting appeal, hut there are still a lot who did not come forward. One hopes their reasons are genuinely legitimate ones.—With seasonable wishes, I am, sincerely yours, 131tERET0N YAIRCLOUGH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Excellent Charitable Concert At . Latchford.</head>
        <p>Excellent Charitable Concert At . Latchford.</p>
        <p>Although they have already done their bit, the wounded British soldiers are always willing to do more, especially if, by doing so, they can help a deserving cause.</p>
        <p>This was evidenced on Thursday afternoon, when, in the Parish Room, Latchford, patients of the Raddon Court Red Cross Hospital gave a concert in aid of the Children's League of Pity.</p>
        <p>There was scarcely standing room available when the programme was entered upon—a pro- gramme which was well sustained and thoroughly enjoyed by all. During the afternoon tea was served and Miss Broadbent took the opportunity of thanking the artistes for their services, and appealing . for more help to be given to the Children's League of Pity. She also presented a prize to Miss Gladys Pierpoint for selling the most tickets, viz., 57. She also complimented a little fellow named Gordon Bpberts, who sold 24.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
