      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>WARRINGTOMAWS DISTINCTION.</p>
        <p>Father Of Five Children Wins</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>DC. M.</head>
        <p>DC. M.</p>
        <p>CUTTHIG THE GERMAN ENTANGLEMENTS.</p>
        <p>For his conduct on the night of December 10th, when for nearly four hours, he was cut- ting the German wire entanglements, Lance- Corporal Wm. Keating, of 68, Catherine-street Warrington, has received the Distinguished Conduct Medal.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Keating is a man with the fighting instinct and, therefore, it was only natural that he should join the Army early in the war. This he did by becoming a private in the 11th Lancashire Fusiliers. He did about twelve months' training in England and then proceeded to France, where he has been in the thick of the fighting. He arrived home on Saturday evening on a 10 days' leave, wearing the ribbon of his medal, which was handed to him by General Plumer at the latter end of December.</p>
        <p>Relating the story of how he won the dis- tinction, Lance-Corporal Keating said:—</p>
        <p>Brought In Three Men.</p>
        <p>"I was in charge of four men and we were sent out to cut the German wires. We•were cutting for over three hours and cut right</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <p>_ CORPORAL T. GORMAN. • Recommended for Distinguished Services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <p>nine children of Mr. and Mrs. Gorman, of 32, Gee-street, celebrated his twentieth birthday this week. He is in the South Lancs. Regiment, and in the course of a letter he said that "the enclosed will show you what I did on the 25th September, at the battle of Loos." The enclosed" was a communication from his General declaring: "Your coramandin); officer and brigade commander have inform:4l me that you have distinguished yourself by your conduct in the field on the 25th September 1915. I have read their report with mile', pleasure and have brought it to the notice of higher authority." For two yearS before the outbreak of war Private Gorman served in the 3rd Laroash; re Fusiliers Special Reserve, and was called up ,in August, 1915, to- complete his training. Like so many in the early days, he was anxious, to get to the Front, and thought he had a better chance by being attached to the South Stafii Regiment, and his request for a transfer was granted. He proceeded to the riain war theatre in June, and figured fearlessly in several 43' ngagements. He is popular among his comrades and all who come on furlough speak highly of him both as a " pal " and as a soldier.</p>
        <p>Gorman worked at the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., for several years, but was employed latterly in the boiler yard of Itt.,.iqrs Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>'SOMEWHERE IN FRANCE."</head>
        <p>'SOMEWHERE IN FRANCE."</p>
        <p>The rugged erogs; the lonely hill; • The bleak sky hanging overhead; The barren plain. And al/ is still Around that spot where he lies dead.</p>
        <p>In life God gave good things to him; Contentment was in every breath— He did not yield it to a whim, • But he was faithful unto death.</p>
        <p>His country called, and forth he went The unknown to face across the foam. Nobly he gave his life, content. It saved the man who stayed at home.</p>
        <p>A Builder's Bon Md.</p>
        <p>James G. Houghton, president of the Building Commissioners' Society, was applauded for a bon mot at a builders' banquet in Minneapolis. "A poor foundation," said Mr. Houghton, "will in the end cause the collapse of every- thing but scandal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>SOUTH LANGASHIRES.</head>
        <p>SOUTH LANGASHIRES.</p>
        <p>The Second Battalion Leave The Fourth And Join The Eighth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>CHRISTMAS IN TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS IN TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>Captain S. T. Boast, of the 2nd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, writing to the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) in a letter dated December 30th, says : I am writing to let you know that the gift of cigarettes mentioned in your, letter of 28th November arrived safely a couple of days ago, and have been distributed amongst the men of the battalion. On their behalf I beg to send through you sincere thanks to the subscribers of this very acceptable gift. Perhaps you will kindly let all concerned know how grateful the men are for the "smokes." It seems a long time since I wrote to you last, and although I know how much you like to hear of the doings of the battalion, I am afraid I cannot tell you a great deal. In my last letter I told you we were then in the famous Hooge Salient, and I think I told you of the part we took in the affair of 25th September. Since then we have left the Salient; in fact we have left our old brigade and division altogether. A Pleasant Surprise.</p>
        <p>We are now ,further south in the line in a new brigade and division, and it may be in- teresting to you to know that our Bth battalion is one of the other units in our new formation. On our arrival- in the new situation it was a pleasant surprise for us to find another battalion of the regiment there, and I found many old friends in the battalion. We left our 4th battalion behind in the old division, after being together for nine months.</p>
        <p>The situation we now occupy is much quieter than what we have been accustomed, to, and we appreciate the change very much. The weather has been very bad for some time— almost continuous rain. The country has bee?t flooded in many places and trenches very wet. The battalion spent Christmas Day in the trenches for the second time, and although everyone had to work hard in trying to drain the trenches, all ranks were remarkably cheer- ful. Indeed, the cheerfulness amongst the men is really wonderful, especially under such con- ditions as they endure—often up to the kneos in mud and water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Enjoyment Near Booming Guns.</head>
        <p>Enjoyment Near Booming Guns.</p>
        <p>At the moment the battalion is in eupp3rt, hut will be back in the trenches before this reaches you. During our present period in " support " opportunities have been taken for having inter- company football matches and smoking concerts. The latter have been held in company billets in turn, and were all most successful. I dare say it will be difficult for our friends at home to realise smoking concerts in full swing with the guns hard at work fifty yards away. Although all ranks so thoroughly enjoy these little re- laxations, it is surprising how very quickly every one falls into his place at the least alarm. Men are always ready and on the alert. From this it will be understood how our men are able to tare advantage of a little enjoyment and at the same time be always ready to perform their duty.</p>
        <p>I cannot tell you anything of our actual local situation, and as I have no other news to send you I must draw this to a close. I send good wishes to all our friends at horns and with kind regards. Believe me, SYDNEY T. BOAST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>PROMOTED.</head>
        <p>PROMOTED.</p>
        <p>Sergeant W. F. Foster Becomes</p>
        <p>A Commissioned Officer.</p>
        <p>Congratulations to Sergeant William Frederick Foster, of the Liverpool "Pals," and son of Mr. James F. Foster, of Museum-street, Warrington, who has been granted a commission in the 3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment Sergt. Foster joined the " Pals " shortly after the outbreak of war, and, earning quick promo- tion, he was made sergeant in January last. He holds eertificates for physical drill and bayonet fighting, having undergone a special course of</p>
        <p>training in these subjects at Aldershot. He was born at Edinburgh, and was educated at Warrington and Wallasey. He has been iv. Warrington about eleven years, where he has assisted his father in the insurance business. Keenly interested in the drama and literature, he has appeared in several productions by the Amateur Shakespearean Players, acquitting him self well, especially in " Julius CEesar," "The Merchant of Venice," and "Macbeth." parents are natives of Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>GROUPS RE-OPEN.</head>
        <p>GROUPS RE-OPEN.</p>
        <p>Another Chance To Avoid</p>
        <p>Compulsion.</p>
        <p>A telegram was received yesterday (Friday) afternoon by the local Recruiting Authorities, stating that the Group System had been re- opened. This will enable men voluntarily to enlist and receive the privileges under Lord Derby's scheme -before compulsion can be brought about. There is only one thing that can -kill the compulsion bogey, and that is the enrolment of the single young men. There are splendid openings for those who wish to enlist directly. Recruits are now wanted for the Royal Horse Artillery, Royal Field Artil- lery, and for the Cavalry. Recruiting in Warrington at the present 'moment is practically at a standstill, but 'several men who attested under the Group System have offered themselves for immediate enlistment. Others who intend to follow their example are asked to take their armlets with I them when they present thzlvc ttt thEy Recruiting Office. 'Armlets are now being distributed to Ih members of the whole of the 46 groups, but a number of men who are entitled to them have not made their application. They are requested to do so as soon as possible. On and after Monday next the armlets will be distributed from the Recruiting Office at the Central Police Buildings, and not from the Drill Hall as hitherto.</p>
        <p>The Rectuiting Tribunals will in all pro- bability get to work a week on Monday. About 130 applications for exemptions from military service have been received by Mr. A. T. Hallaway (the secretary). These will be handed over to the Military Authorities, who will in- vestigate them with the Advisory Committee. If the claim is a good one, the application will be granted, but, if not, or the Advisory Com- mittee disagree, the application will be re- erred to the Tribunal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>ICI. . At,.•</head>
        <p>ICI. . At,.•</p>
        <p>-•••.&amp; , ref. '1#</p>
        <p>t ik</p>
        <p>British Red Cross Society.—This week has been issued a satisfactory balance sheet show- ing the receipts and expenditure for the twelve months ended October 31st. There was a bal- ance in hand of £92 Bs. 2d., which is very encouraging, especially in view of the many other calls for worthy objects.</p>
        <p>Church Parade.—ln spite of the wretched weather prevailing last Sunday morning, the usual church parade took place, a goodly num- ber being in the procession. Representing the Council were Messrs. Gotobed, Holt and Ingham. There was a good muster of the Home Defence under Captain Williamson. The preacher in .the morning at the Lymm Parish Church was the Rev. Horace Gray, who spoke from a text in Revelations. In the evening, the service was conducted by the Rector.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. Alfred Maies.—There *passed away on 'Sunday evening a staunch supporter of the Liberal cause, in the person of Mr. Alfred Mairs, who resided at Cherry-lane. The deceased, who w.as respected' and admired by a host of friends, was a gardener, in the em- ploy of Mr. Johnson, the Old Tannery, prior: to which he was occupied in the same position for many years under Mr. Swan Wick, of High Bank. The late Mr. Mairs had been ailing for some considerable time, hut hi a death occurred with unexpected suddenness. His age was 55 years and he leaves a wife and four children to mourn their loss (two sons and two daughters). His two sons are at present in the Army, one in Egypt and the ether in trainihg. He worshipped at the Wesleyan Church and was chapel-keeper for upwards of eleven years. The interment took place on Thursday, a large number being at the .rave- side. The last rites were administered at the Parish' Church, the Rev. A. J. Morton officiat- ing. Prior to the interment a short and beau- tiful funeral service was held at his home address and was conducted by the Primitive Methodist minister. (the Rev. J. A. Ferguson). Many handsome floral tributes wtre received, and the undertaking arrangements were in the hands of Councillor W. G. Groves.</p>
        <p>LA YMM</p>
        <p>LIEU T.-COLONEL J. E. G. WON ES MENTIONED IN DISPATCHES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>" MESSIAH."</head>
        <p>THE " MESSIAH."</p>
        <p>Performance At The Lymm %Yes'cyan</p>
        <p>Church.</p>
        <p>Those sturdy souls who ventured to brave the elements on New Year's evening—and it needed some courage and determination to do so, for the day hod been of an exceptionally blustering character, high winds and dripping rains prevailing—were rewarded by an excellent musical representation of Handel's inimcirtst oratorio,. or:-at least the most interesting por- tions co, such indeed • as. we have rarely. listened to in a village sanctuary. Only the decorum generally observed in such a .sacred edifice restrained some of the delighted hearers from breaking forth into applause, and hearty and sincere were the many expressions of appreciation at the end of the evening's work. The choir, which was augmented by the addition of a few - friends from Stockton Heath and some members of the Warrington Male Voice Union.• sang the choruses with clear arziP tion and unmistakable, precision. an adequate devotional spirit being manifested -throughout the whole, -a fine climax crowning the perform- ance in the masterpiece, the "Hallelujah Chorus."</p>
        <p>The lovely recitatives and airs with which the work abounds were exquisitely rendered by Miss Florence Gavins, whose chief triumph was in "Rejoice Greatly "; Miss Ada McHugh, who in- fused into "He Was Despised," the, pathos which it is meant to describe; Mr. Harry O'Neill, who gave the tenor passion music, "Behold and See" and "But Thou Didst Not Leave" in beautiful tone and with nice enunciation; and Mr. Herbert Ruddock, mho sang the various baritone numbers "nut Who May Abide," "The People That Walked In Darkness" and "Why do ,the Nations," with great confidence and spirited fervour. . '.Mr. T. M. Eastham, forperly the Conductor of the 'Lymm Choral Society, gave the organ accompaniments with rare skill and nicety of support, and Mr. T. IL Brazendale, the choir- master, conducted.</p>
        <p>The performance was given in aid of the Trust Funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Distinunislied Conduct Of Lymm Officer.</head>
        <p>Distinunislied Conduct Of Lymm Officer.</p>
        <p>The "London Gazette" last Saturday pub- lished a long list of officers and men honoured in Sir John French's dispatches of a little wlle ago, and it is pleasing to record that the honour has been conferred on Lieut.-Col. J. E. G. Groves, the popular commander of the sth Cheshires, for gallant and distinguished services and conduct whilst operating at the Front.</p>
        <p>Colonel Groves, of commanding presence and personality, prior to the war was. a director of Messrs. Groves and Whitnell, brmvers, Man- chester, and the honour conferred upon the popular officer is one which will be acclaimed by not only the workpeople, .but the inhabi- tants of Lymm and the surrounding districts. He lived at Dean's Green and has been con- nected with the Territorial Forces for many years, having obtained rapid promotion by deternitnation and !resolve. As commander of the local Territorials, now serving in 'France, ! he is highly thought of, as many letfers written home by the Lymm boys will testify.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Son In Flying Corps.</head>
        <p>Son In Flying Corps.</p>
        <p>He is the head of a fine patriotic family, for he has two sons serving King and country. Unfortunately one of them—Lieutenant Osborne Groves—is at present a prisoner-of-war in Germany. This officer was attached tO the Royal Flying Corps, and whilst flying over the German lines was brought down by -fire from the German guns. The storm of bullets and shells penetrated the wings of the aero- plane, which made it imperative for the daring officer to immediately- descend. The other son —Lieutenant Julian Groves—was attached to the Ist Cheshires and went through many stirring, engagements, at the commencement of hostilities, including the British retreat from Mons, where he ,was wounded, though fortu- nately- not seriously. Lieutenant-Colonel Groves, St the outbreak of war, was vice-president of the Conservative Club and an en-president. He also took great interest in the village in general and is greatly esteemed and admired..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>LYMM'S ANSWER TO THE CALL.</head>
        <p>LYMM'S ANSWER TO THE CALL.</p>
        <p>Eligibles, 531; Unaccounted, 69.</p>
        <p>‘Ve give below an authoritativi, summary ot the result of the canvass under Lord Derby's recruiting scheme by the Lymm Recruiting Sub- committee, in the Lymm Recruiting Area, not including High Legh and Thelwall. The figures, which reflect great credit On the village, and show a realb; patriotic spirit, are as under:—</p>
        <p>Single. Marrie, Total number of eligible men, ex- cluding "starred " men</p>
        <p>Analysis.</p>
        <p>Men enlisted and attested, and defi- nite promises to attest Rejected and in possession of Army Certificate Number ot men who state they are physically unfit, but not ..in possession of Army Certificate Number of -men on munition work, railways, collieries, &amp;c Post Office servants, police and civil servants Dead Removals, Employers refuse perm i.sion to enlist Conditional promises to enlist No adequate reason for declining to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Total canvased</head>
        <p>Total canvased</p>
        <p>FIRST TRIBE:\ 11 MEETING.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. J. J{ithjwas Appointed</p>
        <p>Chairman.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the first Tribunal in connection with Lord Derby's scheme was held in ' the Council Chambers on Monday night, when Mr. T. J. Ridgway was duly appointed chairman. The meeting was called for the purpose of making preliminary arrangements for the con- sideration of appeals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—On Tuesday evening the devotional meeting of the Wesley Guild was held in the Schoolroom, Messrs. IL Alderoft and A. Loxley. . reading the papers. Mr. H. l'lumpton was the soloist. There was a large attendance.</p>
        <p>New Year's Eve.—The usual New Year's Eve social of the Penketh Wesley Guild was held on Friday evening, the attendance being largor than usual, and a most enjoyable evening was -spent by all. Solos were sung. by Miss R. Loxley and Mr. A. Williams; pianoforte solos by Miss H. • Baker; and an amusing sketch, "Under New Management," by members of the Guild, filled the early part of the evening. After refreshments, a varied programme of competitions and games was gone through. The Misses F. Carman and H. Richardson, and Messrs. G. Dale, J. Moss, J. Faulkner and A. Watson were .the winners of the prizes.—The service in the Chapel was at 11 o'clock and most of the visitors attended; the preacher \%;as the Rev. H. T. Dixon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>Soldiers' Institute.—On Wednesday' evening a concert was given at the SOldiers' Institute, Wesleyan Schoolroom, Winwick. There were songs and duets by Mr. W. Kilduff and the Misses Kilduff, of Warrington, also violin solos by Mr. Ernest Haynes, of Winwick, which were much appreciated. Private Lord added to the evening's enjOyment with a humorous recita- tion. Mr. Kilduff was loudly applauded for the recitations "The Game of Life" and "The Soul of a Violin." Much praise is due to Mr. and Mrs. R. Ormiston, of Winwick, for, their kindly interest in this institute.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <p>• 6 . • 0 • 1. • _ . • . : • ' 411. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
