      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 8, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 8, 1916.</p>
        <p>We can solve that Delivery roblem for you</p>
        <p>P</p>
        <p>May we call round and demonstrate the value of a 41 I I 17r1ora 1 IN a e Delivery—Van Don't forget it means more business and greater facilities for sending the goods home</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Tram Service Suspended.—Lately the tram service between Warrington and Stockton Heath has not been working at all well, and things came to a head on Friday morning when the ser- vice was entirely suspended for a number of hours between Stafford-road and Stockton Heath. Recently a car was, stranded in Victoria-square for threequarters of an -hour, and it was not</p>
        <p>_ until the driver had collected a number of boys and persuaded them to push the car about a hundred yards that it was able to 'proceed to Warrington.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Prize Giving.—The annual prize distribution of St. Thomas' Sunday school kook place in the. Church rooms on Tuesday evening. The room was crowded, and the Rev. James Collin (vicar) waS in the chair. Thy Rev. E. W. Clarke was also present. The enter- tainment was provided by a number of children,' and consisted of the following items: "Washing Day," "Sleeping Beauty," " Chinese Lantern," "Our -Model Policeman," National- Character songs; Coon Songs, Scarf Drill, Flower Bells. ' Items were also given by. Miss N. Berry (" The Dear Home Song") and Miss N. Cook (" Worse Girl in School "). During the evening about 150 prizes were distributed to the children by Mrs. James Richardson. She was accorded a vote of thanks, on the motion of the Rev. James Collin.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Tea and Prize Distribution.—The annual tea and prize distribution of the Wes- leyan Sunday school was held on New Year's Day. Tea was served to the younger scholars, and then to the older classes, after which the scholars assembled in the schoolroom, accom- panied by their parents. Mr. Walter Twiss, one of the superintendents, presided and wished all present "A Happy New Year." The school was favoured with .the presence of two old scholars in the persons.of Mr. and Mrs. W. J. Spensley, of Manchester, who take a deep interest in the school. Mr.. Spensley gave an address to .the scholars, and afterwards Mrs. Spensley pre- sented the prizes to over 80 scholars for regular attendance. A short programme, consisting of recitations by Minnie Williams, Clara Worrell, Arthur Spencer, Mary Ramshottom. Emily Wil- liams, Edith Twiss and Sarah Gamble was gone. through, following which Miss .Ada Ramsbottom contributed a song very sweetly. A hearty vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs'. Spensley was pro- posed by Mr. Henry Brereton, seconded by Mr. C. Wilcockson, and. carried. .11-r T. Brereton and Mr. G. Whitfield 'were the secretaries of a committee of lady teachers and friends who car- ried out the arrangements for the tea.</p>
        <p>preted by the school children, who had heen most carefully trained by -Mr. T. Albinson. Mr. T. Critchley, who presided, delivered a brief address, in which he ,expressed the hope that ere long the present terrible conflict would be ended, a wish which the audiencp heartily endorsed.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the scholars of the above Church participated in their annual treat, and truly may it be said that the children • had a right royal time. The sumptuous repast rapidly diminished. before their youthful appetites, this part of the programme having been eagerly an- ticipated. The entertainment following was a varied one, and was thoroughly enjoyable to both young and old.</p>
        <p>A ,repetition of Monday's humorous dialogue was rendered, the following youthful artistes acquitting themselves with praiseworthy Unction : Lydia Foden, Lydia Upton, Mabel Shackles, Marjorie Plimpton, Mary Gough, May Williams, Cissie Peters, Sydney Barton. Cyril Plant, Frank Peters,' Horace Banner and Harry Foden. Miss Blodwen Jones, a most promising young vocalist, whose contributions were muclt appreciated, sang "A mother and her child in English and Welsh, to the great interest of the audience.</p>
        <p>The -a-c-Companist was Miss Gladys Foden. Prizes were then distributed to the scholars for regular attendance by Superintendents Nelson and Henshall.</p>
        <p>f -: -- ;..,-,..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>HOME ON LEAVE.</head>
        <p>HOME ON LEAVE.</p>
        <p>Artillerman's Long Arnv Career.</p>
        <p>Driver James Whitley, who is a son of Mr. J. Whitley, of the Walton-road Post Office, arrived home last week on leave. For a short period Driver Whitley—who is a member of the Royal Field Artillery—was a patient .at the Lodge Hospital, Chislehurst, Kent, where he underwent an operation. This turned out well and he is practically normal again. Pre- vious to going into hospital Driver Whitley was serving in France. His Army career is a long and distiit'rmished one. He has seen ser- vice in India and South Africa,- taking part in the whole of the Boer War. He was one of the Mafeking Relieving Column, and later took part in the march on Pretoria. Incidentally he fell into, the hands of the Boers. His time expired • and the outbreak of the 'present con- flagration found. him in the employ of the Rural District Council. .He immediately joined the R.F.A. and went into training at Preston. On -November sth, 1914, he went to :France. Though he has not been wounded, he has had many narrow escapes and has taken part in all the big attacks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 9th January:—</p>
        <p>Parade (Uniform). Saturday, 15th January. Fall in at Head- quarters at 3 pan: sharp. Drill. •</p>
        <p>Tuesday, 11th January. Fall in at Head- quarters at 7.45 p.m. sharp.</p>
        <p>Musketry. Monday, 10th January. Friday, 11th January. Fall in at the Range, Bank Quay Bridge, at 7.30 p.m. sharp. For list of men re- quired to attend, see notice at Head- quarters.</p>
        <p>Orderlies. Sub-Section Commander W. Trantom. Private W. H. Hartley. By Order.</p>
        <p>F. A. FROST, 0.C., (Platoon Commander).</p>
        <p>Headquarters, Bth January, 1916.</p>
        <p>PLE %SING ENTERTAINMENTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Independent Methodist Church Cele-</head>
        <p>Independent Methodist Church Cele-</p>
        <p>'wales New Year.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at the Grappenhall Inde- pendent Methodist Church, there took place the annual tea and entertainment, which, by com- mon consent, was voted the best and most pleas- ing it was possible to imagine. Fortunately, the weather was better than during the previous few days, and, in consequence, there was an assembly of goodly proportion. The tea-tables were presided over by Mes- dames R. Nelson, R. Henshall, A. J. Howard, J.'Stanley, J. C. Hoult, F. Garside, W. Barton, 11.. Harding, E. Woods, W. Greenhalgh, G. H. Gough, W. H. Lewis, A. Griffiths, J. Woodward and T. Albinson, and was an unqualified success, the catering being of high-class standard, which, together with the artistic and tasteful display, reflects great credit and praise to these energetic workers.</p>
        <p>The entertainment which followed was excel- lent in every respect, and drew from an atten- tive and appreciative audience hill measure of applause and encouragement. Songs* by the choir were rendered in effective and praise- worthy style, due to a certain extent to the con• ductorship of Mr. G. H. Gough; a gentleman whose vocal efforts with the Male Voice Choir charmed and delighted us some years ago. The Misses Rigby and Taylor also gave of their best in suitable and appropriate songs, the former's singing. of "Land of Hope and Glory" being especially fine. We predict for this. artiste fur- ther laurels to her already splendid reputation. The humorous element was in the able hands of Messrs. Whittle and -Bibby, their items call- ing for whole-hearted applause. Mr. A. Dutton. elocutionist, also acquitted himself in praise- worthy manner, his enunciation being both forceful and clear. Miss Eva Shaw was the accompanist for the musical items. One of the features of the evening's enter- tainment, was a humorous -dialogue entitled Who will win the watches?" capitally inter- (</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—The usual monthly devotional meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on Monday, presided over by the Rev. A. J. Mor- ton. A very interesting paper on Banyan's "Pilgrim's Progress" was read by Mrs. W. H. Cutland, of Ampthill. Living in the district where Bunyan spent the greater part of his 'life, she was able to give a description of some of the places which Bunyan is supposed to have used for his illustrations in "Pilgrim's Progress," this little diversion causing addi- tional interest to the subject. The Soloist was Miss B. London and Mrs. Cottrell was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>• Social Evening.—Tho closing social of the first half of the winter session in comection with the :Wesley Guild was hejd on New Year's Eve. Miss I. Stewart sang in a very pleasing manner," The Land of Let's Pretend" and "The Dear Homeland," and Mr. Seed caused much amusement by his reading, en- titled "Love and Tar." A very interesting competition had been arranged by Mrs. Plin- ston and Mrs. Bate, the competitors being asked to ;guess the names of ten songs and. 10 advertisements, the various items being very ably portrayed by menibers•of the Guild.- The prize-winners were:-1, Mrs." Cottrell; 2, Miss Brough; consolation prize, Mrs. Seed. Re- freshments were served, after which games were indulged in until 10.45. The members attended the usual Watchnight Service, con- ducted by the Rev. A. J. Morton. -• • '</p>
        <p>Parochial Tea and Concert. —The parochial tea in connection with Christ Church was held in the Church of England Schools on Wed- nesday evening. After tea a concert was held, presided over by the Vicar-in-charge (the Rev. J. Hirst). He said he was there in the absence of the Rev. 11. S. F. Collier, who was away on military service. For several years before the war broke out Mr. Collier had held the posi- tion of chaplain in the Territorial Battalion, and it became his duty to obey orders like any other. soldier. He said this war was a testing time, and God was proving them in that crisis as a nation and as. individuals. It was a. great revealing time—revealing them to them- selves and to others. If they looked at the result an d the 'revelations, they had reason on the one hand to be profoundly humble, and on the other to be Profoundly thankful. Speaking of the good work done by the Rev. E. E. Chamberlain, whom he had known for twenty years, he said he had heard how much he had been appreciated by the people of Pad- gate and how much they regretted his, depar- ture to Tamerton .Foliott, in Devonshire. He mentioned the sale of work, which was held to defray. the ,cost of the repair of the fabric of the north wall of the nave of the church, and to repay he loan advanced.: by Vi-oolston to the fund for augmenting the living in Padgate. The amount realised by the effort was .2134 15s. 6d. The reverend gentleman went on to speak of the good work done by Mr. Dickenson, the late superintendent of the Sunday School and captain of the Church Lads' Brigade, who had left the neighbourhood to take -op other duties in London. He wished to speak on a subject which had been exercising ,his mind very much, viz., the church attendance. A former Vicar of Padgate said some 18 years ago that the church needed enlargement, but there did not seem any necessity for enlarge- Inent to-day. He did not think that the old saying was always true, that "a house-visiting parson made a church-going people," because: he (the speaker) had visited a large portion of the houses in the parish during the fourteen weeks he had been in the district. He was, however, thankful to say that those_ who did come to church took a real interest in the parish and its organisations. A brilliant list of -artistes had .ben engaged for the concert, all of whom whe loudly applauded for their efforts. The Misses Gorton played a pianoforte duet, Miss Elsie Hewitt 'sang'. " Valley of Laughter" and "May Morning" (both et- cored), and Miss Wright, of Wigan, received encores .for her songs "Roses" and "Where my caravan has rested." .Mr. Angus gave" The Veteran's Song" and "Queen of Angels," and Mr. W. Harrison (humorist) created much</p>
        <p>• amusement with "How. are you ?" "Taking father's tea," and a monologue. Miss E. Taylor sang "Beloved, it is morn," and "There's only one England." A recital was given by Miss E. Royston, entitled "The Widow Cona- n-11AT." Votes of 'thanks to .the :artistes were. -proposed by Mr. Royston and Mr. E. • Gorton. Mr. F. Blackwell was the accompanist.—The scholars'. tea and social evening .was .held on Thursday, the scholars providing the enter- tainment, after. which dancing • *as indulged in, the accompanist being Mr. 'Wainwright.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>OUGHTRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OUGHTRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Parish Church Sexton.—Mr. Jams T. Mutton, a parishioner. has been -appointed as sexton and verger for the Parish Church. Mr. Mutton is well-known in the district and lives at Oughtrington Bridge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Alcohol.</head>
        <p>Alcohol.</p>
        <p>There is nothing to be said in favour of alcohol.. It is a drug; it is not a food; it is not even a stimulant. It impairs the action of the heart, the liver, the stomach, the kidneys. It congests the blood vessels. ,It lowers the tem- perature. ' It aggravates the effect of shock. It spoils brain control. It retards the rapidity of mental action. It plays havoc with the muscles and with the nerve centres. In the emotions it destroys ideals, it produces callousness to 'moral obligations, and it multiplies lunacy and crime. Lastly, it gives an entirely delusive sensation of increased efficiency. These statements are not arguable. They cannot be challenged. Its results on the conduct of the war• are notorious.--:-Arnold Bennett.</p>
      </div>
