      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>LIBERAL DISSENSION ON COMPULSION.</head>
        <p>LIBERAL DISSENSION ON COMPULSION.</p>
        <p>Knutsford Division Resignation</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR H. ROBERTS'S REJOINDER.</p>
        <p>Mr. John R. Tomlinson, joint hon. secretary of the Knutsford Liberal Association, of which Lord Sheffield is the president, has resigned his connection with the Association on the ground of his deep dissent from what he describes as "the Imperialist foreign policy" of Mr. Asquith, In a letter, to Mr. Haworth, chairman of the Association, Mr. Tomlinson pßints out that at the outbreak of war he criticised strongly the secret agreement in- virtue of which Britain automatically came into the ghastly conflict. Since then, under a Coalition Government and a party truce, we had seen jettisoned- one Liberal tradition after another. Speech was bound, the Press was bound, • the' worker was bound. Criticism was stifled, while• over all hung the black shadow Of the Defence of the Realm Act and secret trial. Into the great and cardinal doctrine of Free Trade a 'wedge had been driven. We went to war for free- dom, and since then there had been no freedom in Great Britain. Now, as a last surrender of Liberal principle, we had a Liberal Prime Minister introducing a compulsory. Military. Service Bill in the House of Commons. The magnificent voluntary response of many mil- lions of men was to count for nought, and at the bidding of a conscriptionist majority in the Coalition Government, who were conscrip- tionists in peace as well as in war, Mr. Asquith was prepared needlessly, wantonly, uselessly to destroy the great tradition and. principle of ..this erstwhile free country, while.. Mr. Lloyd, George was reported to be More than a ready helper. This was the introduction of Prussian- ism here.</p>
        <p>"There are some things," Mr .• Tomlinson writes, "that no nation should descend to even to secure victory. The defiance of this principle led Germany to invade Belgium and to sink the Lusitania. She said 'Victory at any cost.' Are we beginning to say the same? To force an un- willing man to kill or to be killed is a .crime to which none of us should be a party."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS'S REPLY.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS'S REPLY.</p>
        <p>Councillor Henry Roberts, of Warrington, as a vice-chairman of the Association, writing to Mr. H. Haworth, chairman of the Association, says : •</p>
        <p>On personal grounds I very much regret that Mr. Tomlinson has considered it necessary to take this step, as no one could desire tO have a more able or courteous colleague, but I abso- lutely dissociate myself from the reasons which he gives as his justification, as in my judgment he is equally, inaccurate in his facts and in his conclusions, _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Tomlinson erroneously alleges that we "came into this ghastly conflict" owing to certain "secret agreements." The guarantee Of Belgium's integrity to which, along with Germany and other nations we became parties many years ago, long antecedent to the "imperialistic foreign policy of Mr. Asquith's Government" which Mr. Tomlinson so strongly dissents from was cer- tainly in no sense a "secret agreement," but its terms bound us "under pain of national dishonour" to carry out our obligations. As We see matters to-day, the prophetic utter- ance of Lord Roberts to the effect that "when Germany believes her hour has come, Germany ' will strike" was a well-timed Warning. Germany has struck, and for what? She has saused the shedding of oceans of blood, the desolation of millions of homes, the perpetration of crimes, the hideousness of which defy adequate definition, in fact for foul and bestial brutality she has -easily beaten the world's record, and all this has been done to secure to her, world domination in commerce, politics and territory. For many sad months now, we have realised that all Germany's statements as to this being a war of defence into which she was forced by others, have been colossal lies—her real motive is the one to which I have referred, and there- fore it is absolutely vital that we should put into this fight every possible ounce of strength of which we are possessed if we are to prevent the programme of the Huns from materialising.</p>
        <p>And now forsooth because in the considered' judgment of the Government some modified form of compulsion may be 'necessary to secure the "safety margin " . of trained men; 'Mr. Asquith proposes ("under pain of personal dis- honour") to carry out his pledge—a pledge made in sight and with the knowledge of all men, long before the Derby Scheme was fairly "under weigh "—Mr. Tomlinson acpuses the Prime Minister of being "prepared needlessly, wantonly and uselessly to destroy the great traditions and principles of this erstwhile free country."</p>
        <p>A more outrageous charge based- upon a flimsier 'foundation. could hardly by any possi- bility be made. To talk as Mr. Tomlinson does about whether "we can afford a great increase in our Armies" or "carry out our vast engage- ments, &amp;c., &amp;c.," is to assume that our Coalition Government are a set of the most dis- honourable incompetents, who ought not to be allowed to endanger the safety of the Empire for a single moment longer, and yet when one looks around, it is difficult to discover the men who are likely to prove in the alternative, sufficiently capable successors. Candour compels me further to say that Mr. Tomlinson's reference to the proposals for a modified compulsion (which by the way he utterly condemns without the slightest know- ledge as to .their character) as intended "to force an unwilling man to kill or be killed," is a grotesque exhibition of the extent to which a man's unreasoning prejudice will carry him. How. many of the splendid " Volunteers " who ,are now serving at the Front, or of those who have made the greatest sacrifice of; all for Eng- land, have gone willingly "to kill or be killed"? IS it not the fact that these heroes of whom we are so proud and with so many of whom nearly every family amongst us has the closest ties of blood and affection, have realised so intensely-the danger to which we are exposed and have appreciated their personal responsi- bilit- 50 highly, that to secure permonP;tl7 01 that makes our country and its- traditions so precious, they have subordinated every other consideration to their sense of duty? The voluntary system is proved to be monstrously 'unfair in.its.operation, and politically unsound. For inore- thou teo years :low I have been a convert to the .Prin-cipieg of the National Ser- vice League, and I cannot undelitalld .foi the life of me how any reasonable man, it be Will consider the question untrammelled by preju- dice, seeing that we are all'of us living under compulsion in regard to the less important duties of citizenship, should be so wantonly indifferent and even hostile to the application of the same principle for the safeguarding of our liberties,,botlyNational and Imperial, upon the maintenance of which everything on earth that we value depends.</p>
        <p>I do not hesitate to say .that had the princi- ple!if Of the National Service League been in operation even for five years past the war might never have happened at all—(because there was a great deal more in the Kaiser's sneer at our "contemptible little Army" than the brutal in- sult which was intended, and which our soldiers have since avenged in German blood many times over)—or assuming that Wiar was inevitable, we should have been able at a very early date to have put such a large trained lorce into the field, that the period of six months, which Germany's leaders are credited with having prepared for as the probable duration of the war, would not have been greatly exceeded. There are two notorious facts, the considera- tion of which I suggest to Mr. Tomlinson, as having some bearing Oon the tinestion of com- pulsion for the period of the war:</p>
        <p>_ . The first is that very few of the opp - 'lents of "compulsion.' who so loudly proclaim in the most fulsome terms their admiratioji of the Voluntary System' have ever been .Volun- teers themselves, or have at any period. in their lives given either time or effort to qualify them- selves to become competent in the defence of their homeland. The second and even more reprehensible fact is that even after making the most liberal deductions for any and . every reasonable .cause from the total of the "'unat- tested single men," there remains a considerable residue of able-bodied shirkers" whose utter selfishness and absolute lack of patriotism en.. ahles them to enjoy their present comfort • and. security Without a thought or care for those to</p>
        <p>li its</p>
        <p>the mOcinal fumes that escape fro, a Peps tablet dissolving in the rnout and every part of the throat),bronchi, tubes and lungs is directly-bathed wit a germ-killing vapour The result is the(cmlete destru, tion of the mischievtous_inicrolies th; invade the breathingipassageithrbui the mouth and nostiils-altd4tarit cold or throat soreness: The unique-arid distinctive feature 0</p>
        <p>_,. ."</p>
        <p>at</p>
        <p>volatile character. This enables Peps to-'cir,</p>
        <p>whose self-sacrifice they owe an eternal s.lebt of gratitude. . I am testifying of facts which have come under my personal observation as a canvasser and as a district chairman under the Derby Scheme. and this disgusting indifference to our national peril on the part of many physically qualified single men gives one to think furiously of our country's future. For the honest and honour- -able "conscientious objector" I have profound respect, but it is a fact that under this cloak, in the majority of instances, one finds on ex- amination nothing but the most contemptible cowardice and selfishness. .We have got to win this war and to win it so thoroughly that Germany's infamy can never be repeated. To ensure cotnplete success it may be, nay, it is, necessary that no consideration short of dishonour shall be allowed to prevent or even to hinder.the consummation of the nation's de- termined Purpose. For the time being all party strife or dissension should for decency's sake be suppressed. Owing to our utter disbelief of the heinous character of Germany's real intentions we were caught in a state of lamentable unpre- paredness—a small army—the bravest of the brave—well equipped, but our total equipment only made provision for the size of the army then in existence. In 17 months, during the whole of which time the war has been raging. our leaders have had to get the men, train and equip them, regulate our financial position, pro- tect our food supply, and a thousand and one things only slightly less important, and the marvel is, not that errors of judgment have been committed, some of them ghastly in their effect, but that in spite of the Herculean -task %which has been willingly and bravely undertaken and borne, we have come through so far—if one takes a survey over the whole position—with such a measure of success as justifies the confi- dence now generally held in our ultimate and possibly speedy triumph. Finally, I repeat my sincere regret at Mr. Tomlinson's resignation, and I hope that on re- flection he will agree that the reasons which impelled his action are not such as to justify it, and that when we, get back to normal times we shall again have the pleasure of his personal co-operation.</p>
