      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>MEN OF CHARACTER.</head>
        <p>MEN OF CHARACTER.</p>
        <p>The Type Of Men That England Needs.</p>
        <p>The Rev. B. Fell gave a lecture at the Primitive Methodist Church, Legh-street, on Thursday evening, on The Men England Needs."</p>
        <p>_ Mrs. James' Dodds was in the chair, and, in her opening remarks, considered that the three things the world now needed were love, sympathy and comfort. The Rev. B. Fell said that men of courage, principle, heroic spirit and ,faith, men of high ideals, men of intelligence, level-headed men, and men of consecrated courage were the men greatly needed. There was room for them in all callings of life—in Parliament, in the Higher Commands on land and sea, and in the officers and men who stood face to face with the greatest militarism the world had ever known.</p>
        <p>Answering the question "What is 'manli- ness?" the Lecturer said that a fine physique was something to he glad of, but it was not manliness. The quality of a man's coat did not mhke him a man, neither did the length of his purse. Following popular habit was not a true indication of manliness; it was more likely to indicate weakness than strength. The Lecturer also said that sport was not indicative of character, and condemned gamb- ling and drink. The men • the nation needed were men- of character; without character the most distinguished and brilliant career was marred. A man of character was an honest -man and all that he seemed to , be. He was frank, candid-, • and straightforward. He said what he meant and meant what lie said. He was reli- able, and one always felt that he could be trusted. He always answered the call of duty. But it was by union with Jesus Christ, and only in union with Thin, that man 11,994 he complete, .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>FRUITERERS, FLORISTS AND FISHMONGERS AT DINNER.</head>
        <p>FRUITERERS, FLORISTS AND FISHMONGERS AT DINNER.</p>
        <p>Tito ha tlinnêr o ilie lieiVig.famd War, Await. And District Fruiterers', Florist' and VitAiiititigersi Association, was held •at the King'Vgik dii.,Thursday evening. Members and iheir wives and friends, to the number of 63, sat dOirn to dinner, over which the President of the Association, Mr. W. Powell, presided. He was supported by Mr. G. H. Rodgers (secretary of the Association), Mr. McDermid, Mr, G. Miller, Mr. Jas. Bate, Mr. T. S. Steel (solicitor for the Association), Councillor Archer (deputy-chairman' of the Markets Committee) and Mr. E. England. After dinner, various toasts were submitted. The President gave the "King, Queen and Royal Family," and Mr. England gave "The Town and Trade of Warrington," to which Councillor Archer replitd. Mr. T. S. Steel pro- posed the toast of the "Association," to which Mr. J. Moss responded. "The Secretary was also. "toasted," on the proposition of. Mr. Harter (vice-president of the Association). The hope was also expressed that an annual dinner night be held, and that picnic might be arranged for ,the summer months 'with a view to giving the mewher4 an opportunity of meet- in;.eao other.</p>
        <p>_ thiring the evening songs were given by varlons members of the Association and lady friend's, and dancing was ;ifs a interspersed in the prOgritninie.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>TURKEY'S POSITION.</head>
        <p>TURKEY'S POSITION.</p>
        <p>Turkey has made a brave fight for her existence under her German leaders, tut her best regiments have been destroyed, ter re- sources are nearly exhausted, and she can only obtain miiiiitions from .Gcrmany., It is doubt- ful whether she could bring '‘an, effective quarter of a million to the field, The. Bulgarians are excellent oldiersi: -but 550,060 s Ocillt the out- side n niber-ther:cOul4 pUt 'into the -field.-7-Sir Edwin Pears, in tile" 'conteiiiiiik. Review" for Jaduary, •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>"The Common Good Should Be The Common Ideal."</head>
        <p>"The Common Good Should Be The Common Ideal."</p>
        <p>Mr. John Hodge, M.P. (acting chairman of the Labour party) and Mr. W. A. Appleton (secretary of the General Federation of Trade Unions) have given New Year messages to the London News Agency for circulation. Mr. Appleton says :—" The problems confront- ing organised labour are more serious than any which its leaders have hitherto handled. Cherished liberties are threatened, and the -war has compelled the relaxation of trade union regulations and endangered standards of living and comfort. The relaxation of regulations was necessary and inevitable; it has beneficially affected the production of munitions, and it may even improve future industrial relation- ships.</p>
        <p>"Trade unionists; however, must not deceive themselves; there will be no complete reversion to pre-war workshop conditions. • The Govern- ment lute given undertakings in respect of abro- gated or relaxed regulations, and if it remains in power it will, to the best of its ability, honour the promises made. "Labour has, however, to remember that Governments change, and that current circum- stances affect them more than past and em- barrassing undertakings. To mett the after- math of War better industrial orgar.isation and higher productive standards will be necessary. Capital will demand greater mobility of labour, not merely as between district and district, but as between trade and trade. ,</p>
        <p>"Only as the trade union movement concedes the right of every worker to equal opportunity wherever opportunity arises, as it abjures as well as fights exploitation, as it promotes general well-being in opposition to particular aggrandisements, as it unites its forces and brings them into harmony with economic law, will it dominate and succeed.</p>
        <p>"For ' 1916, and always, the common good should be the common ideal.'?</p>
        <p>Mr. Hodge's Wish.</p>
        <p>Mr. lodge's message is as follows :—" My greatest wish for the New Year is a speedy, complete, and triumphant conclusion to the war; that when peace prevails all sections of the community may join together harmoniously in the pursuits of industry; and that there will be the greatest co-operation between capital and labour to repair the ravages caused by the war. Looking forward, as is the custom at this time of the year, we ought at the same time to look back with thankfulness as well as with the most profound gratitude to every officer, man, and boy engaged in the naval ser- vice. We hear too little of their silent vigil amidst. the cold and the stormy waters of tne North Sea. When the history of the war comes to be written do not let us forget what we owe to thorn. In saying this I do not forget the courage and fortitude of all who constitute our great armies, and my message to all of them would be 'We do not forget.' "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>CHURCH ARMY HUTS IN THE THICK OF THE WAR.</head>
        <p>CHURCH ARMY HUTS IN THE THICK OF THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Prebendary Carlile, who has returned safely from his visit to France .to inspect the Church Army Recreation Huts, &amp;c., close to the Front. within reach of shell fire, arrived at one of the huts just after a German shell had shattered a tree close to it and a ,portion of the roof of the canteen beside it. In one of these huts, typical of the rest, he found a piano going at one end, boxing proceeding at the other, ping- pong in the middle; and two English clergymen (one of whom is the son of the Bishop of a South Country see, and the other of whom has since had his leg broken by a shell) serving out cocoa in tin mugs to a crowd of fifty thirsty soldiers.</p>
        <p>"....1141"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>SI,OAN'S LINIMENT</head>
        <p>SI,OAN'S LINIMENT</p>
        <p>velieves the pain of</p>
        <p>Sprains, Bruises, Rheumatism, Chest Pain's, Sore Throat, Neuralgia, Headache</p>
