      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <p>• (Regd. Trade Mark.) COFFEE ESSENCE The Coffee Maid, of Symington's, say "No Coffee like mine, I Only use Symington's." Be sure the water is boiling. THOS. SYMINGTON C., CO., Edniburgh and London.</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>0</p>
        <p>in quite professional style. Those in the east were: The Misses Crawley; Lightfoot, Hardman, :Riley, and Irvine; also Messrs. Dunkley, Morris, Yates Dutton and Gallop. Mr. A. E. Prow and Miss E. Lightfoot were, the. accompanists. Mr. A. Yates Dutton also delivered the annual school report, which was considered highly satis- factory.</p>
        <p>N</p>
        <p>4 1 ••••• - • - S :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>(Eljurril 41il11ii.</head>
        <p>(Eljurril 41il11ii.</p>
        <p>WORKING MEN'S MISSION.</p>
        <p>Good Work Thai Is Bologung Good Results.</p>
        <p>Progress, despite the entrancii into a Eccond year of war, IV itS the word which adequately described in a nutshell the whole of the secre- tary's report of the work of the Working Men's Mission Church Sunday School during the past twelve months, which was presented at the annual gatheting of scholars and parents on Monday evening. After an excellent tea had been heartily enjoyed, the large compan,y present assembled in the schoolroom for the musical and business part or the evening's programme, the Senior Superintendent (Mr. 'l'. Dale) officiating as chairman, and he was accompanied on the plat- form by Mr. Cannon, of Leeds.</p>
        <p>_ in the course of a few rerparhs, Mr. Dale referred to the .work of the. past year, iiid exhorted the teachers and scholars to continue in their efforts to maintain the good name of the school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>Steady Progress.</head>
        <p>Steady Progress.</p>
        <p>Mr. A: Edwards (secretary). in presenting the annual report, said the school had maintained its steady Progress, and although a decrease was noted in the number of scholars, yet that was accounted for in the large percentage of -young men who were on active service. Six scholars had died during the year, two having sacrificed their lives for their country. The number of scholars on the books was 937, and the income showed a- sum of £lO5 Os. ld.. with an expendi- tare of £lOO 13s. 111 d., thus leaving a balance on the right side. On prize Sunday in Febru- ary, 2:!3 books were presented to the scholars for regular attendance; while in connection -With the Sunday School [0 en Scripture ex- amination, 17 scholars -were suucessf ul in secur- ing prizes and certificates. The Secretary re- viewed the leading events of the year, and stated that on Decision Sunday 27 scholars " decided to enlist under the lyanner of their Lord and Saviour -Jesus- Christ." A great interest was being taken in the -missionary work of the school, the seholars lila ia Ily contributing to the sulipo4 iii ii. native missioner at Bidar, in India. The organisations eonneeted with the school came in for special reference ;- good work was being- accOmplisbed in the Christian Endeavour Society, the Band of ilope, the Rechabites Tent, and the Sunday evening lantern service, where over 500 children were receiving - great benefit from the limelight lectures; and the Boys Brigade was steadily 'growing under the • able leadership of good officers. . In connection with the present will tlin report stated that 30 scholars and three teachers were serving under the Colours in various fields. Thanks to Mrs. ichn Crosfield.</p>
        <p>11l conclusion, Mr. Edwatds exprcsed the thanks Of the school officials to Mrs. John ems. field for her noble efforts in assisting the good work which was being accomplished.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>Officials.</head>
        <p>The Officials.</p>
        <p>The officials for 1916 were as follow :—Presi- dent, Mr. T. Dale; vice-president, Mr. G. Dale; superintendents, Messrs. T. -Dale and W. Worm 11; secretary, Mr. A. Edwards; treasurer, Mr. H. Tanner; committee, Mesdames H. Tan- ner and G. Dale, and Mess-rs. A. Tilling, I'. Weave, H. Jone:f.., A. Gleave, A. Walker, G. Head and W. -Stephens. - During the evening Mr. Cannon made a brief speech, and assisted in the programme with a recitation, other items being rendered by Miss A. IlamerlAnd Messrs. H. Foden And A. Morris. General games were afterwards indulged in till a late hour. --</p>
        <p>On Tuesday the. junior seliolars were enter- tained to tea, and a short musical programme was contributed to by the lads and lassies. who afterwards spent a ri:_;ht royal time in various games.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>BRICK-STREET CHURCH.</head>
        <p>BRICK-STREET CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Satisfactory Report For The New Year Party.</p>
        <p>111 ElPite bf the wretched weather _conditions prevailing on the evening of the first day of tlie year. a 1111111Cr011S .gathering assembled in the Brick-street Independent Methodist Mission rooms to partake of a splendid tea and to enjoy an entertainment which for excellence rarely been been outclassed in this particular branch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>War Upsets the Ideal.</head>
        <p>War Upsets the Ideal.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Gallop the worthy and energetic secre- tary. to the • Mission, read the report for last year, which stated that the 12 months had been eventrul in many respects. Although, he said, they could not as yet admit being an ideal church, still it was pleasant to record that both numerically and financially, on the whole, per- fect satisfaction could be felt: The member- ship had increased to 75, as against-72 for the previous year. Unfortunately, the 'Sunday morning and evening services had only been fairly well attended, which was in a way sur- prising considering the great and terrible crisis through which the country was now passing. He hoped that this lack of spirit in church-going would be obliterated in the future. He also regretted having to report the .abandonment of the Junior Christian Endeavour Society owing to lack of workers. In March they decided to open a subscription list with the object of send- ing -cOmforts to the young men serving their King and country. A substantial sum was realised, and in consequence a parcel was for- warded, .to each of them. One of their most energetic; and faithful workers had passed away in the person of the late lamented Mrs. J. Shaw, sen. -llcr death had been a great loss to them as a Church, and her place would be difficult to till.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>Fine Enlertainment.</head>
        <p>A Fine Enlertainment.</p>
        <p>The entertainment which followed was appre- ciated by the audience in Ito half-hearted fashion. The choir rendered selections and part songs in pleasing style. Miss Eva Shaw, in "The Wondrous Story," was admirable. _lp- propriate, patriotic and sentimental songs by Mr. and Mrs. J. &amp;Tilton respet:tively called for unstinted applause, their rendering being .quite in keeping with their reputation. Mr. E. 11. Wright, .elocutionist, was successful in. items of note, especially in passages front "David. Cop- perfield2"llp. humorous part of the programme was in the liand-; of Mr. W. Hopkins, who throughout kept the audience in one continual roar of laughter. The principal attraction of the evening's entertainment was a. humorous sketch entitled " Havi n the'r photographs takken," which created a very. favourable,im- presSion, all the artistes acquitting themselves</p>
        <p>The Matrimonial Circle. Contains hundreds genuine advertisements, appeal- ing all classes ladies and gentlemen (many in good social position) ; post free, 6d., sealed envelope.— Editor, 18, Hogarth-road, Earls Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>"A DIFFERENT SPIRIT."</head>
        <p>"A DIFFERENT SPIRIT."</p>
        <p>St. Peter's Vicar On The Temper Of</p>
        <p>Soldiers.</p>
        <p>In the course of his twenty-third anniversary sermon on Sunday . evening, the Rev. B. vicar of St. Peter's Church, Vl' dealt with our circumstances as a nation, and analysed the teMper of our soldiers. . As all knew, we were at the present moment passing •through a very great crisis, he said. We were just beginning the year of 1916, and in his opinion it would be one of the most event- ful in the history of the British Empire. If we recalled the position of affairs two years ago, he thought it could be truly said that the shin of State was drifting into dangerous , waters, and most people wondered if it would ' -reach port in safety. In Ireland, Ulstermen and Nationalists were drilling and arming for civil strife. The organised .working men of this country were seething with discontent in spite of all that had been done for them, and order could not be commanded. The Church in Wales was. threatened with disestablishment and dis- endowment, and, in England, danger threatened which might easily have ended in rupture. What did we see to-day v—the National Govern- ment. guiding the 'ship of State, • with 'a united People striving to hold it up in every way. .A year ago, continued the preacher, men ,went to • the war in great hilarity,' but now they went with grim determination, IMt.not down- - hearted. • They knew something of the terrible struggle which lay before them. What had the Lord dem, for ns? 'lle had enabled its to keen the enemy's ships front the seas, with little ex- ception. ;111(1 to tarry on our vontmerce far be- yond anything we (I mid have hoped for; and had given us stiffieient food: No doubt Serions and far-reaching mistakes had been made, but we should remember that he who made no mis- takes mode nothing. We had much to do and learn, ond wa Were slow to wake 'tip to the&lt;, facts. But we had reason' to believe that I awakening bad now eome, along with a stern determination to see the war through to a suc- cessful conclusion. •When we thought .of the tremendous amount of organisation, reVelllip, eOl.llll we wonder that mistakes had been made? :No! if we looked at the situation as a whole, we had .every reason to thank God for the position in which we stood. To-day, our soldiers looked upon life in a very different way to how they looked upon it before, for they knew that any moment they might be ,called into the presence vof God. Wp .should prepare for the homecoming of these men, -and instead of so' many picture palaces awaiting them there should be-1111°re churches, for they would most surely want them. The kingdom of God should not be neglected, for if we did not look after His kingdom, Ile would not look after ours. In his .(the vicar's) opinion,- God would finally lead our troops to victory, and so establish an everlasting peace in Europe and men would love and care for each other; nations would regard each 'other as brothers-; finally, God's name would be glorified among men. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>YCLIFFE P.S.A.</head>
        <p>YCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>At Sunday's meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A., 731 r. nat delivered an interesting address; and a welcome was extended to Private Hewitt, who is reCovering.from wounds. On the motion of Mr. lieswick a vote of sympathy was accorded Mr. H: Farrar (the secretary) on the death of his brother, Mr. -Tames Fleming Farrar, who has been deseribed by a Blackpool contoin- porary as "- one of Blackpool's 'oldest and worthiest citizens."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>"THE TABERNACLE."</head>
        <p>"THE TABERNACLE."</p>
        <p>A series of interesting .lectnres on " The Tabernacle " have been given at the Gospel Hall during the week, commencing' on Sunday even- ing, by Mr. 11. Wilson, of Kendal. Good' gregations have been prvsent and have appreci- Ided he leetures. hi tie' opinion of Mr. Wilson the Tabernacle is. the most important figure of Christ and the Church in the Old Testament. His lectures have. been illustrated with diagrams.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>"%X OMEN Vi</head>
        <p>"%X OMEN Vi</p>
        <p>Ciermatt Prisoners Not Except In Gangs.</p>
        <p>At. a recent meeting of the Cheshire War Com- mittee at Crewe, the most important question dealt with was that ,of the employment •of women in .agriculture. Sir SYdney Oliver, the secretary of the Board of- Agriculture and Fisheries, gaid what was wanted first of ,all was. to occasion", farmers, who were a 'little shy of women labour, to draw more upon. womeni workers than they had hitherto done, and they Wanted .to impress women of all. classes.—although ' not. under the -necessity in engaging in Such labour---Twith the idea that the country at-present demanded thei r services, and that with patriotism and public spirit they should go out of the ordinary line to offer theniselvc, to agriculturists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>Ccmc Back Frcm Erin!</head>
        <p>Ccmc Back Frcm Erin!</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Shepherd (Preston Brook) so id that in Cheshire they had been largely • depee- dent. upon Irish labour for farm work,: but the Irishmen had now gone. Ile thought that it they were assured that they would be "starred'' the Irishmen would come back and resume work. Sir Sydney Oliver said that Hie .Board had written to the Irish Department -and asked them whether they could do anything in this direction, but they had not yet replied. Mr. Shepherd said the Irishmen had left farmers in the lurch. The Irish labourers com- prised some of the finest workers in thp -world. H.:farmers were to get in their crops next year they must be brought back. They were not lit for soldiers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>German Prisoners.</head>
        <p>German Prisoners.</p>
        <p>Lord Newton asked the meeting what their view was on the question Of employing the Ceriuml prisoners at Baudforth upon. the land. 11. a .strong case ixiitld be made out for the enqiloyment of these Nen, he thought the authorities might be willing to take it into con- sideration.</p>
        <p>Sir Sydney Oliver said that if suell a proposal was made to the 'Home Office or the War Offiee they ,must be able to put •before them- some form or scheme in which the men might be employN; in great gangs, otherwise lie did not think their employment would be possible. The opinion Was •expressed that the ranks of farm labour would be'further depleted .Presently, when hiring agreements of men and girls came to an end. Several fanners declared that it would, be impossible to _carry on the dairy in- dustry if the milkers left, and they feared fiat the offer of high wages to munition workev would tempt them away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>Dairy Work Menaced.</head>
        <p>Dairy Work Menaced.</p>
        <p>.1 resolution was adopted asking the .Board of Agriculture to use its influence with the Minis- ter of Munitions not to permit the employment in munition work of either expert Male or -female agricultural labour and emphasising the urgent .necessity of preserving to apicul- ture the present supply of women engaged in dairy work and milking, declaring that if these were withdrawn from their present employment, in which they were experts, the ~niost serious situation would he created on the dairy farms in Cheshire. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 8, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 8, 1916.</p>
        <p>44,4c40re *7O 04:4 4•4.42179W.W.V., 4 420* 0 4** @ 0 '4 40,*010 44 4' V • 4 4444'44-** 44 **O..* 4* 4** 44 ee 0.4 4 o*******4*4.24 4.,,4,-44, %:•":. * @ . 4 * + . . • 44 3 • ,:,...,.,.., .1.7, ~. , lik. ~. ..1,.. 4, „ . : . , _ .. ~..• '.. 7., , „...„, -... _. . ,„,.. ~.. . . : . m : . ~.. .. . , . • , , • • -</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Literary and Theatrical Stars at the Front.</p>
        <p>Both in Britain and France, the list of dis- tinguished poets, literary men, playwrights and journalists serving their country is a very long one. Women writers also are nursing, and doing war -work of various kinds. Miss S. Macnaughten ha combined literary accom- plishments with war work, for sincelier return from Belgium she has published her experiences</p>
        <p>in that distressful country. From the begin- ning of the war, Miss Macnaughten was in Belgium, as a nurse and ambulance worker. Miss Eardley-Wilmot, daughter of Admiral Eardley-Wilinot, is a Red Cross nurse. Beatrice 1 I ayiiden joined the Society of Friends, working with the Belgian refugees in Holland, where she has done muck good. -• Actors and actresses have also rendered yeoMan service in all kinds of unfamiliar directions. Many actors are in the trenches, and not a few actresses are at the Front, driving ambulance cars, serving in canteens, and acting as orderlies, cooks, house- maids, in military and other hospital's.</p>
        <p>Frenchwomen and Gifts for Soldiers. As the French military authorities provide no resthouses behind their lines for officers or soldiers, as do the British,. the French people have undertaken the matter, and are doily,-' it very - thoroughly. A huge building in the Champs Elysees, used before the war as a -salle de fetes, has been taken over, and here gifts for the tired men in the resthouses are received, and sent to the.various depots at the Front. At Christmas a gift was -dispatched to every French sOldier in the -resthouses, and many good things to eat found theirovay to the tables. Every- thing for a soldier's use and comfort is sent to the receiving house in Paris, which is .filled to overflowing. every _country of the world con- tributing to the work which is recognised by the r rem+ military authorities as one of the most uk‘ful of the war. Women manage the under- taking almost entirely.</p>
        <p>Uniforms for Women. For many of the occupations in which women are t;iking the 'gave of itten, A tin ifonn is neces- ,ary, and thus a new problem confronts railway,</p>
        <p>tramway and other companies. In every in- stance first attempts at designing a uniform that. that would stand hard wear, rough weather, and not to be too „Masculine, were a failure, and it was not until the help of a firm of well-known ladies' tailors was sought, that the trimmest, neatest aml yet most becoming of suits and over- coats were evolved. Braid is, of course, much in evidence on.the greater number of the uni- forms, not only in black but- in gold and silver. The caps amt hats that accompany the suits have received the attention due to their im- portance, , but the former are gradually- dis- appearing, a neat felt with a round crown and a narrow''brim,-taking their place. In no single instance have objection S been raised to the don- ning of these practical, smart. and serviceable clothes. The effect promises to be far reaching, for it may lead to the adoption by women gener- ally, of a working dress, suited to the occt pa- tion. of each class of workers.</p>
        <p>Fashionable Colours. The kilted plaid skirt is very smart when worn with a flaring threequarter coat of the prevailing colour of the tartan chosen. But such smartness is for the well-filled purse only; for constant wear a plain neutral shade is pre- ferable. For the moment dark green is the favourite colour, perhaps- because it .goes better than navy blue with the dark furs so generally worn this winter.. Reseda is another shade that has suddenly acquired a foremost position. It slats „fair haired girls -admirably', in act green is a becoming colour to the youthful, whether pink or ivory in complexion. Nigger brown ranks next as a fashionable, though more trying colour._ Purple and grey in many shades. mole. and lime, are all used for coats and skirts, and each is effectively trimmed with fur. Garnet, .nattier blue, and beige, are also worn with skunk. Many tailored coats are Russian- in design, and these have Russian fur collars, but a fur stole is 'usually added. This season's coats are belted loosely, the collar is cut, in one with the coat, and the fastening is on one side. Seine- 'times the basques are very -full ana reach the !knee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes And Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>EtiriMont Windows.</p>
        <p>ITo obtain brilliantly clean window glass, let ill be cleaned with a liquid paste made with alcohol and whiting. This mixture removes leeks and imparts a high Ip.stre to the glass. Flair Tonics. •</p>
        <p>To prevent the hair from falling out give the hkad a thorough washing with good white soap; mix together one pint of bay rum, 611'graiins of quinine and half an ounce of cantharides; shako wk.•ll and let this preparation 'stand for two or three days. Rub it into the sealp'with the tips o.s the fingers. This will be lomat. an excellent h4ir tonic.</p>
        <p>The following is a good hair tonic :—One ounce o 4 sage and steep it in boiling: water for 10 minutes; strain and add 2oz, glycerine, loz. powdered borax, loz. Inc sulphur, ioz.• tincture of cantharides, bergamot sufficient to perfume. APply twice- a week with hand and rub thoroughly in. It will 'remove dandruff and s6engthen the growth, It will also, it is. said, prevent grey hairs. Coal-saving.</p>
        <p>Professor Sonnensehein writes • from tha University, Birmingham, to the " Times ": lisitate to write a letter about it..subject on illicit, I am far from being an expert, but when one thinks that one knows something that may ha of advantage to one's fellew-men at it time like this, what is one to do? For the last eight mbnths I• have been ex iwrinienting .in a small why with methods of saving coal, and I have fdlind that the use of salt has led to a consider- itt`?le reduction in Jay coal. bill. My suggestion isithat others should try the same method, and it possible improve upOn it. What 1 have done very simple. I dissolve a large tablespoonful otl salt in somewhat less than a pint of water, aid sprinkle this fluid from a watering can over a'Jcwt. of coal spread out on the Boor of the ca11,,,(410e, ,,,(410e, taking care that each piece of coal gets sique part of. the fluid, .11 have used small and ocap coal, and thus effected a double saving off moneY. The slack burns up brightly in the le, and thve is hardly any smoke. Herein ly possibly lie the 'explanation of. flie whole phenomenon. - I am assured that the state of the thies also show an absence of smoke in the fire, , and that hardly any ashes or cinders remain in oti under the grate. • Steamed Bread-Ad-butter Pudding.</p>
        <p>utter a small pudding basin that will hold sufficient for two people. Cut some thin bread old butter, or use any left over- from-afternoon teh decorate the bottom of the basin - with caOlied peel cut in stars, or a few raisins or</p>
        <p>preserved cherries; put .in the. slices of bread and butter, dusting each slice with castor sugar and sprinkling with a few chopped pre- served cherries. When full, beat up an egg with half a pint of milk, flavour with essence of almonds, and strain over the pudding. Leave to soak for a while, rover with buttered .paper, and -steam for an bout' and a quarter. Turn out, and serve with custard or wine sauce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>.40.++++++•+•4•4.40'40.4••40, • Children' 8 Corner.: • •</head>
        <p>.40.++++++•+•4•4.40'40.4••40, • Children' 8 Corner.: • •</p>
        <p>* Conducted by UNCLE JACK, • 4) ••4.0• 4. • • 44.• 4. 4. • +•</p>
        <p>Children, Join This Corner! Enter The Competitions! Write Your Best! Always Be Glad! Uncle Jack, " Examiner," Warrington-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>RETURNING TO SCHOOL..</head>
        <p>RETURNING TO SCHOOL..</p>
        <p>My DEAR CIIILDREN,T The holidays are now over. I hope you have all had an. enjoyable rest and are quite ready for your school duties on Monday morn- ing. A new year and a fresh start at school! What a, good opportunity for a first-class essay coinpetition. That competition I now propose. Will you write and 'tell me what you think abbut going back to school and.. the work you intend to flO now the Christmas playtime is at an end? 1 will start you with the title Going Back to School.</p>
        <p>That ought to bring me sonitt interesting let.'ters for publication next week; and the one who wins the prize will have reason to be proud of having done so.</p>
        <p>Yvur.loving, . UNCLE JACK</p>
        <p>A PLEASING BOOK.</p>
        <p>Niece Annie .3ila-,wy; :Rope Field Cottages, Sandy-lane, Lymm, acknowledging: the receipt of- a prize she recently won in one orour com- petitions, thanks me for the book and adds:— I have read it ever three times. I: like read- ing books connected with a high school."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>MORE FUN.</head>
        <p>MORE FUN.</p>
        <p>little boy.was sitting on one of the benches in a park; watching people ride on the donkeys. Finally, an. exceedingly • stout lady appeared, hired a donkey, and. was just about to mount when she saw the small boy, and said: ",Little boy. don't you want me to hire a donkey for you, too?" "No, thank -von," said the boy; "Id rather sit here and laugh." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>CURIOUS NESTING-PLACES.</head>
        <p>CURIOUS NESTING-PLACES.</p>
        <p>Every year some fresh discovery is made respecting the curious habits of British birds. The boldness shown by even our shy birds when domestic duties are concerned is, the `-f Agri-</p>
        <p>cultural Economist " points out, surprising. Often you can approach a bird sitting on the nest and take her off, where at another time you would hardly get a glimpse of her. Robins are especially noted for their boldness, and a pair. have been known to build and rear their family on a beam in a school, entering by the window, .which was left open. Swallows seldom build in odd places, but a pair have been known to build their nest against a pane of window glass. A swallOw's nest in a funnel is an ex- ceptionally queer place for a bird of this species to choose.</p>
        <p>Wrens are very ingenious, am] their dome- shaped nests, which are so beautifully woven,. are often 10111)11 in old kettles and tins and even in the pockets of old coats winch, have been left hanging about. A hedge-sparrow's nest in sa kettle was found in a bunch of nettles close to a public footpath. where schoolchildren passed every day, but the faithful bird, with no thought of danger, reared a family quite -un- molested. It may seem strange totalk of water- fowl building in trees and high bushes, butt such is often the case. A moorhen's nest was found ilp 'a • tree in an 'Old pail, which was lodged in the branches twelve feet „from the water. Blackbirds and thrushes have been known to bnilil where. for their shy nature, it seemed almost impossible for them to 'settle. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>Evening Frocks.</head>
        <p>Evening Frocks.</p>
        <p>Satin has for many .generations 'held -a fore- most position Os 0. suitable fabric for evening frocks. Like the blouse ii 'has been many times doomed to be put aside. but every season it is found in its accustomed place. Nothing is more serviceable than soft satin of a good make, and II white it is ideal evening wear for the young girl.. Not only so, but it has the advantage of</p>
        <p>being 'really economical. • This season there are satins with cashmere backs, that, although dearer than the plain makes, are quite uncrush- able, and • perfectly suitable to the ,Present fashions. Many- evening frocks are cut with straight lines from the_top of the low bodice to the knee, and some skirts are composed of alternate panels of velvet and a soft tulle. An Incroyable jacket, double-breasted, and fasten- ing with large buttons, forms the bodice, and tulle in the form of a kerchief, fills up the opening. - Tight-fitting 'evening corsages are a necessary. accompaniment to full skirl-. fore the Waist-line is now preserved, vh h il,t bust is clearly defined and cleverly outlined. Some of the smartest evening gowns have square trains. The New Millinery. •</p>
        <p>The picturesque note is especially seen in the millinery for the New Year. For smart wear there are Cavaliers and Gainsboroughs in velvet and plush, as well as in panne and silk beaver. Very lovely long ostrich feathers fittingly adorn these beautiful models. The feathers are often 511: led from deep or bright, to pale faint toirs. TI ore is little change in small hats, which are still high and yarrow in the brim, or brimless. There are some models- in grey and black that 'resemble a man's tall hat and are very mascu- line, but •these will not attract. the well-dressed woman. Feather mounis have been displaced by buckliss-;. ind small :,Icel-winged ornaments. Sdibir hats with wide brims are decorated with wool embroidery, together with a very little tinsel to brighten it. There are several new and very charming motor hats and caps. One in black panne with a slightly' full CrOWII, was close-fitting to the head; the_only trimming con- sisted of 4 narrow band of grey fur, which was placed rOund the top of the crown. British •Eau de Cologne. Eau de Cologne, although made in Germany, in the city of evil smells, is of Italian origin.. It was the invention of an Italian namell Fenienis, but the business in Cologne was established by another Italian—Giovanni Maria Farina—who acquired world-wide fame and a huge fortune. It. is" only in recent years that British firms have manufactfired this perfume with any con- siderable success, although Jersey has long been noted for its Eau de Cologne. No patriotic Brit ish woman will ever permit. the Gonna 4- made article to reappear on her toilet table, anif ihere is no reason why. she should do so, sin •:,e British brands are quite as good. The perfume I is. antiseptic as well 11:: refreshing. and it is used to a considerable extent in the wards ~)f our military hospitals. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>Smile at every chance you get because there are so few of them.</p>
        <p>You ean invariably count upon a woman to be devoted to n hasband who doesn't deserve it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>EXERCISE NOT FOR WOMEN.</head>
        <p>EXERCISE NOT FOR WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Learned Doctor Says It Tends Neither Toward Strength Nor Grace.</p>
        <p>A famous physician has of late been calmly pointing out to women the , foolishness of physical exercise as a- means of resting the mind. lie :says emphatically that the old theory is all -nonsense—that the Mind cannot rest when the body .is tired. He says that the practice of taking a- long walk at a fast trot when one has done a hard day's work is entirely the wrong thing to do. in his words, "It adds one fatigue to another."</p>
        <p>Another 17elief of his will cause more excite- ment-and discussion than any other. "Women," he 'says, " who do not indulge in physical exer- eise live longer than men." lie does not think women -need exercise. This is a daring .state- ment to make in the middle of a generation that is. absolutely given. over. -to the exercise fad. lie is not alone in this belief, however, for a number of women who have gone to rack and ruin through 'physical exercise agree with his theory.</p>
        <p>The doetor. says that plenty of fresh air day and night and one hour's walk is enough .for any woman. her constitution does not need any more than is given by .a three-mile spin. lf she in the city she cannot .go at such a rate of speed, but .on springy country roads. she ca al'lEll out from two or three miles a day and find herself in lit ccindition.</p>
        <p>Regarding I lie ,qibject of Au, annual vacattion for women the doctor says: " Nine out of ten women 'feel worse after a vacation than- when they 'started, and are unable to resume work properly. A woman does not need a long rest, butt greater number of short ones. It is better to rest four separate half hours a day thaii. to go like mad through the week and rest: 16 hour,; on Sunday. It is better to'diyide'the .12 months of work into half holidays xather than take two months after 10 months of exhauStion,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>EMPEROR'S GIRD NEST.</head>
        <p>THE EMPEROR'S GIRD NEST.</p>
        <p>• it was a dreary day. .Tltie rain had been fall- ing and the ground was deep with mud. The Emperor of Spain, 'Charles, and his soldiers, were in 'camp. The field looked like a little city with tents for houses.' The Emperor and his men had been away from home for: a long time. :How Aired and lioineick they were! All the world seemed dark. They missed the blue sky and bright flowers and green -hills of sunny Spain. -But most of all they missed ,their little laughing boys and girls. . _</p>
        <p>Hack and forth, back and forth, in 'front of the tents, walked the soldiers in their great boots of Spanish leather. • All at once they 'saw something that made them start. What do you think it was? There, on the lop of the Emperor's tent, was a- wee swallow sitting upon her nest. Wasn't that. the queerest place in, the world to 'build a. nest? Such a carefully made little nest as that was! Mrs. Swallow had worked hard to build her nest. She had made ,it of clay and hair and feathers. Hour after hour he had worked, finding a bit of hair here 'or a soft feather there, and had woven them- into a nest..</p>
        <p>If she could have spoken to you, she would have 'told you what a good place the hedge had hel'n to find hairs. They had blown and caught tare from the horses' manes and tails during the battle..</p>
        <p>" Look !" said one of the Boldiers in his gruff</p>
        <p>voice. " The swallow thinks our Euipeior's tent is a shed!" The Emperor, hearing the name, came out of his tent to see what the trouble was. " Let no one touch her," he said as he saw the little bird: " She is my guest." And he smiled. The soldiers smiled, too, -and the day seethed to have grown brighter.</p>
        <p>Not a bit afraid was Mrs. Swallow as she sat on her nest, for she knew that no one would harm her. Had not the great Emperor himself called her his guest? -Until- the battle was ended she 'stayed; a ild even longer, too, for, as the tents were being taken up for the army to move on, the Emperor said "I-Awe mine standing." " So it stood there all alone,</p>
        <p>Loosely flapping, tattered- and torn," until the little birds were ready to fly far, away from home.--Eleanor M. Jollie.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information In Popular Language.</p>
        <p>Natural Vitality and Pneumonia Germs. WO read much these days about the import- ance ,f conserving our natural resources, pre- serving our ferests, and .om-cloning better' varieties of fruits and vegetables; but it is a thousand times more important to learn how.to- preserve and conserve our natural vitality 'so- that we may successfully resist the invasion of disease germs. Some people inherit such An excess of -natural vitality that they can daily sqUander much of it in riotous living and yet have enough left to keep the pneumonia germs at arm's length; while others who are .not so fortunate, even with reasonable care, spend legitimately each day abo)ut all the vitality they have to spare. In other words, having no deposit in the bank, they are simply living from hand to mouth, so as soon as they are ,subjected to some 'unusual exposure, such as .loss of sleep, they are always in danger of taking pnQumonia, for most of our vitality is generated jwhile asleep. Others invite pneu- monia by sonic outrageous 'dietetic indiscretion which produces autointoxication, which in turn cripples the nettle!' forces of the body, so the lbodily defences go into bankruptcy, and the pneumonia germs seize the opportunity to break loose like an uncontrollable mob.—David Paul- son, in January," Good Health."</p>
        <p>Self-Poisoning and Disease. More people are 'poisoned from within than from- without. The large intestine is a paradise for germs and under certain eonditions decom- position takes place very readily, and poi. it substanee are produced that overwhelm lll- entire system just as 'surely as though alcb,l had been-taken.</p>
        <p>'rite luscious fruits, the nutritious grains, the wholesome vegetables ~with dairy products, do not furnish a fruitful field for the germs that naturally inhabit the alimentary canal, while flesh foods are their best culture media, as every laboratory worker knows. When he wants to cultivate germs he plants them in beef tea, not in fruit juice. Is it not foolish to saturate the alimenixy canal • with putreseible material that readily transformed into virulent p, When stagnation of the alimentary canal -, and that is the most common ailment arooi,; civilised- people. thou the conditions are for autointoxication, and this condition only favours pneumonia but also nervous pr -- tration, neuritis. siek headache, depression, and a long „train :01 other evils that arc often sup_ posed to be, due to at "'dispensation Of Provi- dence," when in reality it is largely caused by wretched, unwholesome, and ,thoughtless eating and drinking.—" Good Health."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>PLENTY OF CHANCES.</head>
        <p>PLENTY OF CHANCES.</p>
        <p>• I Was visiting a California turkey -farm lasi month when a boy applied for it job. " Your ielerences are good. I'll try you," said the farmer.</p>
        <p>" Is there any chance to rise, bir?" the is asked.</p>
        <p>"Xes," said the fanner. - A grand chance You'll rise at four o'clock every morning."— " St. Louis Globe-Democrat."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>OBLIGING.</head>
        <p>OBLIGING.</p>
        <p>" Hang it, sir! I've just been stung by one of your bees! I:demand reparation!" " Cer- tainly, sir. You *just show me which bee it was, .and punish the horrid thing severely!" •</p>
        <p>There's mighty little Use trying to have r g,p,(l time being good.</p>
        <p>What We Can Learn From thc Bccs.</p>
        <p>" What can we learn from the bees?" asked a teacher at the end of a Nature lesson, expecting that the industry and thrift of the insects would</p>
        <p>have made a deep impression. The answer came in tones of deep conviction: "We learn to leave 'em jolly well alohe 1"</p>
        <p>A Catastrophe. '</p>
        <p>" Lor Mrs. Green, you aint lookiitg yoursell at all this morning. Whateveer do be the mat ter with you?"</p>
        <p>" There, Mrs. -Budd, you know the trouble we've allus 'ad with our George an' now ah've a postcard this morning sayin' as 'ow he's got t V.C. an' me and Is father teetotalers all Our lives Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
