      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>FATAL SHOCK,</head>
        <p>FATAL SHOCK,</p>
        <p>Accident To Husband Causes Death Of Wife.</p>
        <p>DAMAGE AT A SANKEY</p>
        <p>FARM.</p>
        <p>By its isolation, Grange Farm, Sankey, was exposed on all sides to Saturday's gale, and suffered much damage, besides being the scene of a tragic incident. • Staying at the farm‘with Mr. and Mrs. Ernest Bennett, were Mr. and Mrs. J. C. Carruthers, of Ilindsford, Manchester. A victim of bron- chitk aggravated by the bad weather, Mrs. Carruthers was confined to bed. From her room she could observe tho spacious farmyard, and at half-Past three in the afternoon had the horror of seeing an extensive portion of the cornshed roof raised from its place by the gale, and, flying aeross the yard, strike her husband down: A few minutes later she saw him picked out of the debris • for dead. In her weak condition the shock of the acci- dent proved considerable, 'and her death On Tuesday may be partly ascribed to it.</p>
        <p>Happily, . however, Mr. Carruthers had not been killed, although ho was rendered uncon- scious. For some time he showed no sign of life when he was carried into the farm'house. Fur-</p>
        <p>ther examination, however, disclosed that he still lived, and all haste was made to convey him to the Warrington Inifrmary, where he still lies.</p>
        <p>'a critical Condition</p>
        <p>The ages of Mr. and Mrs. Carruthers are re- spectively 60 and 57 years. . Although residing near Manchester—he has been head -master of llindsford School for over 30 years—Mr. .Car- ruthers was born in Wilderspool-road. Mrs. . _</p>
        <p>Carruthers was a daughtu of the late Mr. Thomas Houghton, baker, of Wash-lane, Latch-</p>
        <p>FARMYARD HAVOC.</p>
        <p>The accident occurred at the -height of the gale. At the time the roof was blown away several people were in the yard, and not one. escaped without being struck by scattering slates. - Mr. Carruthers was walking by him- self in the lee of a building on the other side of the yard, when .the •roof -travelled, swiftly across like an aeroplane, and, stopped by the other buitding, crashed down On to him, at the same time breaking in two a • five .feet square stone, four inches thick. Simultaneously' the bales of straw, 'weighing, in some cases. a him- dredWeight, were thrown about like footballs, and several- large sheaves or corn flew for at least a hundred yards. . The same gust of wind tore off a large portion of the corrugate sheeting from the hay and straw barn, and this, too, was elevated into the air, eventually making a descent on farm imple, meats, and causing j:3O worth of damage in that</p>
        <p>- - way alone. It was a terrific gust of wind.. All Sorts of articles lying about the farm were set on the run, incidentally a couple of wheel- barrows being thrust through a hedge. . As soon as Mr. Carruthers was strusk, efforts were made to extricate him from under the roof, despite the -peril of approach owing to falling slates and bricks. In effecting a rescue most of the men were hit, and Mr. Bennett himself</p>
        <p>[early collapsed from .a blow by a slate in the</p>
        <p>• back: The damage to the shed and barn is estimated at 'over 4200. For some time the shed had been considered a little shaky, and it is an interesting fact that only last week it was decided to strengthen it before the windy weather came. The gale, however, came too soon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>SPLASH OF KHAKL</head>
        <p>A SPLASH OF KHAKL</p>
        <p>Weddinll Of Warrinuton Lad.)</p>
        <p>Teacher.</p>
        <p>There was a splash of khaki kit the wedding of Miss Hilda Johnson, an Evelyn-street Coun- cil School teacher, at the Parish Church on Saturday, both. the -best -man and groomsman being in khaki.. Miss Johnson is the daughter of Mr. and Mrs. James Win. Johnson,. of 291. Manchester-road, and she was married to Air. Donald John Dale, son of Mr. and. Nirs. Stephen Dale, who reside at Elphin Bank, Manchester-road. Rector (the -Bev: Canon Willis) officiated. The bride, whb was given away by her father,</p>
        <p>looked charming in a dress of ivory satin with silk ninon: and skunk; and an embroidered veil with orange blossom. She carried a,. sheaf of lilies, and wore a gold pendant,. the gift of the bridegroom. The bridesmaids. Misses Lajas and May John- son, sisters of the bride, were attired in dresses of white embroidered voile; white• Satin sashes. and shell piiik hats. They carried bomiuets of pale pink carnations. and wore amethyst' lireoches, the gifts of the bridegroom: Private Findlay Dale, of the Liverpool Scottish, brother of the bridegroom, was best man, and Corporal Arthur Johnson, of the Royal Flying Corps, and brother of the bride, was groomsman. The reception was held at the home of the</p>
        <p>_ bride's parents, and as the bridegroom is en- gaged on Government work the honeymoon .was cancelled. Later the happy couple left for their new honi6 at Knutsford,lhe bride travelling in a navy blue costume, black 'velvet hat, and fox' furs.</p>
        <p>'Many useful and valuable presents were re- ceived, including.. a ease of silver pie-seTvers from the staff, and Silver tea knives from the scholars, of the Evelyn-street Council School, Where the bride was mitil recently engaged on the teaching staff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>YEAR'S 'INVENTIONS.</head>
        <p>YEAR'S 'INVENTIONS.</p>
        <p>A FaMin('b Off Except For War</p>
        <p>Purposes.</p>
        <p>According to a report made by Messrs.lughes and Young; 'patent agents, there was a distinct falling Off in the number of patents applied tor in the -United kingdom during the year now closing. In normal times the number of -appli- cations 'approaches 30,000, but the number of 1915 +as fallen to a little over 18,000. Appli- cations from women have 'also materially de- clined, the number. being 280, Vaich is 70 less than during 1914. • The ingenuity of inventors has been devoted largely to the Produetion of armament devices, and among these may be mentioned contrivances for arresting torpedoes, submarine locators, and , means of enabling one submarine' to communi- cate - with another under 'water. A number of patents- for inventions designed to afford protection against Zeppelin -raids have also been applied for. With the 'object of protecting infantrymen during offensive opera- tions a body shield on ;wheels has been devised. This shield is so -constructed .that bullets strike the surface at an angle and glance ,off without causing injury. The rear is open, while the in- terlor is large 'enough 'to itccionithodate !one soldier, who rests on his hands and knees. The device is tiropelled .by pushing 'against the grouncrwith one foot. A slit in the front forms a look-out and also a loop-hole through which to fire.</p>
        <p>, A large proportion of the application relate to chemical processes, utilisation of waste pro- ducts, internal combustion engines, automatic machinery, and labour-saving tools. One of the most interesting toys for boys is a warship that buckles ami&amp;hips and slowly sinks to the bottom of the 'bath. tub When its •Yital spot is struck by a swift toy torpedo.</p>
        <p>• A striking fact is the comiderable number of applications for British patents that have been filed by Germans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Is Warrington Infecting Country Places ?</p>
        <p>. A meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council was held on Wednesday afternoon, Mr. W. Furness being in the chair. Other members present. were Messrs. P. Longten (vice-chair- man), Geo. Mather, Jas. Robinson, J. Back- house, the Rev. A. M. Mitchell. The officials present were Messrs. J. C. Sutton (clerk), Dr. Murray (medical officer), B. S. Newcombe (sur- vieyOr) and F. Kerfoot (inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>REGISTRATION EXPENSES.</head>
        <p>REGISTRATION EXPENSES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk said that he had had a consider- able amount of correspondence with the Local Government Board as to the expenses incurred lw the Council—g 49 7s. sd.—on the 'National Registration Act. He was pleased to say that as he had sent a certificate to the Local Govern- ment Board, they had agreed to pa Y the money.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>CLOCKS ON LAMPS.</head>
        <p>CLOCKS ON LAMPS.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Warrington Gas Manager re the lighting of Sankey, Penketh, Woolston, Winwick and Orford. The letter stated that "As more than half the lamp- lighters had enrolled themselves for military service', the Gas Committee have decidpd to fix clocks for the lighting and extinguishing of the lamps. They are purchasing two types" of con- trollers, one to light in the evening and ex- tinguish at midnight, and the other type, with an additional mechanism, to light the lamps early in the evening and extinguish at daybreak. Should, the Rural Council think it desirable to increase the number of lamps lighted, thd com- mittee will take the matter up with the police." The matter was left for the parish Councils to consider.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>ACKNOWLEDGMENT.</head>
        <p>ACKNOWLEDGMENT.</p>
        <p>The Clerk announced that, in pursuance of instructions, he had communicated to Mrs. Brooks the vote of condolence passed iy the GNAincil. In reply he had received the following letter :—" Dear Mr. SUtton,—Will-you on behalf ot myself and family express to the members of the Warrington Rural District Council our deep s4nse of gratitude for the sympathy extended to -6 in our deep sorrow? It is indeed a comfort to us to feel that Mr. Brooks was held in such high esteem by the members."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>GLAZEBROOK WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>GLAZEBROOK WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>letter was read from the Warrington Water Engineer re the Glazebrook water supply. The letter stated that the matter had been con- sidered by the Water Committee, and they were prepared to recommend the supply of the town- ship, provided the Rural Council •would enter i4to an agreement guaranteeing a revenue equal t 4 ten per Cent. per annum for 20 years on the cOst of providin,, and laying the mains; the town ship to .be divrded into areas and a separate agreement for each. The estimated cost of lay- i4g the mains Was £5,550. The letter added that thistestimate was subject to revision. !The letter will be handed over to the Glaze- book authority.</p>
        <p>The question of dealing with the sewage of the Lord Derby War Hospital at Winwiek was again considered, and it was decided that ardePu- tation should meet the Asylum Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>BLAMED WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BLAMED WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Dr. Murray (the medical officer) announced that during the pa A month there had been nineteen births and nineteen deaths in the 'dis- trict The death-rate was 12.5. During the month sixteen cases of scarlet fever, three of enteric fever, and one of erysipelas had been notified: The Rec. A. M. Mitchell asked if thero were 43, particular cause for the outbreak of sciarlet fever?</p>
        <p>Dr. Murray: I think it is simply-Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>MAGISTRATE FO 58 YEARS</head>
        <p>MAGISTRATE FO 58 YEARS</p>
        <p>Sympathetic References To The Late Captain Ilifithington.</p>
        <p>Pf.fercui es to, the death of 'Captain T. E. INvithitqjon were made at the County Police Coayt on Wednesday and also at the meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council oil tha sn me date.</p>
        <p>At the County Police Court., Mr. F. A. Frost, who was in the chair, said it was to be regretted that since the Court last met they had lest Captain T. E. kWithington, vilito was one of the. olaest magistrates in Warrington. lie had &amp;Awed 58 years, wtlich was a record: They- could not expect to have him with them always, and he certainly had done his share in the administration ,of justice—tempered with mercy—for a very long .number of .years. He was sure that the whole Court would agree with. him in expressing his sympathy ' with the• bereaved family. superintendent Foster and Mr. H. J. Longton. (on behalf of the solicitors) :associated them- selves With the Chairman's remarks. • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>NOT ONE ENEMY IN THE COUNTY:</head>
        <p>NOT ONE ENEMY IN THE COUNTY:</p>
        <p>At • the Pura]: District Council, Mr. Geo. Mither said that Captain Withington was con- neeted with that body when it was the old Highway Board, and also with the Sanitary Authority, of which- he (Cap!Ain Withington) was chairman many years. It was so seldom in the history of any,Local Board that they lost a man of the type of the late Captain Withing- ton. lie was beyond rvproach .and had been a iigitre head in the county for a long number of years.. lie had not an enemy in the whole of lie county, and was one of the most munificent :j\ ers. lie Moved that a letter of sympathy be at to the Captain's relatives. \tr. P. Longton (the vice-chairman) said that was connected with Captain Withington on the old Highway- Board, which was now a part of the Council, and•he agreed with all that Mr. Mather had said.. .</p>
        <p>Mr. B. S. Newcombe (surveyor) and Mr. J. C. Sutton (the clerk) also paid tributes to the late Captain Withington; and the resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>LABOUR DILUTION.</head>
        <p>LABOUR DILUTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. A souffles Offer To The Engineers Accepted: • The Press Bureau has issued an official report of the deputation from the Amalgamated Society of Engineers to Mr.' Asquith and Mr. Lloyd George on Friday. Mr. Asquith pointed out. that Without dilution of skilled by unskilled labour, on a large scale , and whole-heartedly applied, the Amity could not get the required output of munitions, and, further, for the sake of financial stability it was important that the production - should be domestic and not foreign. In spite of the agree- ment of last March dilution had been most sparingly adopted on account of a- natural sus- -picion among the men that. it would be impos- sible to re-establish Old conditions . and trade union safeguards .after the war. Accordingly ho wini prepared to- ask the Minister of Munitions to tc.cept ,an amendment embodying the pro- visions of " Circular L 3 " 'regarding pay and labour conditions, as requested by the deputa-- lion, provided - lie hitd.• an assurance that this concession wnuld " settle the whole matter " and put an end to the delays which ' hindered the carrying out of the March agreement. The deputation afferwards decided to agree 1 the scheme of dilution subject to the con- cession offered by the Prime Minister. -</p>
      </div>
