      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Intercession Day. This naturally turned one's thoughts to the sublect of prayer in general, and I found mental exercise in recalling as much as possible of what I had read respecting it, exclusive of the Bible. There was Nelson's supplication for victory on the eve of Trafalgar, wherein he prayed "May no misconduct in anyone tarnish it, and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet." I well remember somewhere reading years ago how Lord Ashley, before he charged at the battle of Edge Hill, prayed, "0 Lord, Thou knowest how busy I must be this day; if I for- get Thee, do Thou not. forget me." These by the way. Mrs. Barbauld, of whose local associations Warrington readers do not need to be reminded, has some characteristic lines in which the following occur:</p>
        <p>"us ears are open to the softest cry, His grace descends to teeet the lifted eye; lie reads the language of a silent tear, And sighs are incense from a heart sincere."</p>
        <p>Then there is Tennyson's more familiar con- tribution, beginning "More things are wrought by prayer than this world dreams of," in which the poet forcefully asks what are men better than the lower animals, "If, knowing God, they lift not hands of prayer, Both for themselves and those who call them friend." No Answer—What Then?</p>
        <p>I knew a young fellow who was trained for a commercial career that promised to turn out exceedingly well, but on the outbreak of war in obtained a commission, and we have just had news of his death at the hands of a sniper. He was an only son, and the sorely-stricken mother said in my hearing that she had prayed so, earnestly and in faith that she had had no fear that he would be killed. There are many who have been brought to a similar condition of bewilderment and uncertainty. What can one say in the presence of such grief? Shakes- peare, who never fails, even though Carlyle may, goes some way towards meeting the point when he puts this into the mouth of one of his characters:—</p>
        <p>"We, ignorant of ourselves, Beg often our own harms, which the wise powers</p>
        <p>Deny us for our good; so find we profit</p>
        <p>By losing of our prayers."</p>
        <p>Robertson of Brighton used to say that is not true prayer "which does not succeed in moderating our wish, in changing the pas- sionate desire into still submission, the anxious, tumultuous expectation into silent surrender." "Pray," says he in one sermon, "till prayer makes you forget your own wish, and leave it or merge it in God's will." Perhaps I owe the impatient reader an apology for all this; but if any object that I am a journalist and not a parson, I am entitled to the rejoinder that after all I do but comment on a current topic as is my wont.</p>
        <p>"The Peace Egg." Under this title Yorkshire lads, also "in the most gorgeous and ludicrous dresses," perform at Eastertide a similar old play to the one described by Mr. Arthur M-onnfield in last week's article on "Year-end Lore." I have so often heard it repeated in the streets that I have a good deal of it off by heart. While. it answers in many respects to the one outlined in last Saturday's "Examiner," it differs consider- ably as to the text. It is remarkable that, only a day or two before I saw Mr. Mounfield's in. teresting sketch, I happen to have compared notes on this very subject with a casual acquaintance at the hairdresser's who, as a boy in Yorkshire, took part in he play in question. Between us we wele able to reproduce pretty nearly the whole of the quaint little old-time drama.</p>
        <p>" Boys Will Be Boys." Commenting on the cause of the death o'f the unfortunate lad Who fell through_ the trap-door at Messrs. Rylands Bros.' Works, the Coroner said that probably he would have slid down the hoist-chain in like manner when he was a youngster. No doubt many youths do that sort of thing habitually in like circumstances. It is precisely what I (lid at that age. I served my apprenticeship in the composing room of a newspaper office. This was in the third storey, and communicating with the machine room in the basement was a hoist. When this was not in use the cage was kept at the bottom, and whenever I had an errand into the machine room I took the short cut as a matter of course, invariably sliding down the rope. Even that hoist had its fatality and consequent inquest. One day a poor tramping printer wandered in. and on leaving mistook the hoist for the stair- case, fell from the third storey, and was killed.</p>
        <p>It, . ~:I;`7 JS 7 t'if'; ,C'l</p>
        <p>k-'1:4.. ..,,-4•... '1;- -',.7.. 5.. i-</p>
        <p>, ,</p>
        <p>;t:!.</p>
        <p>!-;: •</p>
        <p>t;!`</p>
        <p>k.</p>
        <p>Humour in the Trenches. As I read of the concert at the front on the fourth page of the last issue of this paper, I was reminded of a story told two or three weeks ago by a young relative of. my own on seryice with the R.F.A. in France. He stated that an infantryman on his way from the trenches for a rest halted a moment and in all seriousness narrated this incident :--" Out yonder we and the Germans were so close, that we could talk from trench to trench. To-day, just before we came away--before darkness came on, that is— one of the Huns called out, 'Are there any from London there?' One of our fellows replied 'Yes, two.' Then he asked if there were in the other trench any from Berlin, and on getting the answer that there were nine, he exclaimed 'Well, share that among you,' at the same time throwing a bomb, which exploded in their midst."</p>
        <p>Unsatisfactory Private Roads. I see that the Lymin District Council are taking steps to deal with some of these within their area. I trust that among those they may have to take action against they may not catch a tartar. That was what happened at one place where I used to live. The leading landowner would not be dictated to by the local authori- ties, and when the latter called upon him to put into a reasonable state of repair a road to some of his own farms, he flatly refused, although it was wellnigh impassable. When proceedings were commenced on this account, he retained at enormous cost a formidable array 0 counsel, thus spending much more than would have paid for the work required. How- ever, he had the satisfaction of winning his case, and, although he died long ago, when last I heard from that quarter not a penny had been spent on the road referred to.</p>
        <p>Grateful " Tommies." I have known only one soldier who showed , 'i- himself capable of such meanness as the wife of 14- one was last week convicted of here. By a It plausible tale he persuaded a poor woman to i'. 't , shelter him for the night, and was left to spend , it on a couch downstairs, but by next morning , ( . had disappeared with such portable property as he could easily deal with. Hero is a 'different story: a have in Southport a well-to-do friend k,, who, himself a life-long teetotaler, has all along •:' , been a strong advocate of total abstinence. One I ; afternoon a member of the family reported to him that there were two drunken soldiers in •-•- the garden. He went out and demanded to know what they were about. They frankly con- fessed that they had had too much intoxicating . drink, and were painfully conscious of the -, punishment awaiting them if they returned in that state. They begged to be allowed to lie down there and then and sleep off the effects. And. the old teetotaler had compassion on the misguided young fellows in khaki. He called 't...,' •- for rugs, and saw them comfortably settled ' ' 1 down in a quiet corner. After an interval of tsome hours, he took them coffee, and by the time he sent them on their way they were apparently .. , : , quite sober. They were much touched by the kindness shown them, and overwhelmed their . - host with expressions of thanks, as well as of penitence, and an undertaking not to. repeat , their error. I commend my friend's example.</p>
      </div>
