      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, 'JANUARY 8, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, 'JANUARY 8, 1916.</p>
        <p>MORE YEAR-END LORE.</p>
        <p>How The Famous Twelve Days Were Kept In Merrie England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>JACK HORNER'S CHRISTMAS PIE.</head>
        <p>JACK HORNER'S CHRISTMAS PIE.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR</p>
        <p>Nothing in the annals of the past is more clearly recorded than the facts relating to the universally respected and inoffensive jack Horner. The thumb and the plum are current coin in nursery speech, and the suggestion that the story needs the services of an antiquarian WI!! appear to must people almost unbelievable. Yet in two interesting particulars such is .the case.</p>
        <p>To the uninstructed mind the Christmas Pie of the rhyme is the pie he happened to get at Christmas and which happened to contain a plum. - Such a misunderstanding would have been impossible two or three centuries ago. Then, at any rate, they knew the Christmas Pie in all its technicalities, and the mention of the dredging up of a plum Would make grown-ups tenderly reminiscent of the days of their guile- less youth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>PIE WONDERFUL.</head>
        <p>PIE WONDERFUL.</p>
        <p>For the Christmas Pie, was an institution, which spread itself over the glorious twelve days, more inevitable than the Christmas Tree and much more welcome. AS the Pie is among the forgotten things, we depend , upon such incidental descriptions as may be found in the pages of old authors. Literary folks will re- member a passage in Addison's " Spectator " : The Christmas Pie is in its own nature a kind of consecrated cake and a badge of distinction; and yet it is often forbidden- to the Druid of the family. Strange! that a sirloin of beef, whether boiled or roasted, when entire is exposed to his utmost depre- dations and incisions, but if minced into small pieces and mixed up with plums and sugar, changes in property, and, •forsooth, is meat for his master.</p>
        <p>The point to be noticed is that the Pie con- sisted of minced beef, blended with plums and sugar. To juvenile taste the plums would ap- peal more strongly than the beef, and -would form the most toothsome morsels. Such con- coctions no longer appear on the table, but "getting the plums" is still a phrase for getting the best. Of course, there was an historical Jack Horner, who was entrusted with the carrying of a large pie to London in the troubled days of Henry yin. He put his thumb into the pie, to find that it contained the title deeds of the lands of one of the Monasteries, someone having taken advantage of his daftness to make him the bearer of valuable documents. This was a plum indeed. But we are discussing antiquities, not history.</p>
        <p>Here are one or two other references to Christmas Pie. :Nilsson, a Continental who travelled here and described us, said :</p>
        <p>A favourite with every family at Christ- mas is the famous one called Christmas Pie, the making of which is a grand art, it is composed of a scientific hash of ueat's tongue, the breast of chicken, eggs, sugar, raisins, citron and orange peel, with divers kinds of spices.</p>
        <p>Whether such. mixtures 1% 0 tild commend themselves to the modern palate would be hard to guess, but the fact that they held sway for centuries says something for their toothsome- ness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>"SOME" PIE.</head>
        <p>"SOME" PIE.</p>
        <p>In the noble houses of England preparations for the Christmas Pie were made of the most lavish kind. Let the reader perme a descrip- tion which has come down to us Or': a pie made for Sir Henry Gray and shipped from Berwick to London .to adorn his Christy!)las table. It was prepared upon his estate at llawick and conveyed thence to Berwick for London. These are the contents Two bushels of flour, 201 b. of butter, four geese, two turkeys, two rabbits, fonr wild ducks, two woodcocks, six sinipes, four par- tridges, two neat's tonguee, two curlews. seven blackbirds, and six pigeons.</p>
        <p>A certain Dorothy Patterson, housekeeper at flu wick, compounded this wonderful mixture and made the pastry. When finished the pie was nearly nine feet in circumferonco and weighed about twelve stones. it Was fitted in a ease, mounted on castors, and was wheeled around the table as the guests were served with lavish platefuls. To distinguish the various morsels would surely tax the powers even of an epicure. Possibly nobody troubled to know whether it was duck or piseoni, and the last flavour was not that of pie, 'but ,of liquids which can hardly be mentioned in a respectable weekly journal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>CAUTION.</head>
        <p>A CAUTION.</p>
        <p>The memory of these things suggests a 'caution. Before we English. people ridicule the French for eating frogs, or the Chinese for eating rats and mice (wOlieh they don't), or making puddings out ot: birds' neists (which aren't bad taking), it may be well to remember that our ancestors built up their bodies upon curious stuff, and that the same things may yet be )unning in our veins. They took seagulls; there was pickled goose, with cloves and ginger; soused turkey, boiled in white wine and vinegar and soaked for a month. The stalls of tulips cooked like peas, omelets of maalow stalks, hartshorn jelly, pippin* preserved in jel.l3'—all these were delicacies at an Elizabethan Christ- mas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>PLUM RORRIDGE.</head>
        <p>PLUM RORRIDGE.</p>
        <p>And there was the. Plum Porri age. We can- not do better than quote an old cookery book upon this subject :—•</p>
        <p>Boil a leg anti shin of beef im eiglit gallons of water till Vf /T. tender. When the broth is strong, strain it. wipe Alio pot clean, and put the brotl% back again. Cut:b off the top tsnd bottom .of six penny loaves. Slice up, and let it stiand a quarter of an ,hour, then boil and strrAin and put into the. pot. Boil a quarter lit' an hour. Then pill in five pounds of; currants, well-cleoned. Let it toil a lile, then put in Ike pounds of raisins as_nl two pounds of pitueiis. Let theni boil till well swelled, then • put in. an cunce of mace, an ounce of cloves, two nutmegs, all beat very fine (thei le were, no ground gpiebs then), and mix smooth with a little of the liquor cold; and then stir them, into the pot. Let it boil a little, and then take it off and put in 31b. of sugar, a little salt, a quart of, sack, a quart claret, and the juice of three lemons. Yi rt must boil two poundo oil pruens in a q Unit of water till they are tender, and then 's strain into the pot when it is boiling; This dish .appeared first upon the tab ie dur- ing the great twe-:lve days of feasting an ti took precedence over all the ether dishes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>BOAR'S HEAD.</head>
        <p>THE BOAR'S HEAD.</p>
        <p>The supreme dish, however, was the Boa.,r's Head, served in the great houses with musical honours. The boar was king of the Englicit forest through many a generation and his lieu d was carried in upon. a lordly dish. Ifolinsheo the historian, says that the custom of carryiw in the boar's head at Christmas is as old as the time of Henry IL Here is a description of it, dating from the palmy days of Pizalieth:—, The first me93e was a 'boar's head, wh1c:11 was carried by ye tallest and mightiest of all ye land, before whom as att-enda4ts went one attired in a horseman's coat, hosing a Four's spear in his hands, next to him another huntsman in greene with a. bloody falchion drawn; next to him- two pages in tafetye sareenet; each of theiri WitiL a messe of mustard; next to whom came.',he who carried ye boar's head, crogt with green silk ecarfe, by which hung ye empty secabbar4 of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>MOUNFIELD.</head>
        <p>MOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>ye falchion which was carried before him. As he entered the hall he sang this Christ- mas carol!, ye three last verses of every staffe being repeated after him by ye whole comihanye:—</p>
        <p>The boar's head, as I understand, • Is the bravest dish in the laud, Being thus bedeck'd with a gay garland,</p>
        <p>Our steward has provided this Tn honour of the King of Bliss, Which on this day to be served is</p>
        <p>The head of a pig is still the chief dish at Queen's College, Oxford, at Christmas, a soused pig's head being the nearest approuch than can be made to tho 'boar's head of ancient Oxford days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>ROAST PEACOCK.</head>
        <p>ROAST PEACOCK.</p>
        <p>Neil in esteem was the peacock, served in its own fine plumage. The bird was cooked and then sewn up again in its own skin and feathers, and it appeared with a gilded beak. Its mouth was filled with wool, steeped in spirits, which was ignited us it was placed upon the table. A lady of high degree carried it to the table, and others followed in procession behind her, carrying tho gravy and other etceteras of the feast. e The bird was stuffed with spices and sweet herbs and the gravy was a wondrous and rich decoction. $</p>
        <p>Where we fatten geese and turkeys our ancestors fattened swans. The young birds designed for the Christmas table were taken from the parent birds in August and fattened upon barley in separate Ponds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>MINCE PIE.</head>
        <p>THE MINCE PIE.</p>
        <p>Of course there was the ,)lince Pie, a very old institution indeed. Anciently, as we have already told, the mince pie was made in oblong shape to symbolise the cradle of Bethlehem, and the varied contents recalled the varied gifts and spices of the Wise Men from the East. The Puritans, in their day, looked unfavour- ably upon mince pies. .They took the view that our Christmas usages are derived from heathen habits and refused to share them. In this they were right, but their successors have learned to smile at the scruples which led John Bunyan to preach against them. But the prejudice did not vanish all at once, and throughout the seventeenth century a clergyman who indulged in a mince pie placed himself under very grave reproach.. Butler, in his " lludibras," poured scorn on the Puritan attitude:— •</p>
        <p>Quarrelled with mince pie and disparage Their best and dearest friend, plum .porridge; Fat pig and goose itself oppose And blaspheme custard •through the nose.</p>
        <p>Tlio high-shoe lords of Cromwell's making Were not for dainties, wasting, baking; The chielest food they found most good in Was rusty bacon and rag pudding; :Plum broth was Popish and mince pie oli : that was fiat idolatry.. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>KING OF THE BEAN.</head>
        <p>KING OF THE BEAN.</p>
        <p>Among the sports of Twelfth Day there was one which has some little interest to those who are antiquarian-minded. A ruler or "King" of the feast was .elected, who was called the "King of the Bean.." The title is puzzling in itself and only yields a meaning when we have travelled back to the Roman Saturnalia. Among the young people at their feasts it was cus- tomary to choose a King of the Revels, and the choice was made by the drawing or lots. Beans were drawn and the one to whom the marked bean fell became the lord of the feast.</p>
        <p>Twelfth Day in Christian .usage celebrated the . coming of the Magi, or "Three Kings," from the East, and the old pagan usage was taken over and adapted to new beliefs. The idea of the bean survived, though its original meaning was forgotten, and took the form of hiding a bean in a special Twelfth Day cake. The family and guests each received a piece of the cake, and the one whose piece contained the bean was hailed as the King or Queen. The other guests .then drew .lots to see who would be Ministers of State, maids or lords of honour, and the character they had assigned to them they sustained until midnight. This habit of appointing a King of the Bean seems to have obtained at the Royal Court from time imniemorial.• Among the items entered in the days of the early Edwards we ourselves bare noted an entry of "sixty shillings" paid to one of the courtiers who had to sustain the part of King of the Bean.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>PLOUGH MONDAY.</head>
        <p>PLOUGH MONDAY.</p>
        <p>But all things have an end. There came an end even to the Christmas revels of our ances- tors, and January 7th found them face to face with stern necessities. The Monday following January 6th was called Plough Monday, and marked the resumption of toil. in pre-Refor- mation times the ploughmen kept lights burn- ing before certain images in the churches, with the idea of obtaining blessing for their work, and in order to obtain money for the candles used to go out in procession begging for the cost of " Plough Lights." The Reformation put an end to the candles, but the festival sur- vived. The farm hands, up to a very recent date,- used to go round and trouble the house- holders on Plough Monday. A plough was dressed in ribbons and garlands and the men, in grotesque dresses, dragged it from house to house, asking good folks to put their blessing on the plough, lest they be left without bread at the harvest.</p>
        <p>Plough Monday also witnessed a competition between the men and maids of the farm-houses as to who could rise .earliesti. They did little work during the twelve days, but made a brisk beginning for Plough Monday. I f the men were up and about before the women folks the women Must provide a meal, which included a roasted fowl, for Shrovetide, and vice-versa. But little work was done on Plough Monday itself, and it reveals something of the easy-going ways of our ancestors that after Twelve Days' rest and fun-making. they took another holiday to get over it before settling to their routine once more. Whether life was brighter for them than for us we cannot judge, and it does not lie with us to complain.</p>
        <p>A Merry Christmas. !-("lho.Daily Mail' will not be pnblished to morrow, and for that reason we seize the occasion to-day of bidding our readers a merry Christmas."—" Daily Mail," December 21.6.. And a very good reason, too."--;-" Punch."</p>
        <p>Why Worry About Hash? S.IIIIC peuple find fault when eating hash bo- ca use they don't know what is, in it. Snub 'souls are simply trying to dodge happiness. Would anybody ever start upon a journey if they knew the cars were going to leave the track, or that the bridge was sure to collapse? No, indeed. Would lovers of hash ever order the most toothsome viand were it not for the delight- ful uncertainty- attached to it—the compelling mystery in which it is wrapped? Why be wise when perfect happiness lies in 'ignorance? Hash has stood the test of •time, ,and, whatever it is made of, history has yet to yilace a calamity at its door. Wine has caused the head to rise above the church steeples; pie, has ruined the digestive apparatus and hot bis- cuits .have brought the price • of nightmares ;clown to a surprisingly low figure; but hash, .regular, inoffensive hash, has gone on lown the ages and left nothing in its wake but la fond memory and a sweet taste in the month. Why worry?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>REFLECTIONS.</head>
        <p>REFLECTIONS.</p>
        <p>ikUiilwAim#wCW</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>Some Of The Local Happenings In The Old Year.</head>
        <p>Some Of The Local Happenings In The Old Year.</p>
        <p>TOLL IN LIFE AND LIMB,</p>
        <p>It would be 'seen by the Territorial Roll of Honour, which appeared in "The Examiner" last week, that during the year just ended no fewer than 558 of our local Territorial Battalion had appeared in the casualty lists. The Roll' of Honour, which we have kept up to date, from week to, week, since our citizen soldiers went to the Front, does not include the sick, because -no returns in this department have been available. The year's casualties, so far as we have been permitted to learn them, were divided as follows :—Killed, 95; wounded, 445; missing, 16; prisoners, 2. If we are able to give practically precise figures respecting the Terri- torials and so deal with them in prominent fashion it is because they are a united body of local men with local headquarters and other con- nections. But, as a matter of fact, Warrington and district men have enlisted in other regi- dents in large numbers, and it is impossible to say how many have joined such bodies as the Rifle Brigade, the Scottish Regiments, the Artillery and other branches of the service, altid Navy. We have three Battalions of Terri- torials, so that the wastage in the original .Battalion is much more than made good. Thousands of local recruits have gone to the other regiments, and it is estimated that altogether about 8,000 inen have been sent to the Colours. Of these about 300 have been killed, and if we take the average of killed to the wounded and captured at one to four, we shall get as a result a minimum of 1,500 local casual- ties. The Territorial charge in Juno cost the Battalion 258, or about half of their total losses. including 29 killed and 12 missing. Warrington may be said to have done its duty well, especially when we consider that the in- dustries of the place are largely concerned with war work of various kinds, and that the final "Derby sweep" has brought in eighty per cent. of the remaining eligible unstarred men. How many men the town will_ ultimately pro- vide we vanilla accurately gauge, but the total will be substantial and effective.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>PARADOX.</head>
        <p>A PARADOX.</p>
        <p>It is something of a paradox that, in spite of all this drainage upon the manhood of the town, trade nas prospered and the material condition of the place was never better. There are several causes to account for this prosperity in the midst of such a period of colossal waste as the world as never known. First of all a word of praise* must be given to the Asquith Govern- ment, and Mr. Lloyd George in particular, for saving the country in the first days of the war from financial panic and industrial chaos. The situation was so managed that we had a good, sound foundation upon which to build our new superstructure, of well-paid war work. That work became plentiful, aml the workers being scarce, the pay was good. There is a fear that a surf ion of those who have suddenly found themselves in possession of regular and un- usually high wages are not. making the best use of them, though they have been advised to embrace the present golden opportunity of .pro- viding for the future rainy day which will almost inevitably come, though we hope it will not. lt\ is certainly to the good in this respect that depositors at the Savings Bank increased and that depositors invested no less a sum than £35,000 in the War Loan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0246" n="art0246">
        <head>GREAT FLOW OF PHILANTHROPY.</head>
        <p>GREAT FLOW OF PHILANTHROPY.</p>
        <p>Never, we think, was "there et year in which the hearts and purses of our people were more severely tested than in 1915. In the first place, we have had the provisions made for supplying our wounded, our prisoners and .our men in arms with comforts fitting their condition. Official provision has been made for the wounded at such establishments as the Lord Derby hospital at Winwick, at the Warrington Workhouse Hospital, which is now known as the Whitecross Hospital, and at the Infirmary for accommodating our incapacitated heroes, but outsiders have come forward to show their sympathy in liberal and ,practical manner. Supplementary to these are Such institutions as the 'Haddon Court Red Cross Hospital and the. Thelwall Military Convalescent Home, in which the public have taken a keen interest. There are between 2,000 and 3,000 patients at Wia,vick. Whitecross, our largest town hoS- pital, contains at present 100 men, though as many as 180 have been under treatment there at one time, and the equipment provides 220 beds. This special building had just been com- pleted last March, when the Cluardians, being convinced that its excellent fittings made it admirably suitable for the purpose, offered it to the 'War Office for the use of the wounded soldiers. The War Office authorities inspected the block and, being exceedingly pleased with it, accepted the offer.. The Quardians equipped and ntade the necessary arrangements for the administrative purposes. Recently the War Office authorities inspected another block with the view of taking it over, and they have made a favourable report upon it. The block that was taken over in April by the lifilitary has been under lb% control of Drs. Jagger .and llodgkinson. Mr. A. Bottomley, in addition to his duties as clerk to the Union, has acted as secretary to the hospital. Many have been the efforts made to provide funds for the supply of comforts, and all have brought in a surprising amount of money. Prominent in this class of patriotic work have been the Women's Liberal Association and the Women's Tariff Reform League. The energy of these !arge organisations has been turned in these fresh channels with gratifying results.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0247" n="art0247">
        <head>DECREASE OF PAUPERISM.</head>
        <p>DECREASE OF PAUPERISM.</p>
        <p>Last year showed a diminution of pauperism in the Warrington Union, but owing to the in- creased cost of food and materials generally, the Guardians were unable materially to reduce ex- Peuditure. Owing to shortage of money the same authority did not carry out an intended improvement at Padgate Cottage Homes. and other work was similarly postponed. Before the war the Guardians made half-yearly con- tracts for the supply of provisions', but now this is impossible. The tradesmen find it impos- sible to quote for more than a week's goods at a time, and weekly contracts are now the vogue at the Union.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>TOWN IMPROVEMENTS,</head>
        <p>TOWN IMPROVEMENTS,</p>
        <p>The work of improving Warrington has been rudely checked by the war, but during the year two notable advancesswere made. Our new bridge was finally completed and congestion of traffic at a point which was formerly looked upon as the worst death-trap we had, is now out of the question for generations to come. A free passage over the river 80ft. wide and a free passoge for boats on the river for its whole width, the bridge going over by means of one huge an, give us conditions upon which the town will have reason, as time goes on, to congratulate itsrlf. The other event is the clearing away' of the old property in Ilutter- market-street, including the Old Fox Inn. This improvement is in a raw state, but we under- stand that if sufficient labour can be secured, Buttermarket-street will be again tackled at an early date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>SCHOOLS-AND THE FUTURE.</head>
        <p>THE SCHOOLS-AND THE FUTURE.</p>
        <p>.111 the eligible teachers of the town, save one, have either joined the Colours or have under- taken to do so under the Derby scheme, Women teachers have largely taken the place of the absent men, and the Education Committee have been open for some months to receive applica- tions from married women teachers :who' are willing to return to school for the period of the war. The war has had a serious effect upon the attendance of young men of military age at the</p>
        <p>Continued in Next Column, /</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>PETROGRAD.</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
