      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>War-Time And Winter-Time In The Czar's Capital.</head>
        <p>War-Time And Winter-Time In The Czar's Capital.</p>
        <p>LIFE ON THE NEVA.</p>
        <p>By HAROLD MAYBURY (Author of "A Russian's Impressions").</p>
        <p>Of all the capitals of Europe in winter, there is none so handsome or beautiful as that where the monarch of mighty Russia makes his abode. Already the Neva is frozen over, and with the advent of King Fro'st the spacious city takes on new aspects. On this cloudless night, with moon high in the heavens, the firmament verit- ably ablaze with light, Petrograd looks at its best.</p>
        <p>The temperature is below zero, yet the in- habitants heed it not. It is only Englishmen and those of Southern climes who must need hurry when this vast empire is in the throes of its winter months. The Neva this year is frozen fully six weeks before its usual time, and the Russian people, at all events those who erect stalls on the ice, are thankful in consequence, for its foretells of brisker trade for the vendors, who, day and night, offer their multitudinous wares to the passers-by. It is on this frozen waterway where most of the trade of the boot makers, watch menders, and sellers of produce, &amp;e., is Carried on. Truly Can it be said that there are no people in the world like the ittft- sians, who love to live in the open air, and espe- cially so in winter time. Booths, stalls and tents abound everywhere, anti with the cdngre- gating of these peasant folk one gets a true glimpse •of the real Russian. Hilarity, mirth and noise (the louder the better) is essentially a great outside characteristic of this Eastern race. The human voice to him is sweeter music even than his beloved bells.</p>
        <p>Ofttimes, when a stallholder is not busy with his customers, lie bursts forth into song, full of mystic charm, full voiced, round and clear, as only Russian vocalists are, and ere the song, chant or hymn has died away, others carry on the refrain. Fxi the clear air,•a distance away, the singing or these humble "moujiks" is awe- inspiring, and something which one cannot for- get. Previous to the war, the 'stately and erect policemen of Petrograd had a busy time with these self-same peasants, as also did the fire- men, due to the fact that the occupants of the stalls and booths imbibed too much of their national beverage "vodka." Practically each night there were fires, usually not Very sericius, caused by the overturning of naphtha lamps when the peasants' were in their drunken orgies, but now it is all changed, and somehow or other. the Russian does not seem to feel it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>OBEDIENCE TO THE CZAR.</head>
        <p>OBEDIENCE TO THE CZAR.</p>
        <p>The stoppage of the sale of "vodka" was an Imperial decree, which, no doubt, aCcounts for the fact that the Russian takes it so philo- sophically; certain it is that officialdom did not think it would be such an easy task to trans- form a besotteu nation into teetotalism, yet Such is the case. Obedience to the Czar's com- mands is before, everything to these country- men, and this dutiful fulfilment is a trait in the character of this Eastern race which cannot help but be noticed by the visitor. Ile did not make a loud outcry or ask that the "kabaks" be. opened for only a few hours each day; in- stead, he smothered the gnawing feeling in his stomach with pasties and other fare, for sweet stuffs of all deseription he adores.. Just now it is the height of the season in Petrograd, and for gaiety and splendour outrivals our own capital. Throughout the day along the broad and capacious Nevski Prospeekt can be seen the elite driving along in their sledges, the "ivosehniks" urging the horses along with shrill cries, quaint. yet strikingly musical. Above all, the sound of jingling bells mingling with the noise and hubbub of a busy city. The cinemas and theatres are reaping a rich harvest; for many Russians of both sexes take wounded soldiers- with them to these particular places of amusement. It is only the sight of these wounded warriors that reminds one of, the horrors of war; otherwise Petrograd is as nor- mal as in former times. There are no darkened streets or curtailment et lighting, as in our own land, for the menace to the Czar's capital is considered now a thing of the past, the hopes of the foe lie beneath the waves of the Baltic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>PRAYERFUL AND CONFIDENT.</head>
        <p>PRAYERFUL AND CONFIDENT.</p>
        <p>• The brilliant victory of the Russian Navy some months ago off these Northern shores did more to help -realise the power of Britain's sea Power than anything else since the war com- menced. Civilians, at least those- engaged in indispensable trades and occupations, have im- plicit confidence in their soldiers and sailors. They doff their hats to them in the streets and squares, and greet them in manner as only Rus- sians can. • Sometimes, not very often, the wounded speak of the doings of those in the firing line, of the great, retreat during the month of August; yet in the telling, behind it all, a merry twinkle is to be seen in his eyes. "Roads very bad," he mutters through his shaggy beard. "tterman shells hit you from very long distance. Wait until snow and ice comes and then—" And with these utterances he merely shrugs his shoulders. Strange as it may seem, a moment of especial interest to these people of northern climes is eight in the evening. Then it is that Petrograd for a few moments is silent as a city of the dead. At this time all traffic is stopped. Silent prayers are offered ,to the Holy Father for a speedy de- liverance out of the present conflict.. "Moujiks and banns" uncover their heads and cross. themselves, others take from their pockets miniature "Ikons" or pictures of the Madonna and kiss them. The signal is given by the son- orous boom of the cathedral bell, which can be beard all over the city: Yes, even Petrograd has its silent moments, in spite of fun and frolic, ice carnivals and fairs. •</p>
        <p>It is only after midnight that the principal streets are deserted and quiet, ..when solitary policemen over military age patrol its quays and broad squares, and the noise and bustle of traffic has subsided.</p>
        <p>The mighty idonie of St. Isaac's stands con- spicuous with its gleaming mantle of' white. and the towers and turrets of the dreaded fortress or St. Peter and St. Paul. Shot up into the sky like spears and like sentinels, they keep watch over the sleeping city. The Germans ,are held up, and these Eastern people slumber serenely, without the roar of cannon and musketry, which once was anticipated- by the •common folk.</p>
        <p>• Rustic Critic. A gentleman, whilst riding across the Isle of Wight, a few days ago, took a wrong honing near Carisbroolie, and lost his way. After riding about for some time, he espied a countryman sitting upon a gate, and, going up to him, he said: "My good man, is this the way to llyde?" - The rastie surveyed him critically for some moments, and then said : men, thee turns thee toes out tae</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>WILL IT LAST?</head>
        <p>WILL IT LAST?</p>
        <p>Runcorn Case.</p>
        <p>"But will my cure last?" That is the anxious question of the unhappy kidney sufferer, the assuring word of this. Runcorn resident, who has stayed cured for four years, will be glad news indeed. On January 9th, 1911, Mrs. M. A. Duncalf, of 25, Fox-street, opposite St. Michael's Church,</p>
        <p>Runcorn, said :—"I have been troubled with sciatica and kidney complaint. There was a neuralgic pain which shot down my thigh; at times it was shhrp and stabbing. My back was in dreadful pain, and I don't know how I got through my housework. I could hardly moo sometimes, and the pains made me feel weak 'an d ill. "There could be no doubt my kidneys were the cause of the trouble, for the water was in an unnatural condition.</p>
        <p>" Soon after starting with Doan's Backache Kidney Pills the sciatica left me, and my back was easier. I kept on with the pills until all signs of the trouble had gone. I can give high praise to Doan's Pills, for they have done me a world of good. (Signed) M. A. Duncalf." On Marrh 10th, 1915—oepr four wars later— Mrs. Ouncalf said:—" My health has been first- rate since Doan's Pills cured me. They did me a World of good. I never Jose ak chance of recom- mending the medicine." The whole body suffers when the kidneys are congested, for then the human drainage system is obstructed.</p>
        <p>The kidneys filter the blood and drain off poisonoUs solids and liquid waste, which, it' re- tained, cause dropsy, rheumatism, gout, sciatica, stone, bladder, inflammation, And it is in these ailments that Doan's Pills are so successful, -they remedy the cause, .the weakened kidneys. • Price 2/9 a box, of all dealers, or from Foster- McClellan Co., S. Wells-street, Ox ford-si rect. London. W. :Don't ask for backache or kidney pills—ask dioinettri- for Doriti's Backache -liidney Pills, the same as Mrs. Duncalf had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>INDUSTRIAL UNREST.</head>
        <p>INDUSTRIAL UNREST.</p>
        <p>Conferences Between Masters And Men Advocated.</p>
        <p>In the fourth annual report of the Employers' Parliamentary Association it is stated One of the most painful aspects of the War has been, the revelation of the deen-seated character of the antagonism between employers and employed. Even during the greatest war in history industrial troubles have been too preva- lent. Many methods of dealing with industrial friction have been suggested, but your com- mittee are strongly of opinion that to secure industrial trano i v--and this must bo secured if the nation is to regain her industrial and commercial pre-eminence--the subject must be tackled with the root-cause of industrial warfare well-recognised and clearly famed. The cause of industrial -unrest is largely ignorance— ignorance on the part of the workers as to the difficulties of the employer in keenly competi- tive markets. igeorance on the part of employers as to the eonditions tinder whit+ the workers live. In the opinion of ,your committee, the most practical remedy lies in the principle of conference. Employers and employed must by joint ,conferenee educate each other, so that both sides can view. matters in a right per- spective. The freedom from disturbacce which characterises industries where both. employers and employed, efficiently organised, can confer frequently, .strongly endorses this view. The present policy of appealing to the Board of Trade for an arbitrator is wrong; each side views the other with antagonism, and the cleavage of interests becomes even, more pro- nounced. Furthermore, a study of the results of labour disputes would indicate that the arbitrator, in a very large Proportion of eases. simply divides the demand for an increase in wages by • two. If a demand is put forward for an increase of, say, 6d. an hour, both sides in many cases are aware that an increase of 3d. will be granted. To reduce a question of national importance to the level of a division sum is playing with the problem, and tends really to increase the difficulty. Parliamentary or departmental interference with the relation- ship between capital and labour ought to be dis- couraged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>BANK NOTICES.</head>
        <p>BANK NOTICES.</p>
        <p>LONDON ary AND MIDLAND:</p>
        <p>The directors of the London City and Midland Bank Limited report that the profits for the year ended 31st December last, including .5:421,285 brought forward, amount to .1:1.552,262. They recnoottend a dividend tor the last six mouths of the rate of. 18 iier ceut, per annum, payable no the Ist February next, which with the interint dividend at the same rate paid in July last amounts, less tax, to X 745,801, apply- ing £642,860 to writing down investments, .4:30,000 tolitiilding redemption fund, and .1:20,009 to efficerss' pension fund, leaving £113,598 to be carried forward.</p>
        <p>Thp dividend for. the year 191 t was at the same rate, A:20,000 was applied to officers' pen- sion.fund and 3:421,285 carried forward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>RANK AILALGIII.‘TION.</head>
        <p>RANK AILALGIII.‘TION.</p>
        <p>Manchester &amp; Liverpool District Bank And The Bank Of AVhilehaven.</p>
        <p>We are officially informed that a provisional agreement: has been coneluded for the transfer of the business of the Bank of Whitehaven, Ltd., to the Manchester and Liverpool District Bank- ing Co., Ltd.</p>
        <p>The terms provide for the payment of the sum of £216,766, which is equivalent to 2.22 for each share. The Whitehaven bank shareholders are entitled to receive one half of the purchase price in shares of the District Bank at £36 per share. The current and deposit accounts of the Dis- trict Bank after the amalgamation of the figures of the Whitehaven bank will be approximately I:29,500,000.</p>
        <p>The•Whitehaven bank, which was establish?.! in 1837, is one of the few remaiti;tu; joia banks with a note. issue; in consequence . f be amalgamation the authoriSed note issue f 1:32,681 will, of course, lapse. Three directors of the Whitehaven bank will join the Board of the District Bank, ow!. will, continue their active interest in the busincl.:s the Whitehaven district. The whole of the stair ii I retaillVd.</p>
        <p>Tio. progress 01 the District Bank is shown in the following figures:—</p>
        <p>•. Current, Deposit and Other Accounts. 1910 1911 191:' • V..5,10),07•2 1913 c-):).916,689 1911</p>
        <p>An Unreliably Dog. - "Come right. on in, Sambo," the farmer called out. "lie, won't hurt you. You know a barking dog never bites." "Sure, boss, Ah .knows (lat." replied the: cautious colbured man, " hot Alt don't know- how soon he's going to stop barkin'."</p>
        <p>• •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <p>,e • • *.•</p>
        <p>BLOWN IN!</p>
        <p>The New Year Comes With A</p>
        <p>Rush.</p>
        <p>A YEAR'S RAINFALL.</p>
        <p>The New Year was literally blown in. What rate the wind carried it into Warring- ton cannot be ascertained, for we. have no recording instrument, and are not in touch with "the man .who makes the wind," as George Mozart, the comedian, says. But it was estimated at 100 miles an hour.</p>
        <p>The night was what. the .youthful literary student calls tempestuous; the poet, dark and stormy; and the rest Of us very windy. While choirs and congregations sang at watch-night services in the churches and cha,pels, the wind Howtya outside. Its strokes were both blud- geon-like and sharp. In carious parts of the town and disl net hoardings failed to withstand the onslaught and were wrenched from their stations. In some places they caught passers- by, in one instance at least., a man had to be taken to the Infirmary, lie was Mr. Thomas nelshaw (12), of 21, Hopwood-street, and suffered from injuries to his body owing to the collapse of a hoarding roar the Central Station. Many had narrow estupes. Even admittedly stable I,liildings had a shaking, and where any weakne,s was exposed it was aggravated. In almost every street slates were disintegrated and scattered danger- ously in their journey to the ground. Whilst in a farmyard 111 Hood-lam., Sankey, Mr. John Charles Can 'others, schoolmaster, of 370, 'l'3-Wesley-road, itindsford, Manchester, was Ii it- on the head .and right arm by a portion of a falling roof, and was conveyed to the Intirniary in. a. rather serious condition.</p>
        <p>Almost everywhere there was .a chance for a photographer to fake a picture of damage done by a_Zeppelin raid.</p>
        <p>Before-noon rain began to sail on the wind, and, though it was January, it was as bad as December, which has the discredit of being the wettest month of the year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>ALL ABOUT THE WEATHER.</head>
        <p>ALL ABOUT THE WEATHER.</p>
        <p>Now, if you want to know anyt 'ling reliable about the weather in Warrington you have got to go to Mr. A. Hands, at the Fever Hos- pital, Aikiu-street. He looks after the instru- ments telling everything about the weather: what the temperature is, the humidity of the atmosphere, and the extent of the rainfall. We all know what 'registers the temperature—the thermometer., of course. But it is said that you have to go to a Secondary School to under- stand what registers the humidity of the at- mosphere, for this instrument is described as a hygrometer. One will leave it at that. Its uses have been stated:- its complexities are best dealt with in a text-book. it is about the rain-fall records that one may shine. - This instrument has been out in the wet for years. A special plot, of grass has been allotted to it, and, besides' Mr. Islands, interested per- sonages—such as the young ladies happily con- valescent at the hospital—eoine to visit it. Its outer case is about fifteen inthes long and five incites in diameter, and, looked at from a dis- tance, it seems like a canal barge funnel sink- ing. A peep into its interior and the shape resembles a tuwdish, and below stands the bottle , receptive to • the refreshing liquid .squeezed from spongy heavens. (Rain!) Every Morning at nine o'clock Mr. Hands goes to this bottle—of a "reputed quart'" size-- to see how, much rain has fallen, and, when measured, he enters the amount in a book. Every morning since 1879, when records of the weather began to be kept in Warrington, that bottle has told its atmospheric story.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>BOTTLE'S ANNUAL STORY.</head>
        <p>A BOTTLE'S ANNUAL STORY.</p>
        <p>What is the bottle's story of the past year? Like the duration-of-the-war-teetotalers, it has had less to drink, for on the whole 1915 proved a dry year. An average year's rainfall is 30 inches, but in eleven months lanly 19.77 inches surged dawn and left December to do its best to make up the average., It certainly did its worst, for excepting on three or four days, rain, fanned by fierce wind, descended. , But for all its persistence 5.40 inches only were reached, .and the year's fall totalled 25.17 inches: A dry year. 1915 may be divided into distinct wet and dry periods. 418 inches of rain was contributed in January and February, but early in March the clouds .began to roll away ,and dryness reigned instead of rain until the 22nd June. In no single month did the rainfall touch two inches, and that fact is very unusual. The pendulum swung back in July and August, however, and it was fairly wet, until Septem- ber, when we entered another dry period., What h.appened in November and December is well within memory.</p>
        <p>One of the pleasantest months was Septem- ber.* -Freer front wet than any other which can be traced (and the records go lank to 1879), only 4-.luths inches of rain fell during the whole month. NO fewer than 24 fine days graced it: While September is usually dry, the finest of them generally produces front 11 to 2 inches. October, frequently the wettest month of the year, only produced 1.9 inches. November, an- other month well known for its inclemency, only gave 2.49. This also is below the average.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>GUN-FIRE THEORY.</head>
        <p>THE GUN-FIRE THEORY.</p>
        <p>On a certain day some time ago there was a. great cannonade in Flanders, and it bappened to be. raining rather heavily here in England. One bright-minded writer immediately ascribed the bad weather to the clouds being disturbed by the gun-firing. This theory is now proved to be false. The originator made the mistake of reasoning from one inAance. It is like the case of the woman who, happened to engage as a plumber a tramp who, stole her silver candle- sticks, diamond-studded hair-slide, or some- thing or other, and forthwith condemned all plumbers as being of fraudulent persuasion. As a matter of fact, we had our wet periods when the clouds came from the west and north- west, and not when they came from the east and south-east. And during the fortnight's artillery preparation for the great advance in September the rain we had in the first 2:2 days totalled only .09</p>
        <p>As far as -thunderstorms are concerned, these have been very light—hardly worth speaking of. Ladies have not had to pull the 'blinds down and seek darkness in the pantry, and big brothers have not had to fetdi the youngsters home from school under their umbrellas, while the lightning flashed and the thunder-clouds roared.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>WEATHER PREDICTIONS.</head>
        <p>WEATHER PREDICTIONS.</p>
        <p>If you are interested in weather predictions, you wilt be 'glad to know that Mr. :Rands does not .anticipate much rain .this month.</p>
        <p>Some people are a little diary about fore- caste. Once a friend remarked to Fanner Jenkins:—</p>
        <p>"You never read the weather predictions?" • "No," he answered. "I .skip 'em for two reasons. One is that there's no use o' worryin' about what you can't help, an' the other is that you never can rely on a prophecy till after it's come true, and then it's too late to make any difference."</p>
        <p>The way of happiness %an only be found if it is not deliberately sought. To seek happiness is almost always to miss it.—Claudius Clear.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>N THE COuaTs.</head>
        <p>N THE COuaTs.</p>
        <p>should be made of them. The boots were stolen from the Barracks. The prisoner said that in taking the boots to the pawn-shop she Was only doing what she had been told. The alternative to the fine was two months' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>GOOD START.</head>
        <p>A GOOD START.</p>
        <p>The business at the Borough Police Court on New Year's Day was very light—only two minor charges being before the Bench. The Chairman —Mr. R. T. Fairclough—took the opportunity of wishing the officials of the Court a pros- perous New Year, and expressed a wish that the outlook at the beginning of next year would be much brighter than at present.</p>
        <p>MAGISTRATES WANT NO BUSINESS. Before the commencement of the business at the County Police Sessions, on Wednesday, Mr. F. A. Frost, who was in the chair and who was accompanied by Messrs. C. E. Parker and J. Bailey, said that on behalf of himself and his colleagues he wished to take the opportunity of wishing everybody connected with the _Court a Happy New Year. He hoped that the public would help the Court—and thereby help the Empire out of its troubles abroad—by obeying the laws and giving them no work to do. The less they had to do at the Court the more they could dp in other places for the benefit of the Empire. They looked to the community to maintain the laws.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>"WORST CHARACTERS IN WARRINGTON."</head>
        <p>"WORST CHARACTERS IN WARRINGTON."</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, be- fore Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. T. Locker, W. T. Williams, J. Rymer Young and C. S. Burgess, Patrick Richard Moran, of no fixed abode and Charles Campbell, a labourer, of no fixed pleaded guilty to being drunk in Bank-stz.:•?,t on Monday evening. The qiief Constable said that the prisoners were the worst characters in. Warrington, and were an intolerable nuisance. Moran had been before the Court on 67 occasions, and during the -past year had spent eleven months in prison. Moran said that he was in Group 19 and he had been called up. He was sentenced to a month's hard labour and ordered to find two sureties of £5, or in default to serve another two months' imprisonment. . _</p>
        <p>Campbell was sentenced to a month's hard labour and ordered to find a surety of £5, or in default, to serve an extra month's imprison- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>CHRISTMAS MAIL.</head>
        <p>THE CHRISTMAS MAIL.</p>
        <p>Driver Who Was Deceived By The</p>
        <p>Moon.</p>
        <p>Patrick Maginn, of 1, Arpley-street, was sum- moned at the County Police Court on Wednes- day for driving a motor van without a light at Penketh on Christmas morning.</p>
        <p>The police evidence was to the effect that the defendant was taking the Christmas mails to Widnes at 4.55 on,Christmas morning and that the lights of the van were out. The defendant pleaded that the moon was</p>
        <p>so bright that he aid not notice his -lights had gone out. He thought it was more important that the mails should be delivered than that he should be delayed by the constable. It was the officer's business to assist him. -</p>
        <p>The Chairman pointed out that the fact that the defendant was driving the mails did not exempt him from the necessity of having lights. The defendant was fined Ifis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>CRUELTY CHARGE.</head>
        <p>CRUELTY CHARGE.</p>
        <p>Father Sentenced To Eight Months' Imprisonment.</p>
        <p>Thomas Kearns, labourer, of 3, Gandy's-walk, Warrington, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Wednesday with breaking a window at his house and also with illtreating his chil- dren. , Mr. Arthur Browne; who prosecuteh on be- half of the N.S.P.C.C., said that every Saturday night the two girls had to run out of the house for fear of their father. On one Wednesday he knocked one of the children on to the floor. They often had to go to stay with their step-. sister in Bowe-street.</p>
        <p>Evidence was given •by one of the children, Mrs. Bate, Mrs. Kearns (the prisoner's wife), and Inspector Wainwright, bearing out this statement. _ . . _</p>
        <p>With regard to the first charge, it was stated that when the prisoner came home one Satur- day evening he smashed a window, because he could not get into the house. Prisonef was sentenced to six months' im- prisonment for ill-treating his children and two months for damage to the window.</p>
        <p>DRUNKARDS COME IN SHOALS.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Wednesday a number of persons were charged with being drunk. Most of them were of no fixed abode and were strangers to Warrington. The Chief Constable commented that these people came to Warrington in shoals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>ARMY BOOTS.</head>
        <p>ARMY BOOTS.</p>
        <p>Notorious Woman Fined £lO For Attempted Pawning.</p>
        <p>A notorioUs Warrington woman, who endea- voured to defraud the Government by assisting a soldier to pawn a pair of Army boots, has, by being fined .210, found that the boot is on the other leg. She was tried by the justices at the Borough Police Court on Wednesday. The defendant was Margaret McLoughlin, of no settled abode, a woman who had been before the Court on 98 occasions.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable pointed out that the Government had suffered enormously through thefts of this character, and when these people were caught it was desirable that an example</p>
      </div>
