      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>ANTIGON CAPSULES.</head>
        <p>ANTIGON CAPSULES.</p>
        <p>Dr. LERMONT'S scientific preparation gives instantaneous relief in all Bladder and Urethal trouble. Contains no Cubeb or Sandal Wood Oil, thereby avoiding that objection- able nauseous repeat, and more effectively killing the goenococci germs. All 'sufferers from such complaints are advised to take this remedy. To be obtained only from the Sole Agent in Great Britain, post free, in sealed boxes, 2/6 and 3/6, from A. DARCH, Chemist, 84, PRAED STREET LONDON; W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>"PITCHED A TALE."</head>
        <p>"PITCHED A TALE."</p>
        <p>Six Months' Imprisonment For False</p>
        <p>Pretences.</p>
        <p>How a young Yorkshireman in khaki had travelled about Lancashire' for two months, paying his way by obtaining goods by false pretences, was described by the Chief Con- stable at the Borough Police Court on Friday, when Thomas Goodison, a _native of Sheffield, pleaded guilty to the offences indicated. The Chief Constable explained that on 'the 30th ult., the prisoner went to Mr. Frederick Bunch, an assistant manager at Dallam Forge, and represented that he was on his way from Sheffield to Birkenhead, saying that his gov- ernor's motor car had broken down and he had left it to •be repaired at Messrs. Bellian and Davies'. garage. Prisoner said that he had gone to Mr. Bunch because he heard he was the captain of the Boys' Brigade. He him- , self, he said, had been a member of a Cadet' Corps at Sheffield. Having dwelt on this idea of association, the prisoner told Mr. Bunch that he wanted to go to Birkenhead that night, but had no money and would be glad of a loan of some shillings. Mr. Bunch was satisfied, in a measure,' with his story and gave him 35., but, as soon as he had gone, he teleplion..d to Messrs. Bellian and Davies to see whether a car had been left there or not. Finding that his visitor's story was untrue, Mr. Bunch mounted his bicycle and raced after the pri- soner, who had been stopped by a policeman, who suspected he was an absentee. He was then arrested. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Inquiries had been made, continued the Chief Constable, and it had been found that the prisoner had been to Mr. J. B. Starkey, of St. Mary's Street, and told him that he was a member of an old Wesleyan Cadet Corps at Sheffield and was clerk to the adjutant of the Bantams' Battalion, stationed at Birkenhead. He " pitched a tale" to Mr. Starkey and ob- tained 4s. For two months the ,prisoner had been travelling about Lancashire obtaining money in this way, and had also got some from a charitably-disposed solicitor in Warrington. He had been convicted at Sheffield for stealing furniture in 1910. He was the son of very respectable parents, who, unfortunately, could do nothing with him. . In sentencing the prisoner to six months' im- prisonment, Mr. Sam Wright (the chairman) observed that it was one of the worst cases which had come before the Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>"CARES FOR NOBODY."</head>
        <p>"CARES FOR NOBODY."</p>
        <p>Chief Constable's Opinion Of 1. Defendant.</p>
        <p>"The defendant absolutely cares for nobody. You cannot get near him, and when you go to his house he treats you as if you were dirt," remarked the Chief Constable at Thursday's Police Court, where Henry Charles Bennett; confectioner, of 113 Lovely-lane, was charged with (1) allowing his dog to be at large with- Tit a collar with his name and address thereon; and (2) with suffering to be at large an un- muzzled ferocious dog. The defendant said he did not know that the dog was dangerous, but the Chief Constable in- sisted that the animal had frightened the whole of the district. . Three witnesses declared that the dog—an Irish terrier—was ferocious. "It flies at every- body," Said one. The Chief Constable intimated that the de- fendant had been summoned before becans.3 • f ' the dog. When the Chairman (Mr. J. Rymer YouT•p) said that the defendant would be fined £1 on the first charge and ,C 2 on the second, the defendant asked loudly "What will it be if I don't pay ?" Mr. Young: You will have to go to prison. Defendant: Well, I'll ro to prison.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: There is plenty of room at Strangewaysi •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>NOVEL PROSECUTION.</head>
        <p>NOVEL PROSECUTION.</p>
        <p>Imprisonment For Stealing A War Badge And Rubber.</p>
        <p>An important case was before the Borough Bench on Friday morning, when William Reyes, of no fixed abode, was charged with stealing a quantity of rubber and various other things, in- cluding a war badge, the property of the St. Helens Cable Company.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. T. S. Steel, who prosecuted, said that this was the first occasion on which a War badge had figured in a Police Court action. The prisoner was employed by the St. Helens Cable Company on November 24th, and h left on December 16th. He was in lodgings in Warrington. and after he had gone away rubber was found: in his lodg- ings. He was taken into custody at LiverpooL When he left the Cable Company's employ he was paid his wages and given his Insurance Card. • He was also particularly asked whether he had got his war badge, and he replied that, he had never been given one. Mr. Steel pointed out that it was a very serious thing to steal war badges, because men who 'wore them had a greater chance of gaining access to prohibited areas than men who were without them. 'There was a lot of pilfering going on at the company's works. Apparently, it was the custom for some of the employes to take a piece of rubber away every time they went out of the works, and when they had accumulated a certain amount they sold it. The firm wished to stop this kind of thing. The prisoner, who had pleaded guilty, now said he took the rubber, but aid not intend to steal it. He took it home to show what kind of work he was engaged on. The Chief Constable detailed the history Of the prisoner, and said that he apparently came to Warrington in May, and worked at the Wire Ropery and at Messrs. Rylands's before going to the St. Helens Cable Company. A dastardly part of his conduct was that when he left War- rington he owed h 1 landlady (Mrs. Ince) .24 6s. 6d., and this lady was dependant upon lodgers for her living. Prisoner was sentenced to three months' im- prisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>IMPERSONATION CHARGE</head>
        <p>IMPERSONATION CHARGE</p>
        <p>DISMISSED.</p>
        <p>At Widnes on Thursday, Privae_aohn.Gou</p>
        <p>1-4,, • • ing was charged with representing himself, to be the husband of a woman whose husband was on service at the Front. The soldier's wife, Mrs. O'Connor, stated in her evidence, "I told him I did not think he was my husband from be- ginnirg to end. I did not believe him all the time." • The charge was dismissed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>SECOND WAR-YEAR SEASON AMONG THE JUNIORS.</head>
        <p>SECOND WAR-YEAR SEASON AMONG THE JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Storm Causes Six Matches To Be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>Abandoned.</head>
        <p>Abandoned.</p>
        <p>The hurricane on Saturday caused six Northern Union matches to be abandoned, and in only one case—the match between Hull and Oldham—was full time played. Dewsbury are again at the head of the League, their victory over Huddersfield giving them this position. The results were as follow : Barrow, 8 points; Wigan, nil. tSt. Helens, 2; St. Helens Recs., 0. "1 -Swinton, 0; Salford, 0. Dowslrary, 3; Huddersfield, 2. Leigh, 6; Hull Kingston R., 0.</p>
        <p>„ tHalifax, 3; Leeds, 0. tHunslet, 0; Batley, 0. tßramley, 3; Brighouse, 0. - tßradford, 3; Rochdale H. 0. Hull, 11; Oldham, 3. tAbandoned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Bad Weather Spoils Good Play.</p>
        <p>The weather played havoc with several gam•?.3 in the Intermediate League last_ week. In addition to the awful condition of the grounds, the fact that there were heavy rain a teriiie wind made first-class play out of the questioq altogether Of the matches played. Parochi-,1 just got the better of Bolton by six points to three, ‘. hile Heathside defeated St. James' by the same score. Rylands's beat a weakenei Beamont team by six points to nil. The matzo between Sacred Heart and St. Thomas' shoull have taken place at Victoria Park, but had to be cancelled. Weather again!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0278" n="art0278">
        <head>PAROCHIAL STILL ON TOP.</head>
        <p>PAROCHIAL STILL ON TOP.</p>
        <p>The League champions added another victory to their record at Wilderspool on Saturday, When they defeated Bolton by six points to three. If anything, the conditions' for this game were worse than they were when Heath- side and St. James' were on tahe field, and, of course, good play was impossible. Parochial. however, were too good for Bolton, and even when playing against the wind, gave them a good tussle. The Parochial forwards early got to work, and their quick passing movements on several occasions bewildered the Bolton backs. The Bolton forwards also played well, and they gave some fine exhibitions. On the whole the game Was very well played. As stated above,. the score was E—Parochial, g points; Bolton, 3 points.' f</p>
        <p>superiority. One of their forwards missed a glorious chance- of scoring, through failing to gather .the leather, while noxworth had an unsuccessful drop at g0a..1. A forward transfer spoiled a fine bout of passing, in which the whole of the visiting forwards indulged. J. Shaw. and Jack Jolley combined well, but they could not break through the defence of Run- corn, who were now playing an improved game. The spectators heartily applauded James Jolley for his sound tackling. The final whistle sounded with the scores : Ben Jolley's team, 5 points; Runcorn, nil.</p>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>At Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>St. Thomas' v. St. Jame,,'. Referee, G. Cooper. Kick-off 2.15. St. Mary's v. Beamont. Referee, G. Crozier. Kick-off 3.15.</p>
        <p>' At Victoria Park: Parochial v. Sacred Heart. Beardsall. Kick-off 2.30.</p>
        <p>Referee, J.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>BEAMONT JUNIORS v.'BOLTON SCHOOL</p>
        <p>JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>Kick-off 2.30.</p>
        <p>Beamont: J. Anderton; J. Darbyskire, W. Downes, W. Brookes and A. Mason; R. Bibby and J. Coleman; W. Smith (captain), G. Armi- tage, J. Higgins, A. Hackett, H. Hardman and H. Monks. Reserves.—A. Acourt.</p>
        <p>PAROCHIAL v. SACRED HEART.</p>
        <p>Parochial: J. Clarke; Plant, Dutton, Grady and Leah (captain); Farrington and Callon; Tichard, Dutton, Harrison, Dorrian, Sankey and Mercer. Reserve.—Foden.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0279" n="art0279">
        <head>HEATHSIDE'S DIFFICULT WIN.</head>
        <p>HEATHSIDE'S DIFFICULT WIN.</p>
        <p>Heathside commenced the New Year well by defeating St.James' at Wilderspool, but they were very fortunate to do so. At one stage of the game they looked as though they were it for a severe trouncing. Owing to the boisterous wind, the game was divided into four quarters. Heathside had the wind in their favour in the first half, but though they managed to keep the play in the St. James' zone, they could not</p>
        <p>pierce the latter'S defence. Time after time they were robbed of a try by the sound tactics of the St. James' backs, and the first quarter of the game ended with the score sheet blank. With the advantages transferred to St. James', Heathside were soon in a quandary; for, within a few minutes their opponents, with the wind as their ally, opened the score by gaining a try. The goal-kick was a failure.. In the third quarter of the game Heithside had their revenge and scored a-,couple of tries, making the score 6-3 in their favour. No other score was regis- tered, the game concly.ding in a heavy down- pour of rain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>RYLANDS'S WIN AT HOME.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS'S WIN AT HOME.</p>
        <p>Before a mere handful of spectators, on a water-logged pitch, and in a downpour of rain, Beamont and .Rylands's met on the latter's ground on Saturday. Beamont were rather weakly represented, and were finally defeated by six points to nil. Bylands's did most of the pressing, but .it was not until just before half- time that they were able to break down the strong defence which Beamont put up. The try was scored from a forward rush, but the goal. kick failed. This was the position of affairs at half-time.! In the second half Rylands's again had slightly the better of matters, and they scored another try, which again went uncon- verted. The. game would undoubtedly have proved a very interesting one had it not been for the disagreeable weather which prevailed. There was hardly a passg movement through- out the game, and even accurate kicking was spoiled by the wind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>CANCELLED.</head>
        <p>CANCELLED.</p>
        <p>The Sacred Heart v. St. Thomas' match, which should have taken place at Victoria Park, was cancelled owing to the inclement weather.</p>
        <p>RESULTS OF SATURDAY'S MATCHES. Rylands's, 6 points; Beamont, 0. Parochial, 6; Bolton, 3. Ifeathside, 6; St. James', 3.</p>
        <p>Silver-street and Whiteeross have withdrawn from the League, and the table now stands as follows:</p>
        <p>Points P. W. L. D. F. A. P.</p>
