        <p>....5.3 5 0 22 44 .... 11 2 8 1 17 81 5</p>
        <p>BEN JOLLEI 'S TEAM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>Beats Runcorn On Their Own Ground</head>
        <p>Beats Runcorn On Their Own Ground</p>
        <p>A team composed of Warrington, Wigan, Dewsbury, Salford and Cadishead players, and selected by Ben Jolley, the Warrington back, , opposed_ the Runcorn team at the Irwell-lane ground on New Year's Day-. A. familiar figure apPeared on the Runcorn team in the person of the veteran James Jolley, who occupied the half-back position. Ikn- wick, the 'ex-Warrington player and now of Dewsbury, appeared at full-back for Jolley's :team. while other Warrington men were J. Baker, H. Baker, Callon, J. Fearnley, J. "'ranter and Mather. The teams were as follow : Jolley's Team: Renwick (Dewsbury); Baker (J.), Jolley (B.), Jolley (W.) (Wigan) and Baker (H); Holbrook (Cadishead) and Callon; Fearn- ley (J.), Tranter, Mather; Fearnley (G.) (Sal- ford), Wall and Douglas (Cadishead). Runcorn: Butterworth (D.); Shields, Kelly. Shaw (J.) and Jolley (John); Jolley (Jas.) and- Hoxworth• (D.); ••O'Garra; Hughes, Booth, Stevenson, Harrison and. Coleshill. Referee :,Mr. Harold Yates. • Unfortunately, bad conditions :prevailed, and. there was a poor gate. At the commencement W. Jolley and H. Baker indulged in a :good round of passing, but tile wing man was finally well held. Alf. Hughes found welcome relief for Runcorn, after which Tranter and Baker executed a fine pass- ing:bout, which- resulted in Baker getting over. Renwick, however, failed to add .the extra points. On resuming, J. Jolley found touch. and from the ensuing scrimmage the home for- wards came away with a fine rush, but the ball rolled into touch. The visiting team were now, playing a much superior game, Tranter being very smart in the forward line, which was far better-than that of Runcorn. Runcorn forced matters, and from a burst, Kelly almost scored. Runcorn were now awarded a penalty-kick, but James Jolley failed at goal. From a forward 'lrish Butterworth Saved finely, and nearing the interval, Ben. Jolley dropped a fine goal from near the centre of the field. In the second half Runcorn had the assist- ance of the wind, and they . quickly assumed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Much Bad Weather And Very , Little Play.</p>
        <p>Saturday being New Year's Day, and also a general holiday, all followers of the Soccer code had been looking forward to a good after- noon's sport, and also to commence the oecond portion of the season in 191546 in great style. Alas! their hopes were doomed, and, as during the Christmas holiday, disappointment was their lot. The terrific wind, accompanied with rain, made it nearly impossible to play football of any description.</p>
        <p>None of the senior clubs in the town were playing. The Warrington Association Club were unable to arrange a fixture. Thus they have had a very disappointing holiday. On Christmas morning Kirkdale (Liverpool) failed to appear, and on Boxing Day a strong team representing the Cable Works also failed to appear owing to the bad weather conditions. Financially, it is'a great loss to the club.</p>
        <p>Victoria Park presented a very unusual spectacle for a Saturday afternoon, when there is often half a dozen games being played, either Rugby or Soccer. The Park was vacant. No teams turned up A few hardy spectators wh3 braved the elements, fully expected the Emmanuel Institute to play. their fixture. The Institute could be depended upon; it was the visiting team that failed to appear.</p>
        <p>It was too much for some of the juniors to be disappointed of their sport on two consecutive Saturday afternoons, and several' games were played.</p>
        <p>In the Junior League to-day there should be a keen struggle between the leaders and the Scouts, as, the latter are determined to avenge themselves for the defeat in their first engage- ment.</p>
        <p>Fairfield 'United Reserves and the Howleyites should be a keen game. as the latter have lately greatly improved 'their team by introducing new talent.</p>
        <p>The resignation of the Padgate C.L.B. has</p>
        <p>made a great difference to some of the club's averages, and 'caused a reshuffling of positions.</p>
        <p>The result of to-day's games will have a great effect on the first three positions in the League.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0284" n="art0284">
        <head>\X IRRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>\X IRRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>, Goals P. W. L. D: F. A. P. St. Elphires Stockton Heath Scouts 8 5 3 0 11 12 10 Fairfield United Res. 6 4 2 0 23 7 8</p>
        <p>Howley United Orford Juniors Mersey Juniors Penketh Albions Bewsey A.F.C. Penketh Albions have two points deducted for playing ineligible players.</p>
        <p>ORFORD JUNIORS v. PENKETH ALBIONS.</p>
        <p>On the Longford-road ground. Orford: Gibson; Rawlinson and Hargreaves; Dutton, Boyle and Morgan; Barrett, Griffiths, Hirst, Chic and Belshaw. Penketh A.: Hatton; Watson and Thompson; White, Deakin and Swift; Loxley, Atkinson, Glover. Pearson and Finch..</p>
        <p>The home captain won the toss and played with a strong . wind in his. favour. Glover set the ball in motion for the visitors, who were. unable to make any advance against the wind and rain. They were soh acting on the de- fensive. The visitors' defence for a while played a strong game, but after twenty minutes' play. Hirst, the home centre, made a great in- dividual effort, beating three opponents, and scored with a hard drive, Hatton being well beaten.. The homb men continued to have the best of the exchanges, the visitors rarely get— ting past the half-way line, but their defence played finely and prevented any further score before the interval, when the game stood : Orford, 1 goal; Penketh, nil. , The visitors with the gale in their favour, were quickly attacking the home goal, and the home defence was severely tested. toxley made the sco-res equal with a shot that Gieson failed to clear. The visitors played with more vim, and in a few minutes Glover gave tl,iem the lead. The wind now appeared to abate a little and the game was more even, As the game entered its. closing stages the home team made a strong rally and Griffiths succeeded in .Making the scores equal. Both teams now made strenuous efforts to gain the lead, but the respective de- fences proved too good for the attack, and at.the call of time the score was :—Orford Juniors, 2 goals; Penketh Albions, 2 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0285" n="art0285">
        <head>BEWSEY A.F.C. v. HOWLEY UNITED.</head>
        <p>BEWSEY A.F.C. v. HOWLEY UNITED.</p>
        <p>At Lovely-lane. The home captain, winning the toss, set the visitors to • play against the wind:. It was difficult for the players to control the ball, and for - some time play was chlfly near the touch-line, but at length the home team broke away and Roylance, securing the ball from a centre, scored from an ,offside posi- tion, and the point was disallowed. _Play was now all in front of the visitors' goal, and their defence was severely tested. During a very hot attack Smallman scored for the home team. The visitors made a strong effort to equalise, but could not "get past the home backs. Half-time score :—Bewsey, 1 goal; Howley -United, nil. j The visitors, with the gale in their .favour, 1 at once raided the home citadel, but found the defence in very good form. Hassall and Roy-- lance were playing a good game for the Bewsey lads, and several times they - made raids into the visitors' quarters, but were easily driven back. As the game proceeded it 'became ex- citing, the visitors making desperate efforts to equalise. The home defence would not yield to the attacks, and when there was still ten minutes .to play the ieferee decided to abandon the game, the score at the time being</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <p>in the home team's favour—Bev,-y. I gual Howley United, nil. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCH.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD UNITED RESERVES v. STOCKTON HEATH SCOUTS.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former. Owing to the heavy rains the ground was in a very bad con- dition, and as neither team was fully repre- sented the captains agreed to play a friendly game, and substitutes were selected to fill the vacant positions. Fairfield: Mollineux; Stockton and Tunstall; R. George, A: George and Pugh; Bolton, H. Boyle, T. Boyle, Holden and Kidd. Scouts: Ford; Foster and Baguley ; Johnston, Woodcock and Manuel; W.• Baguley, E. Has- lam, Haslam, Fitzgerald and Kenwright. The visitors were set to defend the railway goal, a very strong wind blowing ai2ross the ground was not of much advantage to either side. The game started in favour of the visi- tors, who succeeded in opening the score after five minutes' play, Fitzgerald doing the need- ful. The home team made strong efforts to get on equal terms, but could not defeat the visitors' defence, and at half-time the score was 1-0 in favour of the Scouts.</p>
        <p>On resuming the home team started with, more vigour and attacked strongly. Holden was suc- cessful in making the scores equal. This suc- cess encouraged the home team, and the ScoutS were soon on the defensive again, when Kidd obtained the lead for the Reserves. After this reverse the visitors made it lively for the home defenders and a strong attack ended in Wood- cock equalising for the Scouts. From the centre- kick the home team had the best of the ex-</p>
        <p>changes, and Holden again obtained the lead, and a few minutes later Kidd increased the lead. The .visitors rallied splendidly and made another strong raid on the home goal, with the result that Haslam reduced the arrears. From now till the final whistle the home team had the best of the game, but could not penetrate the visitors' defence. Result :—Fairfield United Reserves, 4 goals; Stockton Heath Scouts, 3 goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0288" n="art0288">
        <head>TO-DAY'S MATCHES.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S MATCHES.</p>
        <p>Mersey Juniors v. Bewsey A.F.C. Fairfield United Reserves v. HowleY United. Stockton Heath Scouts v. St. Elphin's.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>BEWSEY V. MERSEY JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>At Arpley.</p>
        <p>buwse-y : -C. Perks; T. Blease and J. Stephens; B. Dickenson, E. Roylance and H. Eradoury; J. Edwards, N. Pearson, J. Smallman, J. kirk and A. Hassan. Reserves, D. Davies and W. Fear.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE V. PENKETH</p>
        <p>ALUI UN.</p>
        <p>At Victoria Park. Kick-off 2.30. institute: T. lulight; _E. Crozier and S. Hughes; E. Parr, .E. Hardy and W. Ayres; A. N. other, A. Oakes, T. hughes, H. Stubbs and Wm. _Eaton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0289" n="art0289">
        <head>JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>The Championship Protests And Resignations.</p>
        <p>The usual monthly meeting of the Warring- ton and District Junior League was held at their headquarters last Tuesday night, Mr. Simpson (l'earnhead) presiding over a full at- tendance of delegates. It was decided that the club gaining. the greatest number of points during the season shall be winners of the League. If two clubs obtain the same number of points, then there will be a final tie for championship honours. A protest arising out of the match between the Mersey Juniors and the Penketh Albions on Boxing Day was considered. A second ball was burst after 53 minutes' play, the score being two goals against none in • favour of Mersey Juniors. It was decided to play the remaining portion of this game at the conclusion of their return fixture.</p>
        <p>A protest was heard arising out of the Bewsey- A.F.C. and Howley 'United match, owing to the game not being played sixty minutes. It was decided that the match should be replayed in full.</p>
        <p>A protest was lodged by the Howley 'United against two of .the Penketh Albions -players being ineligible. Certificates were produced. Deakin was proved ineligible, and two points were deducted.</p>
        <p>The Secretary reported that the Padgate C.L.B. had sent in their resignation, and it was decided to deduct all points. Latchford C.C. and Bewsey Albions had also withdrawn from the League, and Penketh Albions had been accepted as members since the last meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0290" n="art0290">
        <head>RIFLE SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>RIFLE SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>Appleton 2nd In Liverpool League.</p>
        <p>The Appleton Rifle Club are going strongly in the Liverpool Miniature Rifle Association Winter League, having been successful in four of the five matches they have shot. Appleton are now second in the League table, being two points behind the leaders, Garston, with a match in hand.</p>
        <p>Belo* are the scores in the last match, A;ersus Ormskirk. The teams consist of eight melh, the best six to count :</p>
        <p>Appleton.—A. Shepherd 98, P. Harrison 98 H. C. Westmoreland 97, H. Warburton 96. Fl\ J. Grocott 96, F. Black 96; total 581; F. C. Bennion 96,C. Shepley 91. . .</p>
        <p>s Ormskirk.—A. W. Whiteman 98. M. Cave 98, G. T. ROyle 96, A. E. "Hunter 96, W. Whiteman 96, W. B. Esther 95; total 579; R. Wignall 91, G. Norris 88.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH BEATS TORQUAY.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH BEATS TORQUAY.</p>
        <p>) The Stockton • Heath Platoon, Cheshire Regi- ment, and No. 2 Company, Torquay, opposed each other in the second round of the knock- out competiticn for the "Mackworth-Praed " Challenge Cup, arranged by the Society of Miniature Rifle 'Clubs through the Central Association, Volunteer Training Corps. Each 'shot on its own range in the presence of an umpire, the ,Stockton Heath Platoon winning •by 1(i points. The conditions were eight men a side, who had made themselves efficient, 10 shots at a decimal target with a one-inch carton (10), and a two-inch black bull at 25 yards' range, possible score 100, open sights and no slings.- The individual scores were as follows :</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath.—A. Shepherd, 99; E. L. Rob- inson; 97; L. R. Rowson, 97;P. I. Fletcher, 97; R. M. Swinton, 96; J. H. Fletcher, 94; J. F. Fergusson, 93; S. Owen, 92; total, 765. Torquay.—Mr. Saunders, 97; Mr. Coleman, 97; Mr. Chipman, 97; Mr. Clark, 95; Mr. Trace, 93; Mr. White, 92; Sergeant Smith, 90; Mr. Miller, 88; total, 749.</p>
      </div>
