      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>• IuRED st</head>
        <p>• IuRED st</p>
        <p>NN orkmen Fined For Disobeying Orders.</p>
        <p>Fcank Harrison, a boiler fireman, of 21. Howard-street, Warrington, was brought before the Mupitions Court on Monday, on a charg:,. of "refusing or neglecting to obey lawful orders from a person having authority over him and fop leaving his work without a certificate of exemption." Mr. T. J. Ridgway was in the chair, awl the assessors were Mr. John J. Watts, of Wipt,:ng ton, Northwich, and Mr. John Francis, if St. Helens.</p>
        <p>The defendant admitted leaving his wo without authority. He added that he found substituto, so " there was no —arm done."</p>
        <p>In reply' to the Chairman, the defendant stated it was 6.30 in the evening when he left work. He was " firing " the boilers, and had only been at work an hour. Two relatives visited him and asked him to go home to do a hit of " peculiar " business. He asked the fore- man's permission to leave work, but this was not granted. He found a substitute out of the fitting shop.</p>
        <p>A representative of the firm said that the man who had been taken out of the fitters' shop had been at work from 6 am. and had to continue at work all night and all the follow- ing day. When the defendant asked• for per- mission to leave work he declined to give any reasons. He left work and did not return until December 28th. The Chairman commented that they con- sidered this a serious ease. The defendant was fined ss. John Gleeson, a smelter, of 14, Barn-street, Widnes, was fined 10s, for a similar offence.</p>
        <p>A MOULDERS TROUBLES</p>
        <p>Works in Warrington and Lives in Birkenhead</p>
        <p>A moulder -who works in Warrington and lives in Birkenhead, applied to the Bench for a Cer- tificate of discharge, remarking that he had secured another situation in Birkenhead, and that out of his present wages-475. 6d.—he could not pay his railway fare, pay for his lodgings in Warrington and keep his wife and children. He admitted that when he was engaged he knew that he would not be allowed to leave his present employment. A representative of the firm which employs the applicant stated that ten more moulders were wanted by the firm before ,their full complement was made. up. But when these were secured, the firm would be only too willing to release the applicant. • - The application was refused</p>
        <p>KILL THAT INSECT, TOMMY. Send your pal, " out yonder " some tins of HARRISON'S NURSERY POMADE. —they'll be very acceptable. When you haven't time to wash there's a big chance you'll have " companions." A little HARRISON'S POMADE KILLS EVERY INSECT on hair and body. Insist on having HARRISON'S POMADE. "Tins of Comfort," at 41d. and `9d. Sold by all Chemists— or by post from HARRISON, Chemist, Reading.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>EDUCATIONAL PROBLEMS.</head>
        <p>EDUCATIONAL PROBLEMS.</p>
        <p>The Shortage Of Teachers As A</p>
        <p>Result Of The War.</p>
        <p>The effect of the war on the supply of teachers , was discussed at a conference of educational associations in London. The conference unanimously resolved that the local education authorities " should be required to make every effort to secure an adequate supply of teachers, and should be 'prepared to Offer such salary and conditions of service as would induce ex-teachers to return, to the pro- fession during the absence of teachers on war service. The resolution suggested that until every effort had been made to obtain qualified teachers the Board of Education should refuse to approve the appointment of persons with no teaching experience. The Creation of Hooligans.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Mundella ( National Education Association) said that more hooligans were being created at the, present .time than ever before owing to children leaving school early, and this would constitute a problem at the end of the war.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. W.. Palmer (Natiorial Farmers' Union) opposed the resolution on the ground that the present exceptional circumstances amply justi- fied the employment of boy labour on the land. Mr. Walker (Agricultural Labourers' Union) contended that the farmers had never proved a genuine shortage of labour, but he would grant that there would be a shortage if the land of this country was cultivated as it ought to be. A resolution expressing the opiniOn that the age at which- children could be exempted from school Was already unreasonably low and pro-</p>
      </div>
