      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0307" n="art0307">
        <p>By Virtue of Leap Year.</p>
        <p>Evelyn: "You've been courting me now for a number of years, George, and I want to make a little leap_ year proposal."</p>
        <p>George: -"-I—l- am- not in a position to m—marry,just yet, but—" 'Evelyn (interrupting): "Who said anything about marriage? I was going to propose that you stay away from here and give somebody else a chance."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0308" n="art0308">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 8, 1916.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, JANUARY 8, 1916.</p>
        <p>DEATH BUSY.</p>
        <p>Celebrated Draughts Authority Passes Away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0309" n="art0309">
        <head>MRS. JOIN 111SSALL.</head>
        <p>MRS. JOIN 111SSALL.</p>
        <p>Popular Bank Quay Mission Worker Dies Suddenly.</p>
        <p>We regret to record the death of Mrs. John Hassan, of 31, Bold-street, Warrington, who passed away with tragic suddenness in the early hours of Tuesday morning. The deceased lady was 64 years of age, and had been ailing for some little time, although not bedfast, in fact she was out visiting on Monday, so that her death was totally unexpected. The late Mrs. Hassall had for some years past occupied the position of caretaker to Lady Crosfield and Mrs. John Crosfield, and was highly esteemed by the ladies in question, who 'naturally have been perturbed by the tragic death of Mrs. Hassall. For many years the de- ceased was an ardent worker at the Bank Quay Mission, her devoted and untiring efforts on behalf of this place of worship earning her many devotees and admirers. For upwards of thirty years she interested herself in the women's sewing classes, and was considered an</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0310" n="art0310">
        <head>MR. FRANK DUNNE.</head>
        <p>MR. FRANK DUNNE.</p>
        <p>A Draughts Player Of International</p>
        <p>Fame.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank Dunne, boot and shoe maker, of 6, Manchester-road, Warrington, who like most of those who follow his trade, was a thoughtful, studious, enlightened and therefore modest man, died at his residence last Saturday morn- ing at the age of 62, from bronco-pneumonia. A good citizen who put his best in all he did, and a particularly bright star in the firmament in which our indoor games are accommodated, to which we shall refer at length, Mr. Dunne will be missed by a large circle of friends and admirers who are not confined to this locality, this county, country, or even this continent. He had not been well for some time, though up to three weeks before his death he was able to follow his occupation with some degree of regularity. His last walk from home was to visit the Warrington Chess Club. He leaves a widow, three daughters and two sons. Dr. Anderson was his medical attendant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0311" n="art0311">
        <head>GREAT DRAUGHTS PLAYER.</head>
        <p>A GREAT DRAUGHTS PLAYER.</p>
        <p>It was as a draughts player that Mr. Duane gained national and international fame. Many years ago he founded a draughts club in War- rington, the home of his adoption, but he never</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0312" n="art0312">
        <head>OLD DRAUGHTS-BOARD.</head>
        <p>THE OLD DRAUGHTS-BOARD.</p>
        <p>On the old draughts-board, once 60 fr,ndly adored, With a tearful eye I gaze; Come down, old friend! for an hour we'll spend, O'er a game of the by-gone days. I play ones more, as in days of yore, To the moves that tasked the brain— To the lesson done, and the victory won, • In the old house down the lane.</p>
        <p>Once more in their rows my brave men repose, On thy mystic squares, old friend; And these warriors of mine, with the veterans of Tyne, In visions and science blend. And I seem to stand with the veteran band, To list to their voices again, As they tell, with zest, of the games played best In the old house down the lane. '</p>
        <p>Then our cares were few, and our friends were true, And our griefs were rare and light: Then draughts was nought (so we fondly thought), But a fountain of pure delight. But its glory bath sped, and time hath fled, With hopes we cherished in vain; And passed away are the grave and gay From the old house down the lane.</p>
        <p>But we'll sing no more of the days of yore, For the tear-drop dims the eye: Rest on, old board, by thy silken cord, As in days that have glided by. Still memory shall trace in thy checkered face, The records that cast no stain, On deeds that were done, and victories won, In the old house down the lane. JOHN HEDLEY.</p>
        <p>Chelsea (U.S.A.) Public. Reproduced from the Draughts Players' Guide."</p>
        <p>MR. T. S. YATES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0313" n="art0313">
        <head>Veteran Journalist Who Was A Native Of Warrington.</head>
        <p>Veteran Journalist Who Was A Native Of Warrington.</p>
        <p>Many newspapers, during the last few days, have recorded the death of Mr. Thomas Shuttleworth Yates, a widely-known and much- respected journalist, who was born in Church- place, Warrington, in March, 1831. He was the second of four sons of Mr. W. B. Yates, who for several years held positions of impor- tance at the flint glass works at Cockhedge (now demolished), belonging to Messrs. Alder- son, Perrin and Co. In his early youth ,the subject of this notice was also employed by the firm as junior clerk, rising to a higher place and seeking another sphere of usefulness in 1851. Like other " alive " young fellows he took part in the Temperance movement to a con- siderable extent, along with the Rev. P. P. Carpenter, minister of Cairo-street Chapel; and associated with him were other earnest youths, including William Houghton (afterwards a wire manufacturer), Ralph and Joseph Win- stanley, Thomas Birchall (who became manager of the Whitecross Ironworks, Bank Quay), Fred Monks (who gained an important position in the same concern), Joe Pye; and along with some others whose names cannot be called to mind, a young man named Moulding, who went to the United States, settled at Chicago and prospered much. Another was John Fraser. To very old Warringtonians these names will be familiar.</p>
        <p>All, or nearly all, were members of• the Warrington Mechanics' Institution, and there young Yates, when merely a boy, learned chess and in a few months was able to defeat one or two local players of note. He had a county reputation later in life. Of the associated young fellows named it may be mentioned that they all learnt to swim, and, along with the Rev. P. P. Carpenter, most of them (including the subject of this notice) bathed every Monday evening—time, 5 o'clock —near Buttermilk Bridge, on the Sankey Canal, carrying the practice throughout the year, however severe the weather. Boating was also a pastime, and many a voyage did Yates, Winstanley, Pye and Birchall take down to, and sometimes beyond. Liverpool, in the " A stea," a boat they owned. The Warrington Cricket Club was in exist- ence then, the ground being in Mr. Wilson- Patten's Park. It was exclusive. The young democrats decided to try and learn cricket and make the game popular, and, engaging a "coach" from Hampshire, who had taken up his residence in Warrington, they started a club, and young townsmen came to see the novel game and how it was played, and even- tually cricket became known to the multitude.</p>
        <p>As the years progressed, these companions* so very united, became separated, business avocations bringing that about. Yates went to Preston and engaged in the glass trade, but through the bad conduct of a person in whom every confidence had been reposed it came to nought.. The young man had studied short- hand and English literature, and he decided to try his hand in journalism. He went to Huddersfield at the suggestion of Mr. Frank Curzon (another man well known in Warring- ton in the 'forties), and started as local corre- spondent of the "Leeds Mercury," afterwards being appointed as a reporter on the "Hudders- field Examiner," and it may be mentioned that though he left it for a time, he was in its service, chiefly as sub-editor, for 45 years, re- tiring in 1910 on a handsome pension. He married a Warrington girl, Miss Ellen Grim- shaw, and had a numerous family, of whom one son and four daughters survive. Mr. Yates died at his home, Springfield, Huddersfield, two days befofe Christmas, in his 85th year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0314" n="art0314">
        <head>ASSESSMENTS.</head>
        <p>ASSESSMENTS.</p>
        <p>£76,000 Spent On Sewers Since The Year 1854.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CLUB ASSESSMENT.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Assessment Committee. was held on Friday morning, Mr. W. Furness presiding. Mr. H. Johnson made an application for a re- duction on the assessment of the Liberal Club and also on the John Crosfield Memorial Hall, but, after a long consideration, the application was refused.</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) read the follow- ing letter from Mr. D. P. Davies, of Manches- ter, re the assessment of licensed houses: "The Central Control Board visited Manchester on November 23rd with a view to inquiring whether an Order should be made to schedule Lanca- shire as a munitions area and place further re- strictions upon the sale of alcoholic liquors. Their decision has not yet been made known. and under these circumstances I shall be glad if you will be good enough to ask your com- mittee to allow a further postponement until the decision of the Board has been made known." This course was agreed to. Value of Warrtv,ton Sewers.</p>
        <p>The Clerk also read the llowing letter "Since 1854 the Wazrington Corporation have expended the sum cf 1.16,000 in the cnnstruction of sewers. Many zawors, whose cost is included in the above swn, have been taken out cr re- constructed, awl I am of opinion that a sum of £25;000 would rep:esent the present value of the sewers constructed uring the past 60 years. I beg to recommend an assecstnent of £1,050 gross. and £875 rateable for the Warrington- township. ---Ernest Gunson (valuer)." Mr. George Mather suggested tbat for the future the meetings of the committee should be not held so frequently, and it was decided that the meetings should be held monthly, but if the business on the agenda for a meeting was light, the Chairman could use his own discretion as to whether a meeting should be held.</p>
        <p>Slander Action Causes Bankruptcy. The public examination of David Fulton, a music hall manager, who is also known as David Stratton, and who resides in lodgings at 10, Waterloo-road, Runcorn, was held on Friday at Warrington. He gave as his cause of failure the losing of a slander action brought by hitn against the proprietor of another picture •house, and heard at the Liverpool Assizes. The costs of the action were £lO7 and his own Costs were VI His deficiency was £llO 15s. The examination was closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0315" n="art0315">
        <p>adept in this particular branch of work. She was also on the Ward Committee of the Liberal Club, where by her untiring energies and initiative she earned the gratitude of her fellow workers. Of kindly demeanour and engaging manner her place will be hard to fill, for she endeared herself to all hearts by her sympathy and mode of good living. The inter- ment takes place to-morrow at the Warrington Cemetery, at which Mr. Thomas Dale is to administer the last rites. At one o'clock a funeral service is to be held in the Working Men's Mission. Dr. Langdale, her medical adviser, certified that the cause of death was heart failure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0316" n="art0316">
        <head>MRS. RISHWORTH.</head>
        <p>MRS. RISHWORTH.</p>
        <p>A Well-kmm n Publican's Wife Succumbs To Pneumonia.</p>
        <p>The death took place on Saturday afternoon of Mrs. Elizabeth Rushworth. wife of Mr. James A. Rushworth, licensee of the Horse and Jockey Hotel, Winwick-road. Mrs. Rushworth caught a chill on the previous Monday, and had since been attended by Drs. McKee and Martin. Despite their attentions she died from pneu- monia and heart failure. Mrs. Rushworth, who was 61 years of age, and leaves four children, came to Warrington when 16 years old. She entered the service of the late Councillor Joseph Taylor, then licensee of the Norton Arms Hotel, and remained there until she married a Mr. Woodhead. Upon the death of the latter, 38 years ago, she married Mr. Rushworth, and assisted him for ten years in his business as licensee of the Rose and Crown Inn, Bridge. street. An active Conservative, she had for a long period been identified with the Warring- ton Women's Unionist ang Tariff Reform League, and latterly held the position of presi- dent of the Bewsey Ward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0317" n="art0317">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The itineral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Wednesday afternoon in the pres- ence of numerous relatives and friends. The Rev. G. Vale Owen (vicar of Orford) officiated. The chief mourners were: Mr. Rushworth, Miss Florrie Rushworth and Mr. Arthur Rushworth (daughter and son), Mrs. Sinclair (daughter), Mr. Percy Sinclair (son-in-law), Nurse Carr, Mr. J. Pickstock and Mr. S. Pick-stock (brothers), Mr. W. Hamblett and Mr. Robert Eastwood (nephews), Mr. W. Scott (brother-in- law), Mr. Waldron, ,Mr. Carpenter, Mr. W. E. Wright, Mr. C. Sinclair, Mr. J. W. Plinston, and Mr. W. Marshall. The bearers were Messrs. W. Hankinson, J. Shaw, B. Smith, W. Walker, L. Aukland and R. Pickstock. Amongst many friends present were: Mr. T. S. Steel, Mr. P. Darbyshire, Dr. McKee, Mr. Hickman and Mr. Brookes. Beautiful floral tributes were for- warded by: ." Husband and children," "Annie and Percy," "Elsie," "Hilda," Mr. J. Pick- stock,. "Robert, Lizzie and Jack" (Wakefield). "Aunt Pollie and girls" (Halifax), Mr. and Mrs. .Waldron, Mr. and Mrs. Carpenter. Mr. and Mrs. T. Partridge and family. Mr. and Mrs. W. Ankinson, Mrs. Cross, Mrs. Sinclair andi family (Lodge-lane), Mr. and Miss Watson, Mr. and Mrs. J. Shaw, Dr. and Mrs. McKee, Mr. and Mrs. G. Taylor, " Ellie " and J. W. Plinston. Mr. and Mrs. Redfern (Huddersfield). Mr. and Mrs. Pollitt (Lassalls Hall), Mr. and Mrs. L. Aukland, Mrs. Russell and family. Mr. and Mrs. Horlock and family, Mr. and Mrs. R. Chadwick and family, Mr. and Mrs. R. Chad- wick, jun., Mr. and Mrs. P. Darbyshire, Mrs. Higginbottom, Mr. and Mrs. Bert Smith, Mrs. Ravenscroft, Fred Goult (Liverpool). Mrs. Brophy, Mr. W. E. Wright, Mrs. McMullin and family, Mrs. T. Howell and family, Mr. T. Mason, Mrs. Riley, "Nellie," "A few old friends," Mr. Hodget and family, "Annie and Nellie" (Halifax), "The itei2libours," " Mar- shall's family," "Maggie and Emilie." Mr. and Mrs. Scott (Winsford), and the Warrington Women's Unionist. and Tariff Reform league.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0318" n="art0318">
        <head>MR. CHARLES E. HINDLP:</head>
        <p>MR. CHARLES E. HINDLP:</p>
        <p>Oldest Past Master Of The Oldest</p>
        <p>Warrinuton Masonic EARN(</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles E. Hindley, who, in foi'mer years, was a teacher at the Parochial Schock. in Church-street, Warrington, and who rose to be Mayor of Eccles, in the Jubilee year of the late Queen Victoria, died on December 28th at the age of 59. He was the oldest surviving member of the "Lodge of Lights,'" No. 148, Warrington Freemasons, cf which be had been Worshipful Master. He had held many im- portant Masonic offices. The late. Mr. Hindles married a (laughter of Mr. John Chadwick, chief mining engineer for Messrs. Richard Evans &amp; Co., and thus be- came associated with various eolliery under- takings. He died •at his hdrne in Eccles, but was buried at Abergwili, near Carmarthen, the Bishop of St. David's officiating. He leaves surviving one son—Dr. John mid- wick Hindley, now serving with the Med iterH ranean Expeditionary Force—and one daughter ' —the wife of Mr. Edward Lester, Gwynfa,, Carmarthen. His sister is Mrs, Rigby, 121, Church-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Among the wreaths sent for the funeral wag one from the "Lodge of Lights." Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0319" n="art0319">
        <p>found any talent here that would be worthy of his ability, and he went farther afield. He represented England against Scotland on three occasions, and represented Lancashire in county matches. He was given credit in draughts periodicals and newspapers for being the first man to play blindfolded, but this he disclaimed in order to give "merit where merit was due." He, however, attained promi- nence as a blindfold player forty years ago, when blindfold play was a rarity. He has played as many as 24 opponents simultaneously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0320" n="art0320">
        <head>TRUE GENIUS.</head>
        <p>A TRUE GENIUS.</p>
        <p>Well up in draughts playing as Mr. Dunn was, he was more skilled and better known as a writer and instructor on the game. He was what players call an "analyst "—one who makes a science of the game and 'its moves. And so we find the "Draughts World,' over twenty years ago, saying:— "Mr. Frank Dunne has never excelled as an exponent of cross-board play; but, as an analyst, critic, problemist, editor, author or annotator of games, he is the peer of any." If, as Carlyle says, the capacity for taking pains is the mark of genius, it must be admitted , that the analysis of a game of draughts may contain for those able. to discern it, the stamp of genius as unmistakably as the works of those who have been accorded a niche in the temple of fame. ' Genius chooses the sphere of action most congenial to itself, at least where its environments permit of choice; and what tends to heighten the innocent rational enjoyment of even a few, is no frivolous 'occupation, but has a grave significance in the affairs of life.</p>
        <p>We find experts deferring to his judgment-- thus the "Draughts World," speaking of some 'opening variations," says: "But, in deference to such players as Messrs. Frank Dunne and Willie Gardner we. shall always give" and so on.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dunne was draughts editor on leading national weekly newspapers, such as the "Leeds Mercury Weekly Supplement," and he wrote for the local weekly newspapers at different periods in, his career; but beyond this he published several standard works on draughts which are first-rank authorities both in this country and America. In 1890 he published "The Draught- Players' Guide and Companion "—a. guide to the student and a companion for the advanced player. , "The Draughts World," referring to this publication when it had been 'before the public for two years, and there had been time to forth a mature judgment on the book, said it was "one of the best and most complete books ever published on everything relating to draughts." His "Draughts Praxis or Modern Match Games," published in 1905. ran into a second edition in 1012, and was largely sold in this country and the 'United States.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0321" n="art0321">
        <head>A' YOUNG, APT PUPIL.</head>
        <p>A' YOUNG, APT PUPIL.</p>
        <p>Ile late Mr. Dunne was born February 23rd. 1853, at Easington, in the East Biding of York- shire, where his "ancestors, of Scottish descent, had been settled for many generations. He first learnt draughts when eight years of age from his sister, and; exhibiting great aptitude and fondness for the game, was soon able to vanquish his gentle instructor. , In his early teens he was employed at Armstrong's Elswick Ordnance Works, Newcastle-on-Tyne, and prac- tised the game upon the workmen in the meal times with such effect that he was soon able to beat all corners among them. Later he moved with his parents to Birmingham, where he at- tracted the notice of Mr. David Kirkwood, a player of repute known as "Denny," who gave the rising player his first love for the real ,!eience Of the game. Mr. Dunne was soon in the front rank of Birmingham players. About 1872 Mr. Dunne removed to Leeds, where he made the acquaintance of John Hedley (" Souter the celebrated editor of the " Draughtsboard," and alifelong friendship began which had much to do with Mr. Dunne's subsequent literary career.</p>
        <p>It Wqr. -'z77 that Mr. Dunne came to War- so that 13.1 was within a year or two of bping a forty yec.rs' resident in the town. Mr. Dunne was also a c...?ess player, a gift which gave hint opportunities Ilea* that his corn- maii?lng position as a driro,77hts player denied hixr Ho VMS a frequent winner of the local choss championship, and at the time of his death waz President of tho Warrf±:ton Chess Ci</p>
        <p>Mi. r.urno 'had roan 3.- social and dotie virtues. Ho was a trained nraeieian, a:ad plastid the tenor horn in a regimental band for about ten years. His baritone songs were in great demand at draughts meetings, at; which hz was always: a welcome anti hsnons'eci guest. is well-known to E2l -1):11,76f0 fat' tt kindir, homely dia;:osition," urrat:A) a :tondon draughts magazine years ago. "'IT:, has a help- ing band for anyone '-nterected in game. and his advice and judgment aTe of the gfeatest value to players of all grades."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0322" n="art0322">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The fure.ral took place at Warrington Ceme- tery on Wednesday afternoon, in the presence Of a. large number of relatives and friends. The chief mourners were: Mrs. P. Dunne (widow), Mr. P. Dunne and Mr. W. Dunne (sons), Mrs. J. Devaney, Miss Dunne and Mrs. P.F Boardman (daughter), Mr. H. F. Kirk, Leeds (brother-in- law); Mr. J. Devaney (son-in-law), Mrs. J. Pardon (niece) and Mr. J. Roberts. Beautiful wreaths were received from His</p>
        <p>sorrowing Widow, "Lena and Jim," "Percy," "May and Willie," "Percy and Trix," "Frances," Mr. and Mrs. E. , Wood, Percy Wood, Mr. and Mrs. Thompson, Mr. and Mrs. Sampson, Mrs. Thorniley, Mr. and Mrs. J. Roberts, and "Neighbours and Customers."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0323" n="art0323">
        <head>£2,859 DEFICIENCY.</head>
        <p>£2,859 DEFICIENCY.</p>
        <p>Theatrical Manager's Run Of</p>
        <p>111-Luck.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0324" n="art0324">
        <head>IN EAST COAST AIR RAIDS.</head>
        <p>IN EAST COAST AIR RAIDS.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. M. Loader, late manager of the Royal Court Theatre, Warrington, and formerly re- siding at Edale, Latchford Without, appeared for his public examination at the Warrington Bankruptcy Court, before Mr. Registrar Ridg- way, on Friday. The statement of affairs disclosed a deficiency of £1,859, his assets being expected to realise £lO 17s. 10d. Debtor had filed a deficiency account, which commenced with a deficiency of £1,190 10s. on November 4th, 1914. Since then debtor had earned £360 as salary, but that amount had been wholly absorbed in slomestic expenses and the deficiency had been raised to the sum already stated. Debtor said he was 46 years of age, and had lived in Warrington for the last eight or nine years, having been previously at St. Helens, where he had managed a theatre since 1901. He came to manage the Royal Court Theatre, Warrington, in 1909, and was also appointed manager of the Aldwych Theatre, London, in 1913, holding all three positions until January, 1915.</p>
        <p>In reply to the Official Receiver (Mr. Grant Gibson) Mr. Loader said that excepting for two years in the pawnbroking business he had been in the theatrical profession all his life. When he was simply manager at St. Helens he got £6, which remuneration was not in- creased when he came to Warrington, he con- tinuing to manage the two theatres for the same amount. In July, 1913, he got the post of manager to the Aldwych Theatre, London, at £l2 per week, so that, in all, he was making £lB a week. All of the theatres were owned by Sir Joseph Beecham. While managing in London he had to have two residences, one there and one in Warrington, and the house- hold expenses were considerable, to say noth- ing of hotels and the expense of going up and down the country. The Official Receiver: Were you not allowed travelling expenses? Debtor: I had a contract between Warring- ton and London, but I had to pay my wife's expenses. _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0325" n="art0325">
        <head>Author of Dramas.</head>
        <p>Author of Dramas.</p>
        <p>Had you some years ago started a theatrical business which was rather unfortunate in a financial sense ?—Yes. You got up some travelling companies?—Yes. How long were they travelling on the road?— About eighteen months.</p>
        <p>The companies acted two dramas written by yourself, and also a pantomime?—Yes. Was anyone else associated with you ?—Two gentlemen. I put in £l5O and they contributed £4OO between them. That money was logt?—Yes. And there was created at the time two large printing debts?—Yes. £5OO ,for the one and £740 for the other. They were for colour posters and bills. I have paid £316 out of my salary since the failure of the venture.</p>
        <p>Your partner did not order the goods?—No. I did that. I wits also the author of the plays. The balance of these two debts have remained like weights round my neck ever since. Were the actors and actresses paid?—Yes. Musical Comedy Rights.</p>
        <p>Quite recently did you acquire from Mr. Geo. Dance, who is well-known in the theatrical world, the performing rights for a musical comedy called "The Quaker Girl"?—Yes. And were the terms on which that was acquired £250, this including dresses and scenery?—Yes. And there was an arrangement by which the owner of the copyright was to receive 11 per cent. of the gross takings?—Yes. These are the usual terms, but if the. play is unsuccessful the terms are reduced.</p>
        <p>To run that play was a limited company, known as the Eric Burton Tours, Ltd., formed? —Yes. I had £l5O in it. I had a OEIOO ordinary share and a £lOO deferred share. So that as far as the liabilities are concerned, you are not responsible for more than £l5O, the amount you put in?—That's it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0326" n="art0326">
        <head>Successful Piece.</head>
        <p>Successful Piece.</p>
        <p>The run of that particular piece was success- ful?—Yes; the company made money. Perhaps as a result of that you acquired the acting rights of "The Girl From Utah?"—Yes, I paid Mr. Dance £5OO for that, and £4OO for "The Dancing Mistress," but nothing for "The Shop Girl," as this was an old play. I only paid him 71- per cent. instead of 11 per cent. of the gross takings. Regarding The Girl From Utah," how much would the dresses and scenery be worth? —lt would cost about £2,500 to put a musical comedy on the stage in first-class., condition. So that £5OO would not be out of the way?— No. The dresses only wanted renovating now and again.</p>
        <p>When you paid the money everything became your property out and out ?—Yes.</p>
        <p>There is no right on the part of Mr. Dance to retake possession of. the dresses and scenery at any time ?—No.</p>
        <p>By taking these four musical comedies you had made yourself liable for £1,150 P—Yes. _ _</p>
        <p>Part of:that money is now Owing, and Mr. Dance is seeking to prove on your estate?—Yes. The Eric Burton's Tours Ltd. is now in liquidation F=-Yes.</p>
        <p>Are they going to pay the balance on these production rights:P—l do not think they will be able to do.</p>
        <p>£4,000 For Production Rights.</p>
        <p>The last piece you acquired from Mr. Dance was." The Chinese Honeymoon ?"—Yes.</p>
        <p>Did "The Chinese toneymoon" tour com- mence on the week ending Bth August, 1915, and end on the 4th December, at Folkestone —Yes.</p>
        <p>Although £674 was made in one week, it</p>
        <p>HOUSEHOL LAUND •</p>
        <p>"THE EXAMINER "</p>
        <p>OFFICE,</p>
        <p>DEWSEY STREET, FOR EFFECTIVE PRINTING OF ALL DESCRIPTIONS.</p>
        <p>SPECIAL QUOT HOTELS, INSTI</p>
        <p>'ATIONS FOR TUTIONS, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Printed and published by John Walker and Co. (Warrington), Limited, at the EXAMINES General Printing Works, Bewsey-street, War. rington; also published by them at the EXAMINER Offices, '2, Victoria-road, Widnes; 32, George-street, St. Helens; 3, Market-street, Earlestowfi, all in the County of Lancaster and at Church-street, Boneorn, in the County of Chester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1916-01-08_WidnesExaminer_art0327" n="art0327">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>&lt;</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
        <p>cannot be said to have been successful on the whole?—No.</p>
        <p>There were times when a fair profit was made, but there was more often a lous?—Yes. How much did the theatres you visited get? —Forty to• forty-five per cent. of the gross takings. The travelling company gets more than 55 per cent. and usually nearly 65 per cent.</p>
        <p>Most of the theatres have got down 40 per cent. as their share. Mr. Dance's accountants say that in October there was a shortage of fees paid by you to. Mr. Dance?—Yes; the affairs of the play got less prosperous. £5OO was to be paid for the play and you had paid him £3OO, so that on that account and because of the lapse in the fees, Mr. Dance claims ..e443 6s. 3d. Is "The Chinese Honey- moon" still running?—l believe so. It was booked up to the end of March. Towards the end of the year, owing to the fees not being paid, Mr. Dance withdrew my licence to pro- duce it. I have had nothing to do with it since.</p>
        <p>In concluding his evidence in chief, Mr. Loader declared that in the last year he had paid Mr. Dance nearly £4,000 in fees and for production rights. Musical Comedy Properties Seized.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Browne, debtor's solicitor, next examined Mr. Loader.</p>
        <p>You say that there is owing to Mr. Dance only At.% on "The Chinese Honeymoon" F Yes.</p>
        <p>Was the whole of the production seized at Folkestone?—Yes.</p>
        <p>And are they still running the show?—Yes. As well as the properties, they seized the week's takings, amounting to ,R6lB. And in taking the show away from you they have spoiled your chance?—Yes. I had hoped that from Folkestone onwards I should recoup my losses. If we had been allowed to go on until March we should have made a substan- tial profit.</p>
        <p>In the contract it says that you were to pay Mr. Dance 10 per cent. of the gross takings, but have not your managers been paying 11 per cent.?—Yes; they made a mistake; 11 per cent. being the usual amount.</p>
        <p>Is not it a fact that your company was in many of the air-raids on the East Coast?— Yes; I was in every air-raid, and businesa was greatly affected. Si that ill-luck has dogged you right through this last tour?—Yes. If "The Chinese Honey- moon" properties had not been seized, I should have made a substantial profit. I was hoping to make £l,OOO at Christmas, and as all the best towns had been booked between then and March I should have well recouped losses. And Mr. Dance knew that you had the best towns to go to yet?—Yes, of course.</p>
        <p>Mr. Loa-der added that they made something like 100 per cent. profit on their capital in "The Quaker Girl," and Mr. Dance was a bit surprised that men like him (Mr. Loader) and his friends could make so much. The examination was then closed.</p>
      </div>
