        <p>Thelwall. He distinguished himself in the great charge by his Battalion on the 16th June, but before the day was over he was rather badly wounded. He lay in a hospital in London for a few weeks, and was then able to make the journey home, where he remained until Christmas, when he returned to his Battalion at the Front. , Mr. Frith, who is 23 years of age, was first educated at Bowdon College, and afterwards became a student at the Manchester School. of Technology for the examinations of the Insti- tute of Chemistry. Always interested in mili- tary affairs, he joined the Manchester 'Univer- sity O.T.C. in 1911, and, on war breaking out, immediately applied for a commission, which was granted him in the following November. •••••INMOOMMEMID through you to the Battalion, the great pride with which the Town has noted the mention of so many of your names in the recent dispatch of Sir John French. We all appreciate the honour, and the more so because we know how fully it has been deserved. • Believe me, very sincerely yours (signed) PETER PEACOCK, Mayor. LieutenantrColbnel B. ,Pairciough, 4th South Lancashire Regiment, do G.P.0., London.</p>
        <p>I TERRITORIAL RCILL OF HONOUR. WOUNDED. Sergeant Wm. Creamer. Lance-Corporal Joseph Platt. TOTAL CASUALTIES TO DATE. Killed Wounded Missing Prisoners 560 WOUNDED. LANCE-CORPORAL JOSEPH PLATT. • Territorial Bomber Struck By Shrapnel. "I think I got off lucky," says Lance-Corporal Joseph Platt, in telling his parents, who live in Plumpton-street, Sankey Bridges, that he has been wounded in three places. His left forearm was fractured and he has also a wound in the muscle of each arm. His wounds were caused by shrapnel and he s.lys that he does not know how it Inissed his head.</p>
